      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <head>FREE CHTJRCHES.</head>
        <p>THE FREE CHTJRCHES.</p>
        <p>THE EDUCATION BILL.</p>
        <p>The National Free Church Council is putting into operation all its vast machinery of 700 local Councils in opposition to the Education Bill. The bill embodies a principle with which the Free Churches have resolved to wage relentless war—the subjection of purely _educa- tional authorities to authorities which are elected primarily for quite different purposes. The National Council may fairly claim a con- siderable share of the credit for destroying Education Bill No. 1. As soon as it was in- troduced the General Committee passed reso- lutions strenuously resisting the measure. The local Councils all over the country took the same line, stirred up their neighbourhoods, and pointed out plainly to the local M.P.'s the way in which they should go. The ‘result of this activity is now known to all the world, and it is hoped that by meeting Education Bill No. 2 with the same attentions its fate will be sealed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>NEW WESLEYAN PRESIDENT.</head>
        <p>THE NEW WESLEYAN PRESIDENT.</p>
        <p>The Free Church Chronicle for August contains an interesting sketch, with portrait, of Dr. W. T. Davison, the new President of the Wesleyan Methodist Conference. Dr. Davison is well- known to his brethren, but he is rather a shy man, and, while he preaches regularly every Sunday, his personality is not familiar to the great Methodist people. He has been a theo- logical tutor for 20 years, and has largely moulded the thought of the present generation of Wesleyan preachers. Dr. Davison has never sought the honours of his Connexion, but, as he says, Methodist preachers have to do what they are told, and hence he steps into the Presidential chair. He ha l won reputation as a journalist as well as a theolo,ian. For four years he edited the Methodist Recorder, now in the capable hands ' of the Rev. Nehemiah Curnock, and has contributed frequently to the Critical Review, the Evpository Times, and the London Quarterly _Review. We wish him a year of distinguished service.</p>
        <p>THE BREAKDOWN OF THE REV. HUGH PRICE</p>
        <p>HUGHES, M.A.</p>
        <p>The news of the second breakdown, within such a short period, of the Rev. Hugh Price Hughes, M.A., has been received by the multitude *whom he has served with the most poignant re- gret. In the middle of February Mr. Hughes completely broke down at Manchester, whither he had gone to do his share in the simultaneous mission. The second breakdown is entirely of the same character. It is the case of an over- worked brain and body. Mr.. Hughes is one of those restless fiery spirits who spend their nervous energy with prodigality, and who live on the capital of their stock of health. He has now been ordered six months' complete rest, and Mr. Hughes's medical advisers and his friends must force him to take it. Mr. Hughes is 54 years of age, and must begin to slacken the pace. By the way, the Rev. J. Gregory Mantle, who in September will became a colleague of Mr. Hughes, has just written a readable biography of the famous Wesleyan. Messrs. Partridge are the publishers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>CAMPBELL MORGAN SOUVENIR.</head>
        <p>CAMPBELL MORGAN SOUVENIR.</p>
        <p>The Rev. G. Campbell Morgan has begun his work on the other side of the Atlantic with every promise of success. All the papers in the States have printed columns about his work in England, and have expressed their belief that he is a worthy successor of Moody. They are making the most of the physical dissimilarities of the two men. Mr. Moody was short, corpu- lent, and ruddy faced; Mr. Morgan is tall, lean, and of an ascetic countenance. The National Free Church Council has just issued an interesting souvenir, entitled, "Eng- land's Farewell to the Rev. G. Campbell Morgan." The booklet, which is published at 6d., contains a preface by the Rev.- F. B. Meyer, 8.A., and verbatim reports of the speeches delivered at the farewell meetings in London. There are many illustrations, including a frontispiece portrait of Mr. Morgan on art paper.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>DR. BROOK.</head>
        <p>DR. BROOK.</p>
        <p>Dr. Brook, the new President of the Free Methodist Conference, is one of the most re- markable men in what are called "the minor Methodist bodies," or, indeed, in all Non- conformity. He has been somewhere about a quarter of a century in active circuit work, and all that time he has remained a severe and ardent student. He is a D.C.L. of Oxford, and the only Free Church minister in the country who has won that difficult and honour- able degree. Dr. Brook is a quiet and incisive preacher. A good specimen of his style is the sermon entitled, "What is the real folly ?" which he contributed to "The Call of God," the volume of mission discourses published by the National Council. A good preacher, a good student, Dr. Brook is also a good administrator. He was secretary of his denomination's Twentieth Century Fund, and raised the sum aimed at, 100,000 guineas, making the Free Methodist's the first Twentieth Century Fund to be completed.,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>"CHURCH AND CHAPEL" MARRIAGES.</head>
        <p>"CHURCH AND CHAPEL" MARRIAGES.</p>
        <p>Anglican statisticians are very fond of quoting the figures which show that many more marriages take place in their Churches than in "Dissenting Chapels." They forget the diffi- culties in the way of "chapel marriages. For example, the Rev. John Greig, of Hadham Con- gregational Church, says: "I recently married a couple at Buntingford. The register is at Roy- ston, seven miles away. For Much Hadham the register is at Bishop's Stortford, five miles away. In neither case is there railway communica- tion. The journey involves expense and the loss of half a day's work at the least—serious matters for working people." The circumstances are similar in hundreds of rural districts. Our Anglican friends Also know, of course, that hun- dreds of couples who care nothing for any of the Churches go to the Established Church to get married.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>"HARRY LINDSAY."</head>
        <p>"HARRY LINDSAY."</p>
        <p>Mr. H. Lindsay Hudson ("Harry Lindsay "of Methodist idylls fame) has resigned the editor ship of the Methodist Weekly. He is going to devote more time to literary work.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>NEW LONDON LUMINARY.</head>
        <p>A NEW LONDON LUMINARY.</p>
        <p>Dr. John Hunter, of Glasgow, who has accepted the pastorate of King's Weigh House Chapel, London, will no doubt soon take his place among the foremost of London preachers, and will undoubtedly prove to be a distinct addition of strength to English Nonconformity. We fully believe that Dr. Hunter will main- tain the splendid traditions of King's Weigh House. The most illustrious of 1113 pre.:e- cessors was Dr. Thomas Binney, the author of the well-known hymn, "Eternal Light, Eternal Light." A writer in the Free Churchman recalls the interesting fact that it was Dr. Binney whose advice weighed greatly with Dr. Parker in his decision to leave Manchester for the pastorate of the Poultry Chapel, Cheapside. The City Temple is the immediate successor of the Poultry Chapel.</p>
        <p>CADBURY'S CoCoA, on the testimony of the Lancet 'represents the tandard of highest purity." It Is entirely -tee from all to .igt, substances, such as kola, malt, hops, Ac.. nor is alkal used to darken the colour (and so deceive the eve), Dr. Andrew Wilson, in a recent article in the Illustrated London News writes : "Cocoa is in Itself a perfect food, and requires no addition of drugs whatever." CAD- BURY'S Cocos is absolutely pure, and should be taken by old and Yonng. at all times and in all seasons ; for Children It is an ideal beverage, promoting healthy growth and development in a remarkable degree. Insist on having CADBURY'S. as other Cocoas are often substituted for the sake of extra profit. Sold only in Packets and Tins.— Advt. 3)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>Mc DOUG P4TENT SELF-RAISING FLOUR 120Egy. LADIES' LETTER:</head>
        <p>Mc DOUG P4TENT SELF-RAISING FLOUR 120Egy. LADIES' LETTER:</p>
        <p>MY DEAR NELLIE, Speaking of chapeaux reminds me that cavalier hats, with sweeping ostrich plumes, are the most fashionable wear for full toilette; Leghorn° or Panama straws are dyed in pastel tones to match the frocks they accompany, and the ostrich feathers must be shaded to accord. Large white lace toques or plateau hats continue to be worn by the smartest women, trimmed with nothing but black velvet, and perhaps an antique gold orna- ment. Such hats go with any dress, and whilst conveying an idea of elegance, never look too dressy. For young faces, quite the prettiest hat of the moment is a wide-trimmed, low-crowned shape, covered with tiny frills of cream lace, fulled on all over, with a narrow band of black velvet tied round the crown, and a wreath of minute green vy leaves lifting the hat off the head at the side.</p>
        <p>Amongst some delightful garden party frocks was one of white cashmere, with a plain skirt covered to above the knees with diamond-shaped lace insertions, each framed with garlands of leaves cut out in green-shaped silk, and appliquéd. Bands of black velvet confine the fulness of the bodice, whilst the collar is formed of finely-tucked lawn lace medallions and the green appliqué. A charm- ing hat is of lettuce-green straw, trimmed with creamy roses. Another gown is of black and white spotted muslin, trimmed with crossed bands of pale blue linen, with diamond-shaped lace inser- tions on skirt and bodice. The fulness of the skirt is set into a lace stomacher. The top of the bodice is of pleated lawn, with scrolls of lace form- ing a semi-yoke, and a black chiffon scarf loosely tied in front. A Tuscan hat is wreathed with marguerites, with a large velvet bow under the bean.</p>
        <p>Linen frocks are made in the moat fascinating colours this season, and the material itself is soft and silky, without the aggressive harshness we were wont to associate with linen. Mauve and reseda are, perhaps, two of the most charming colours, but.blue is very popular. To be a success the linen frock is tailor-made nowadays, and usually -strapped with piqu6 or encrusted with lace. The basqued habit bodice is, undoubtedly, newer than the bolero or Eton coat, but it is somewhat trying to wear. Wherever bright colours are per- mitted-to be seen, they are veiled with mousseline or chiffon, for Telles] effects are playing a prominent dress. The eoliennea, cryetallines, and • , which are so fashionable, all lend them. sea to this idea, allowing of a faint glimmer of the lining underneath.</p>
        <p>In a smart little coat in a stylc mach in vogue, the folds of the silk are drawn round the Elvis and fastened on the left side by straps of velult studded with diamond buttons. The muslin &amp;hit is bordered with ruched silk, and the high collar opens to show a lace jabot in front. The fine white chip hat is covered with white roses outside and turned up at the side with a large black velvet bow and buckle. Another hat is of pale blue chip, trimmed with blush roses, and black velvet loops, whilst underneath are a couple of parasols, the top one of white taffetas, trimmed with garlands of black chantilly lace, framed with rows of chiffon buoillonees, which also border the edge. Another one is s. simple pink spotted muslin affair, with muslin ruche round the edge, and a rustic handle.</p>
        <p>A waist-bag is a travelling accessory which is almost essential. At the moment velvet bags with large silver mounts in anti4ue style are fashionable, but a morocco or pigskin bag is really more business-like and practical. It is best to have it fitted with a loop through which to pass the belt of the blouse instead of the ordinary hook which is so easily unhitched, whether accidentally or by the wily thief. A "courier bag" strapped across the shoulder is, of course, still safer. A word on the question of trunks may not be amiss. It is advisable not to choose very large boxes, as their weight, if at all closely packed, renders them difficult and sometimes dangerous for the porters to handle. A couple of tiat-topped cabin or over-land trunks of compressed cane covered with black waterproof canvas, and a bonnet-box to match, will hold a reasonable outfit for a holiday of at least a month or two. It is well to select a trunk long enough to take parasols and umbrellas, as this will enable the traveller to dispense with that terrible annoyance to herself and her fellow. passengers—a bundle of sunshades and umbrellas. Your loving JENNIE</p>
        <p>ARGIt MANDOLINES, BANJOES, 1 I GUITARS, Spceial Offer.—Guaranteed ALIAN MANDOLINE, MAKER'S name inside, Else- wr .1, ornamented; I2s. 6d. Carriage paid. ARCHVR 'ONS. ST' EORGES' CRESCENT, TOP OF LORD- Tit EST, ...I EIiPOOL. Established 1848.</p>
