      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>There has been a change of gardeners, both at Wind- sor and Sandringham. Mr McKellar, who was for many years head gardener at Sandringham, has been pro- moted to Windsor Castle, and his place at His Majesty's Norfolk home has been taken by another Scotsman, Mr J H Cooke. Mr Archibald McKellar belongs to Argyllshire, and he was formerly in charge of the gardens at Floors Castle, the seat of the Duke of Rox- burgh. There is, of course, no truth in the gossip, that some of the servants in the Royal Household have been harshly dealt with. Many, it is true, were given six months notice last January, but in all cases they have been most liberally treated whether they were entitled to pensions or not. The Sovereign never gives any ser- vant a character, but no one who has been in the Royal service ever has any difficulty in finding further em- ployment. Do have been a servant the Royal House- hold, is quite enough to make it easy ior that perscuago to obtain another situation.</p>
        <p>Wa are all familiar with that tyi eof ghost which appears at great houses on the eve of a death in the family. Charles Dickens has introduced such an inci- dent into his " Bleak House" as a preliminary to the awful tragedy, upon which the interest of the book centres, and many less skilful authors have brought similar spec'ral warnings into their books. Now it is suggested—al little absurdly it would seem—that the plans of the Duke of Cornwall and York have been affec- ted by a similar stcry, that he intended to buy Hough- ton Hall, Norfolk, but was induced to re-consider the matter upon learning that the place was haunted. This particular spectre seems quite an old friend, for we have heard of her so often.</p>
        <p>The ghost is said to be that of" a little lady dressed in brown satin" who appears either at Houghton or Raynham, just before a death in either the Walpole or Townsisend family. The poor lady seems to be a very harmless person, and it is scarcely likely that the Duke would have any objection to her presence, particularly when ke considers that Hampton Court Palace is said to be haunted by a similar spirit. Besides the " little lady" is said to have died a hundred years ago, and we may be sure the Duchess of Cornwall and York would much like to know how she manages to preserve km brown satin.</p>
        <p>That peculiar and somewhat melancholy air of the lavender hawkers, now So frequently heard about the streets, reminds us of the advance of summer. The song, " Who'll buy nay pretty lavender ? Sixteen spikes for a penny," like that of " The last rose of summer," always seems to suggest the waning year,and in London there is an old saying that " When the lavender comes the season is over." Nevertheless, in spite of these rug- gestions, a walk through the lavender fields when the plant is bursting into purple bloom, and spreading its delicate odour, is well worth going a long way to enjoy. Most of the lavender from which lavender water is prepared is grown in Surrey, but from time immemorial it has been a general practice to Waco lavender among clothes and linen, in drawers and presses. One would scarcely believe that it requires nearly half a million of roses to make an ounce of the essence known as attar of roses, and even these flowers must be of the red variety, as the yellow and white species do not contain so much essential oil. Very little if any of this ccstly perfume is made in this country. It is mainly produced in India and the Mediterranean coun- tries, while that which we import mostly comes from the Balkan provinces. The real genuine perfume is most expensive,costing as much as £2O or £3O an ounce but it is usually largely adulterated with olive oil. its original purity, the perfume would be altogether too overpowering, as a siogle drop is enough to impregnate a large bottle of hair dressing. Hats with wide puckered brims, are to the fore just now. Should one form not prove becoming, a pull with finger and thumb quickly rearranges the pleats, thus one hat may be made to do duty for several by com- pletely altering the shape of the brim: Ribbons threaded through and through the straw are still cor- rect, and certainly have a pleasing appearance, while chiffon, waft silk, and sweeping ostrich plumes, suffice to furnish dressy, up-to-date headgear. Artificial flowers, roses particularly, are too plentiful to please the exclusive ones. The select few indeed. who like to be in advance, have given up roses alto- gether, and other artificial blooms will quickly follow them into temporary oblivion. The woman with limited allowance, will regret this state of affairs, for she knows how cheaply, simply and effectually a few sprays of artificial flowers smarten up a hat or toque. The multitudes have taken up, with such excessive zest the traneparent yoke and sleeve idea, that the well dressed woman has perfcrce had to relinguish the mode for outdoor day wear. The greatest care was ever necessary that no trace of vulgarity should be suggested, and the exhibitions familiar to one and all just now are such as to make us very promptly fill in frocks boasting a transparent neck and sleeves. Happily nothing is more easy than to transform hcelices so made. Long plain half sleeves to the elbow, there sewn inside the frills, can be made of the same material as the bodice, or of something that harmonises with it suitally. The chemisette can match these sleeves, or can be of some quite different fabric, touches of which,in other portions of the bodice attract the eye, and prevent a patched appearance. In some cases it is enough, merely to put silk linings under the transparencies. The nouveau art—Frencb in origin—came timidly to us across the water in the form of tie slides and other small ornaments. Finding favour, it now reaches U 9 in larger pieces and we are, at the present time offered toilet table sets, and tea and coffee sets made in it. The ware is decidedly phaaing, warmer than gold, and softer in coleuring than bronze or copper, yet with ehades and glints of all these metals. The designs are excellent,and for clasps and buckles much to be commended, but knowing full well the fickleness of feshion. it were well, before investing in whole services made in it, to question whether the inevitable change that will ensue, will not be rather a serious matter. Art nouveau has one decided merit. It can be used with silver, uxydised, or gold ornaments, without the Comparison being in any way invidious. An excellent and inexpensive drink for hot days may be made as follows, take 4oes of pearl barley, wash well, place in a large jug with five lumps of sugar, and half a lemon carefully peeled. Pour a quart ot boiling water into the jug, and leave until cold.</p>
        <p>MADGE."</p>
        <p>FRODSHAM.</p>
        <p>parry, held last eaW7ceds.neiszdroadyshaad mn THE RECENT BAZAA R AND GARDEN PARTY .--Th r. EROfofMl53lO. The first daT's bazaar and garden "rf:i.uurzs.ddre yt ,h ei n ht: the forme ue n (si sum t proceedings brought in £/30, and £l3O was taken en ire Second day This amount goes to provide the iripend of a curate for a poor district in he parish. DROWNING FATALITY. qII SAtUrchy. Mr J C Bate, crroner, held an inquest at the Bradley Orchard Farm. Frodsham, touching the death of George Entwiale,agel n 19 months, the scn of Tnomas Entwistle, farmer, of Bradley OrrMard.—Th. e2idenc,, showed that on Thurs. a Out t o play, and a short time I ter b his both' wag found in a pond near to the farm It orris ' ,t hi es ; parentsitle boym wentissed thought that while at play he slipped in the water—The jury returned a verdict to the effect that the child was accidentally drowned.</p>
        <p>The inoculation employed in stamping out cattle plague from the Soudan has been highly successful, for ur.l; t no CRP, has been reported for some weeks. AltorP"-r p enite :.tnh e tlnderpest. gatt t h work of aubj oe disease whahl been which rt 6nab se</p>
