      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>ATH OF DR. GORNALL Welty-one Years Medical Officer of Health.</head>
        <p>ATH OF DR. GORNALL Welty-one Years Medical Officer of Health.</p>
        <p>w,,ith profound regret, which will be sharec n -ifreaders, that we record the death of Mr.. nirinson Gornall, the Medical Officer o , for the County Borough of Warrington, who away at his residence, Ribblesdale, Grappen- tea minutes to si on Thursday evening last 'Or some monthopast the deceased gentleman's f health had given his relatives much anxiety a February 19-,h be found himself unable :barge the duties devolving upon him as kl Officer of Health. On that day be visited ice at Bank Rouse for the last time, but on occasions since then he has been able to iort walks in the neighbourhood of his home' ttely his strength declined, and he was 1 to take to his bed. His son, Dr Guest Gor- ttended to him most assiduously throughout greeted illness,and on Wednesday night per- a marked change for the worse. He at once telephonic message to one of his brothers, was in London, with the result that he was lo leave at midnight, and reached Ribblesiale ara on Thursday, when the son was just ?Weed by his dying father. Subsequently Mri 451 J became unconscious and died at the time • from pernicious ems-ale, after a hog and 44 Painful illness. He leaves a widow and `vith4 children, one of whom is serving qtet,., the South African forces. The late last public app?arance was on the viet"le the funeral of her late Majesty Queen thelit'a, when he attended the memorial service at ltlarish Church. .as,,t4ediately the sad intelligence of his death lT „alleeived in Warrington the flags at the Town • 5d Liberal Club were hoisted half-masti Liph„"'s Dr Gornall was the son of Mr John Gornal Qedtaaceutical chemist, and was born on the toe. 4.lovember, 1841, at No 1 Market Place, Pres- iis grandfather also was a highly respected °nian, and died on the 15th March, 1867, at 4..1• ',130 old age of 98. The late Dr J H Gornall educatd eat Mr Thomas Hammond's school, at 1141 10. ton-le-Nylde, and at the Liverpool College, tuglly 0 napprenticedof w a ver t rwe ei h his uncle, the late Dr 511081573 f . itlieedbiceicnaemien 1861, a stu at the Liverpool - took the qualifications of the member of the 0)751 College of Surgeons (England) and Licentiate tit..he Apothecaries Society (London), in 1864. His sinointment was as Junior House Surgeon to her_ Preston Dispensary, his senior colleague Idep the .late Mayor of Preston. Mr John ser:eri. JP. He was appointed resident 0n at the Warrington Dispensary in 1865 posit. lon which he occupied for several years. He en co Noem mmenoed private practice.but shortly after- 187 e 8,17 as appointed Union Medical Officer, and in Olio Vaccinator. The former appointment 4sasteigried, but the latter he retained until his He was appolnted Medical Officer of Health asor the Borough of Warrington on October 26th, ' and haa discharged the arduous duties deVolv- • I.Don him in a manner which has gained tl4ellll the highest esteem of all who came into Contact with him. On the same date he was 11111;2'.inted Public Analyst and held the position 1 tic,;l a few weeks ago when he resigned the office, u h also ..,. is Son was appointed in his stead. He has ae_.'ilacharged the duties of Medical Superinten- -ogt of the Fever Hospital since January Ist 1878. tint,ebtaining the first named position he retired ygether from private practice. Dr Gornall was 6.,", of the honorary consultant Medical Officers of 's Warrington Infirmary. In 1885 the valuable nlrrices which he had rendered to the town were tingly recognized by his appointment as a Justice "i the Peace for the Borough, and until his health ' ,atnmenced to fail him his attendance on the Bench Zits most regular. Dr Gornall was also a e'ternber of the British Medical Association otti the Incorporated Society of Medical 0 f Health. 0 January 26th, 1866, • ar3 p: was married to Mary, third daughter the late Mr John Guest, tanner, who survives 02. In politics he was a staunch supporter of the tliherul cause, and by the members ot the Liberal Isilh and Association his demise will be very deeply 14re tted. ---- INith every mark of respect, the remains of the br J ii Gornall, medical officer of health for Borough, were interred in Warrington • etery on Saturday. It was after five tsOck when the corter reached the cemetery itss:ra a number of sympathising friends had abled. At the invitation of the Mayor, tObers of the Corporation assembled at kh,Town Hall at half-past three and drove to dep"lesdale, Grappenhall, the late re4denca of the steed, which was reached at about four o'clock. (4"8, present included: The Rev John Youge 41,4.Y0r's chaplain), the Town Clerk (Mr J Lyon Alderman Sutton, Councillors S Davies, J iarn Roberts, W Brough, T Burton,J Smethurst, Ilkinson, and W J Forshaw; .11r Robert Garnett, Birdsall, Mr J W Hayward, Mr H Thornton 194°„r°11gh magistrates) : Mr T Longdin (borough i10..,7eY0r), Mr Ralph Norman (chief inspector of tto`"aeces), Mr James Deas (manager of the depots water engineer), the Chief Constable (Mr iNeu°t), Mr William Jones (chief school attendance • Pector), Mr Robert Young (Cemetery regie.- ea, ate, Mr James PoWell (assistant inspector of rhit'arices), and Mr (31" Holtord. At R.bbles • roo3 the procession was joined by the k 11 TrIRrA from the house, namely—lst carringe, r %as Gornall (widow), Dr J Guest Gornall and Mrs tio"t Gornall (son and daughter-in-law), and Miss tkr_nall (daughter) ; 2nd cei.iage, Miss Annie ,Crball, Miss Mary Gornall, the Rev fl K Gornap, ad eareenr Frank Gornall (dauehters and sons) ; 3rd 14 cila_ge, Mr E J Guest, Mr George Guest, the Rev the Koberte, ant Mr R G Ilenshall ; 4th carriage, thy llama and Miss Lawton, and the fifth carriage 1011" ,servants from Ribbles4ale. Amongst others theplao joined the procession at Ribblesdale were cicoinror,the Deputy-Mayor,(Alderman James Fair- er.. -g4). Alderman Feancomb, Mr John Feirclough, trotoillor Ellison, Councillor Greening, Mr Arthur t) oT,ne• !%lr S K,-nyon, and Mr Walter T Flood. The Pojile-ssion was headed by members of the Borough steoe