      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>Speedy GM Inalmani se</head>
        <p>Speedy GM Inalmani se</p>
        <p>Bathe the affected parts with Hot Water and. Cuticura Soap, to cleanse the surface of crusts and scales, and soften the thickened cuticle. Dry, without hard rubbing, and apply Cuticura Ointment freely, to allay itching, irritation, and inflammation, and soothe and deal, and lastly, take Cuticura Resolvent, to GOA and cleanse the blood. A single set (price, 65.) is often sufficient to cure the most torturing, disfiguring, and humiliating skin, scalp, andblood humours, with loss of hair, when all else fails.</p>
        <p>This sweet and wholesome treatment affords instant relief., permits rest and sleep in the severest forms of eczema and other itching, burning, and scaly humours of the skin, scalp, and blood, and points to a speedy, permanent ,and economical cure when all other remedies fail. The agonizing itching and burning of the skin, as in eczema; the frightful scaling, as in psoriasis; the loss of hair and crusting of the scalp, as in scalled head; the facial disfigurement, as in pimples and ringworm; the awful suffering of inta.nts and the anxiety of worn-out parents, as in milk crust, tetter, and salt rheum aldemand a remedy of almost superhuman virtues to successfully. cope with them. That CUTICITRA. remedies are such stands proven beyond all doubt. No statement is made regarding them that is not justified by the strongest evidence. The purity and sweetness, the power to afford immediate relief, the certainty of speedy and permanent cure, the absolute safety and great economy, have made them the standard skin cures and humour remedies of the civilized world,</p>
        <p>PRICE, THE SET, 68.; consisting Of CIITICURA SOAP, la., CI:MOUE! OINTMENT, 28. ed., CT:TI- M:rill. RESOLVENT, 2s. ed., of all chemists throughout the world; or. post-paid by F. NEWBERY SC Soria, 27, Charterhhouse Square, London, E. C. "How to Cure Every Rumour," post free. POTTER DRUG, AND CHEM. CORP., Sole Props., of CIITICURA. REMEDIES.</p>
        <p>and the Borough Treasurer (Nlr John Fairhurst and the following members of the Warrington Medico-Ethical Society :—Drs White, Fox; Joseph, Richmond, Braide, Peacocke, Bowden and Lange- dale, and Dr Watkins (Newton-le-Willows.) j The service at the graveside was impressively conducted by the Rev H. G Roberts,M. A. ( iiresleya ministfr). Dr Gornall's favourite hymn "Rock of Ages" was snug. Mr Nicholson on behalf of Messrs R Garnett and Son, was responsible for the funeral arrangements. The coffin was of fumed oak, with brass mountings. The plats bore the following inscription:—"John Hankinson Gornall, born No- vember 22, 1841, died July 18, 1901." Wraaths were sent by the following:—Widow and family, Dr and Mrs Guest Gornall, Master Laurence Gornall (grandson), servants and gar- deners at Ribblesdale,Mr and Mrs Down, the Mayor and Mayoress, the officials of the Corporation, Mr and Mrs W Pierpoint, the Warrington Medico Ethical Society, Mr Norman and staff of the Health Department, Mias Walker (matron of the Infec- tious Diseases Hospital), the nurses and servants at Aikin-street Hospital, Mr and Mrs Hands (lodge keeper), the Liberal Club, an old servant (Mary Marsh). There was also a•wreath made from flowers taken from his own garden, "the garden he loved," as it was described. Attached to , one of the wreaths was a photograph of the camp of the Imperial Yeo- manry at Buluwayo, and which had been sent by one of deceased's sons, who is on active service in 1 South Africa. It was received on the morning of the funeral, the sender, of course, having no idea that his father had passed away.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>Pulpit Reference by the Rev. H. G. . Roberts.</head>
        <p>Pulpit Reference by the Rev. H. G. . Roberts.</p>
        <p>Preaching in Bold-street Wesleyan Church, on Sunday morning, the Rev II G Roberts alluded to the late Dr Gornall in the following terms :—I cannot close my address this morning without reference to the service I was called upon to conduct yesterday afternoon, when we commended into the hands of mercy the soul of our departed brother, Dr Gornall, and committed his body to the earth. He and:several members of his family have been associated with this church. Dr Gornall was one of the first to welcome me at the commencement of my ministry in this town—a ministry I shall close in a few days, and I feel it my duty to pay a humble tribute to his memory. Doctors, taking them as a whole, are a race of men that we may justly be proud of. I do not know another class of men for whom I have greater admiration. No other class bf men are more devoted to their work, more self-sacrificing, more ready to run hazards, and suffer inconvenience, and he at the bock and call of all who are ill and even fancy they are ill. They display a heroism that cannot easily be i paralleled n any other pursuit or profession. If a fever or plague breaks out, they stick to their post, while other men escape tor their lives. They walk in and amongst the. sick and suffering, dying, thinking not of themselves, upon one subjoct—to lessen the ravages of disease, and minister to the needs of those who are sick. We praise our soldiers for their courage, devotion, and heroism. We cannot but honour the orave men who fearlessly peril their lives. We hold in high memory those who have nobly eridured and heroically died for the sake of country: But they are no more deserving of praise, no more worthy of honour than many of the doctors of our towns and cities, who fight unseen foes fearlessly, and often lay down their lives .in the cause of hurnaeity. Dr Gornall was singularly devoted to his profession, and from what I have heard from a reliable source be was scrupulously observant of the minutest reqeire- ts and details of the responsible position he held in this town for so many years. He always had a deep interest in the material and social well are of the people, and sbbscribed liberally, as I can bear witness, to many agencies and institu- tions which have for their object tbe domestic uplifting the educational elevation, and moral ennoblement of his townsmen. The Wesleyan Church cannot claim him as a member, for he was baptised and confirmed in another section of the holy Catholic Church, but he elbowed a very practical interest in all churches that endeavoured to meet the needs of the age. As a man, he despised whatever was little and mean, but had true sympathy with the suffering, the struggling, and the weak, as I can bear witness. In the sketch of his life which appeared in the local press of yesterday. I read that he did not associate himself definitely with any creed. Personally I have no fear. A man is not necessarily outside the Church of God because he can- not accept all the dogmas of the Christian faith. Indeed he may be very near the Kingdom of Heaven, while he is able to accept scarcely any of them. Religion is not a question of opinion, but of life, and many of those who stand outside our churches because they cannot pronounce our shiblioleths are doing work . with an energy, and enthusiasm, and deVotion that put to shame some of us who make louder profession. Their Seeming indifference to creeds may after all imply a deeper,truer, and more acceptable loyalty to Got and tiod's creatures than the unruffled orthodoxy of those whose faith has never been subjected to the strain of a perplexed mind and a wounded heart. Providing a man is honest, and sincere, and anxious to find the truth and love it and 'oyal to the highest and deepest instincts of his nature, we may take comfort at the thought that hi e s not far from the Kingdom of God. Into thc sacred privacy of his life it would be something like an impertinence to intrude, but I think I may tee allowed to say here that deep down in his nature and instinct, Dr Gornall remained loyal to the faith of biS early days, When death drew near to him, and he stood on the herder line of eternity, be dis- covered that he had not parted from his beliefs, that that he could not escape from t them, hey tehraetstrieilligreiognaant' was not a matter of opinion, that it was a part of his being. I fancy that the majority of people find out sooner or later that this is very true. Some of tie are mere or less cynical at times and all who think have to pass through periods of doubt. We fight our way through darkness into the light, we win our victory in the valley of despair. No man can help his doubts—no man can hush the voice of reason. Mysterious questions will obtrude themselves—questions for which we can find no answer. There are so many mysteries which we can only nehTohldeseat aardeisptaentpcele,x7indg in the attitude of reverence. problems in glace and providence which we can only solve on our knees. This is a critical age, but the spirit of inquiry is not all that is needed in the face of divine mrterie.s, it must be supplemented by a spirit of reverence— we must bow our heads and recognize the divinity of the mystery. Doubt as we may despair as we may, in our most solemn moments, when sorrosv touches our hearts, or eicknes3 lays its hand upon us,or death looms near, the imperishable instinct of our nature will assert itself, and our hearts cry out for the living God. We pray that the bereaved family will,bs sustained by the consolation of divine grace. During the service a special prayer was offered, on behalf of the family of the deceased, and the organist (Mr F Hickman), played th,, volunta.:es : "Blest are the departed" (Spoht) and " The Bette! Land" (Cowen). The service concluded with the singing of the hymn" Give me the wings of faith to rise."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>H E EX A m IN14.1(, FRI I )AY, J ULY 26 1901</head>
        <p>H E EX A m IN14.1(, FRI I )AY, J ULY 26 1901</p>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>I is surprising how many things come people dc aot learn from experience. THE mills of the gods grind slowly, as a rule, but they sometimes get a :mace on that paralyzes some of us. IT is a singular fact that the man who knows it all usually disagrees w.t.. the other man who knows it all. A MONOPOLY is a good deal like by a baby. A man is opposed to it on general princi des until he has one of his own. OFTEN THE CASE.--" The minister weighs his words very carefully." "Still, he doesn't seem to realise how heavy his sermons are." AFTER TIIE ESTRANGEMENT.—HObSOII : "Do you and Ciaatterton speak nowadays ?" ll,bson : " He does—l listen." "WHY, I'd like to know," said a lady to a judge, "cannot a woman become a successful lawyer?" "Because she's too fond of giving her opinion without pay," answered the judge. "OUT of work again, Pit? I thought that old -Ikinflint gave you a job?" "He did, sor ; but 'II be kilt afore 01'11 starve to death for the sake t kapin' aloive, sor." •• GliosafE, I'm glad . to see that you are polite offer sister the oranges first."—" ~so then she has to be polite, an' take th' little</p>
        <p>HE: " I have called on you seven years, Miss Waite. All this time I have passioLately laved you. To-morrow or the next day I shall probably come up and ask you to marry me. What will you say ?"—Miss Waite (confused): "Oh, this is so sudden 1" •</p>
        <p>--- IT WAS AT TILE CLUB.—Waiter (at 11 p.m.): "There is a lady outside who says that her hus- band promised to be home early to.night."—All (rising): " EXCUSE: me for a moment." Ma. HOOLIGAN: "Shure, 'tis a :mighty con- vanient arrangement living in wan apartment. Mi Finueaue. Woi, whin Oi want to go fro:a the dbrawing-room to the shtudio or the lotchen, Ui just stay where Oi am." ON reaching a certain spot the driver turned round on his seat and observed to the passengers : —" From this point the road is only accessible to mules and donkeys ; I must therefore ask the gen- tlemen to get out and proceed on foot. THE MAGISTRATE : "Didn't I tell you the last time that you were here that I wanted to see your face in this court no more?" Weary Wat- kins "You did, yer onner, and that is exactly what I told the cop. PERKINS: "When I knew Harry he used to paddle his own canoe; but now he seems to be a perfect wreck." Jorkins : "Yes ; but since then he got married, and his wife is one of those crea- tures that like to rock the boat." His DEFINITION. Little Nell: -" What is imagination, Willie ?" Little Will : "It is what makes you think a bee's stinger is six feet long:" RESCUE.—" He shall bite the dust !" hissed the wicked tyrant. "Is there no hope ? " faltered the heroine. "Hark !" exclaimed the hero. " MtthiLks I hear the clatter of hoofs. Perhaps it</p>
        <p>is -a watering-cart." BANK DEFAULTER. : "I would give ten years of my life to get OUD of this scrape." His Lawyer : "That's about what you will get if I don't succeed in getting you off." FARMER HORNDEAK : "You know how old Zeb Flint hain't had no faith in banks, an' has been keepin' his money in a teapot in the barn where he could go an' gloat over it whenever he ;eh like it?" Mrs. Hornbeak " Well, what has hap- puled, Ezry ? " Farmer Hornbeak (drily): " Wa- al, Zeb, hain't got no faith in barns now." • MILLICENT:"How long did your trip to Rome occupy ?" Madeline: "Oh, a week altogether— there and back." Millicent: "And you saw everything?" Madeline : "Oh, yes; you see, there were three of us. Mother went to the picture-galleries, I examined the monuments, and father studied local colour in the cafés." LORD GLASGOW was once travelling by rail, and tendered a five-pound note to the booking-clerk for a ticket. Put your name ma it," said the youth, and Lord Glasgow indorsed it as requested, putting simply the name "Glasgow," as is cue- tomary, with nobles, and handed the note back. " Here, hi ! you stupid man !" cried the clerk ; "I want to know who you are, and not where you're going to." HUSBAND (breathlessly)—"l am to start on a trip in two hours, and you can go with me if you can get ready." Wife—" Certainly I can. It won't take me more than 10 minutes to pack, and that will leave me one hour and 50 minutes to dress."</p>
        <p>- "Now, children," said the Sunday school teacher of the juvenile class, "our lesson to-day tells us of the powers possessed by kings aml queens. Can any of you name a still greater power?" " Yes'm ; I can," replied one little fellow. "What, Willie? " asked the teacher. "Aces," was the unexpected answer. DOLLIE -4 I wonder why Love is represented always with wings?" Mollie—" They are for him to use in flying, out of the window when Poverty comes in at the door. Don't you know that much ? " . _</p>
        <p>NcrwED—" According to you, I never told you a single truth before we were married." Mrs. Nuwed—" Oh, George, you weren't qui to SO bad as all that. Don't you remember you always used to say you were unworthy of me?" MISTRFSS : "Not going to marry that sweep after all, Jane? Why, I thought it was all set- led." Jane : "So it was, mum ' • but the fact is, I saw him with a clean face for the first time last night, and I can't marry him! You've no idea how ugly he is when he's washed, mum." TALK to woman about religion, she sighs ; talk to her of love, she simpers ; talk to her of art, she yawns; talk to her of science, she goes to sleep ; but talk to her of dress, and she will open her eyes and give you the entire attention of her ears. HE KNEW' THE SEx'S WAYS.—Mia Shopper: "Do you think you will be able to match this tibbon ?" Draper's Young Man (with a sigh of re. signation) : "After you have looked at everything else I shall." A DOUBTFUL SIGN.—" I used to think nly hus- band loved me," said Mrs. M'Bridie. with a sigh.. "And doesn't he ?" inquired her bosom friend. " Well, he insists upon our living within his in- come." A RICH bil,t ignorant lady, who was rather am. bitiuus in her conversational style, in speaking of a triend said—" He is a paragram of politeneEs." " Excuse me," said a wag sitting next to her, " but do you not mean a parallelogram ? " "Of coiirFe I do," immediately replied the lady ; " how could I have made such a mistake?" " WIiEN are you going to settle this bill?" "We've had a settlement already." " When?" "The last time you called." "How so?" "Didn't I tell you then that I meant to settle the bill ? " "Yes." "Very well, then ; wasn't that a settle meant? MINNIE : " Mr. Billie, if I should consent to be I your wife, are you sure you would never object to my dressmaker's bills, never refuse to buy theatre. tickets when asked, never—er -in fact, never object to any expenses which I might wish to incur?" Mr. Billie "Darling, I swear it Minnie: " Well, Mr. Billie, if you can't hold on to your money any better than that I must say</p>
        <p>• "OH, papa ! " exclaimed little four-year-old Ned. "Look what a bright star ! " "Yes." replied the father, "and it three times as large as our earth." "Oh, no, it isn't," said Ned. " Why do you doubt it?" asked his father. "Because if it was it would keep the rain oft" SUNDAY-SCHOOL teacher (illustrating the "still, small voice ")—" What is it, dear children, that makes you feel so uncomfortable and unhappy after you have done something which you ought not to do?" Dear child—" A lickin.' " FARMEICS Wife (of vinegary visage): "Nem you get away from here quick, or call my husban I." Tramp: "Your husband ain't :it home." Farmer's Wife: "How do you know?" Tramp : " Well, mum, I've milers noticed that when a man is marr ad to a woman mot looks like you he's never at ht.me 'cepting at meal times." LITTLE Elmer: "Pa, what is an extemporaneous speaker?" Professor Broadhead "One who can talk fluently about nothing without any previous nrenaration.”</p>
