        <p>could nct forget. xet I saw her for but a moment. The old woman, a brown, wizen-faced hag, urged her forward in a sharp tone, and reluctantly she passed on with shuffling gait away z.ait of my sight. On several succeeding days the strange pair passed; always about the same hour, the young girl always lingering, the aged slave always hasty and ill-tempered. From the folds of her clinging silken draperies a sweet odour of attar of roses was left upon the air, and as she walked her golden anklets jingled musically. I longed to speak, to inquire the cause of the ineffable sadness in those dark, brilliant eyes, but dared not. Time after time we exchanged glances, but neither of us spoke. My fame as a reciter of mysteries and romances had been carried, I suppose, within the walls of the great Fada, or palace of the Sultan Rabab, that powerful and dreaded ruler of all that wide, wild country north of Lake Chad. Of the magnifi- cence of this wonderful palace there were many rumours, but when, being commanded thither, I was conducted through its handsome courts, I found it even more extensive and luxurious than reported. It was in the gilt Hall of the Divan, wherein, under a canopy of amaranth silk, the dark- faced Sultan sat, with his Grand Vizier, Aghas and other officers surrounding him, that I obeyed the Royal command and related some of my most popular stories of mystery. For a full hour I entertained them, and even the Sultan himself grunted approbation. How his manner would have changed had he known he was entertaining an officer of the Infidels on secret service! "Whence didst thou learn of these strange adven- tures ? " the Sultan asked, handing his pipe to a slave boy, and generously ordering the keeper of the Treasury to give me thirty pieces of gold. "In the north I learnt them, 0 Ruler," I answered, bowing until my brow touched the carpet. "In Algeria, the country now over-run with those dogs of Roumis, eh? " "Even so, 0 Sultan." But the mere thought of Christians aroused within the great obese potentate a violent ebullition of anger. "May the Infidels be consigned to the tortures of Al-Hawyiat and may their vitals be burnt !" he cried, with flashing eye. "Their Christian idolatry is more hateful in the sight of the One Allah than Pagan blindness. The purest and wisest religion cannot be revealed to the most unthankful and ignorant of mankind. The pearl must not be cast before swine, and the children of Allah must not be deprived of their inheritance. But the worm must not fly, the ignorant judge nor dust perfume. May Allah burn the invaders! " "From thy mouth fall pearls of wisdom, 0 Ruler," observed the Grand Vizier, a white- bearded old man, who humbly prostrated himself before his Royal master • but the latter rose with impatience, and gave the signal that the audience was at an end. As, on my way out, passing through the carious courts of the great Fade, the entrance to each of which was guarded night and day by gigantic black slaves armed with keen dokas—great curved swords, broad, and razor-edged—an old female slave crept towards me in the dim half-light, whispering: "Wilt thou, El-Adth, stay thy footsteps for one brief moment? We must not be observed, but one who knoweth thee desireth brief speech with thee. Come, follow me." In silence, wondering what friend could desire to speak to me, I accompanied her across the ciniet court, where a cool fountain softly plashed, then, entering a low door, she took up a lighted lamp, and we passed swiftly along a narrow passage until we came to a second door, which she unlocked with a key she took from beneath her garments. As I stepped forward my nostrils were greeted by sweet odours rising from gold perfum- ing pans, and when my eyes grew accustomed to the semi-darkness I found myself in a small but luxuriously-furnished apartment. From a divan opposite a slim female form in wide trousers of white chine silk, a velvet gold-embroidered zouave, with dainty little fez covered with seed-pearls set jauntily on her head, rose with languorous zrace, and stretching forth her hand, welcomed me. She was veiled, but our eyes met. It was the sad-eyed girl who had halted to hear portions of my romances. "Peace, 0 friend," she said in her musical Arabic, motioning me to a divan near her own. "Bhadidja, daughter of the Sultan, desireth thine aid, and giveth thee welcome." "If it be within my power to grant thee any service, command me," I answered, seating myself. She motioned to her slave, who vanished; then, after referring to the occasions she had passed me at the street corner, she said: "I am, alas! in sore distress, and confess to thee, imploring thine aid and -counsel, for I know that thou, an Infidel from Algiers, can assist me—" "How didst thou know that I am not what I have said I am? " I gasped, astounded. "Thy mission hath been discovered. The plan of the city that thou hest prepared for the Army of the Infidels is already in the hands of Stavil, our Grand Vizier, and to-night he bath arranged for secret audience of my father, the Sultan," she said in an earnest whisper. Then, noticing the look of terror upon my countenance, she added quickly: "Obey me; act quickly without fear, and thou ;halt of a verity succeed." "But knowest thou of what lam in search ? " I</p>
        <p>inquired. "Thou seekest the great Koran of Hamouda, our book of Everlasting Will," she answered. "Then give ear to my counsel, for through me may thine efforts be crowned with success." "Then tell me, 0 Khadidja," I cried, "tell me how must I act to obey thee, and thus secure the reward." 1 "An hundred eyes watch thee and note thine every movement," she answered. "Hence, only by the secret way that leadeth through the Treasure House canst thou leave this city. In return for thine aid I will make a compact with thee, by which ' thou canst secure that which thou seekest and by right of conquest belongeth unto the Infidels; afterwards will I skew thee the only channel of escape." "And of what nature is the service thou desirest me to perform ?" "Know, then, 0 friend, that I am sore distressed because my father bath arranged for me a marriage with Afan, the Prince of Bagirmi, an aged despot, the reports of whose cruelties have reached me here, even in the seclusion of mine own apart- ments. I hate him, but love another. Alas ! he is of the tribe of Beni-Guil,and dares not approach this, the Accursed City. Briefly, I desire to leave this place, upon which Eblis, the Evil One, bath set his seal, and journey to the north unto the man I love. News hath been conveyed unto me that thou art the faithful of the Infidels, there- fore I may place my trust in thee. Take me hence unto Biskra, and as reward assuredly shalt thou place beneath thy gandoura the Koran of Hamouda." She spoke with a terrible earnestness, and by the dim light from the great hanging lamp of chiselled brass I could see the yearning, wistful look in her beautiful eyes. "I agree," I answered anxiously, springing to my feet. "I will take thee, and with mine own life defend thee until we reach the Oasis of Biskra, if only thou wilt shew me where the great Koran is hidden." "No," she said, "I cannot shew thee. I dare not take thee unto the place where the Will of , the Prophet is concealed ; but I will indicate to thee the right path, and if thou fearest not, then shalt thou secure thy reward." "No danger shall deter me, 0 daughter of the Sultan," I answered fervently. "Then let not thy footsteps tarry a single instant," she cried, rising quickly and readjusting her tiny green slipper. 'Already thine enemies seek thee, and if we were discovered our heads would fall beneath their dokas. So fly I—hither ! Rough is the path and high the mount on which the treasure is preserved," and taking up the lamp the old slave had left, she crossed the rgom, and as she raised a heavy curtain a small door was disclosed. "Be thou but resolved to tread the' crooked and laborious path, with its descent and ascent ; thou shalt not return, but I will meet thee beyond, when thou bast discovered what thou seekest. Thou wilt find the treasure concealed in a chest, the lock of which no key can open; but to thee the secret will be revealed. Go, and upon thee be perfect peace." And as she opened the small door I took the light from her hand and passed into a narrow passage similar to that by which I had approached her private apartments, then, descending sharply, I soon entered a kind of natural cavern. The lower I went the more filth ' and stench I found, to which submitting with Patience I. by a long passage, sometimes crawling</p>
        <p>inner rugged arches, sometimes wading in mud and dirt, attained the end of the cavern, where I</p>
        <p>stumbled on some narrow steps; but the lamp shed little iight, and I became nearly suffocated by the noisome vapours. The widening ascent was so</p>
        <p>intricate and cloggei with dirt and rubbish that I worked like a mole in the dark; nevertheless, by</p>
        <p>diligent industry I gained ground considerably; yet as I endeavoured to mount the steps slit ped from under me, and ever and anon I would tome tumbling down with a weight of dirt upon me. Nothing but my intolerable situation could have supported me in the odious undertaking, but meanness and wretchedness know no evils greater</p>
        <p>than themselves. 111 After various labours, however, I suddenly entered a great cavern, quite dry, which, judging from the many bales of merchandise it contained, I concluded was the Sultan's treasure-house. From the Fads to the place wherein the great Rabah's wealth was concealed this secret way existed, no doubt, for purposes of escape in case the Accursed City were invested by a victorious foe. From the roof hung great stalactites that glittered and shone in the lamplight, while around the rough walls of this natural chamber were heaped many vessels of gold and silver, bejewelled shields and swords, trinkets, girdles, and a miscellaneous collection of the spoils of war. There were great camel's packs, evidently ready for a journey, and prepared, no doubt, in the event of the Sultan Rabah being corn- _ .</p>
        <p>pelled. to flee before his enemies. At first-, how- ever, I could see nothing of the iron chest of which lihadidja had spoken; but at length I discovered it—a ponderous casket bound with great bands of rusted steel. Eagerly I examined its massive lock _ _ . . . . . „ _ .</p>
        <p>—the lock which7she.had _told me "could not be</p>
        <p>opened by any key "—and found it of strange and curious manufacture. Across the keyhole a piece of thick cloth had been pasted, and this I quickly removed. Then it occurred to me that if this chest could not be opened by a key, then only by some secret device might the lid be raised. Long and patiently I tried to force up the heavy lid, but, even though I tried for quite an hour, until the perspiration rolled from my brow in great beads, and my breath came with difficulty, all my efforts were futile. At last I paused to rest, glancing around half fearfully in the impenetrable darkness that encompassed me, and feeling that even at the eleventh hour I must relinquish my search, and-perhaps pay with my life the penalty of my intrepidity, until presently, remembering the words of the veiled daughter of Rabah, I again returned to the attack with an iron crowbar I</p>
        <p>found upon the ground. But, alas! the lid would not budge one hair's breadth. The great sheet of iron must have weighed fully half a ton. Suddenly, however, as I bent once again to examine the lock, a bright flash proceeded from it, and in an instant, as I fell back terrified at the terrific explosion, the ponderous lid was torn from its hinges and hurled off. For a second, my eyes blinded by the flash, I feared to look, but,with the pungent odour of gunpowder filling my nostrils, it occurred to me that the chest must have been</p>
        <p>hermetically sealed, and that the lock, a false one, had been filled with an explosive, so that in case of alarm it might be quickly opened and its</p>
        <p>contents secured.</p>
        <p>A spark from my lamp had accidentally come into contact with the powder. .</p>
        <p>As the smoke cleared I peered eagerly within, and there, amidst a heap of magnificent and rare jewels, lay the historic manuscript in its binding of solid gold and setting of diamonds and emeralds, just as it had BO often been described. With a cry of joy I drew it forth, and examined the enormous centre diamond that sparkled in the lamplight with a thousand iridescent rays; then tearing a piece from my soiled burnouse, I wrapped it carefully therein, and heedless of the other wealth, took up the lamp to seek some means of</p>
        <p>exit. For a long time I was baffled, but at last I discovered a low hole, through which I was com- pelled to grope on all fours, then, ascending a little, found a place where rough steps, similar to those by which I had approached, began to enlarge. These I eagerly climbed, and by degrees perceived from above a glimmering light, to which ascend- ing, the nearer I drew to it the plainer I could hear a confused sound of voices echoing from the top, which increased as I rose, until I could plainly distinguish it must proceed from some concourse of people without. When I had reached the uppermost step, over which a hole opened sufficient for a man to crawl through, the clamours were so terrifying that I feared to proceed; at last, considering that death must be the consequence of remaining in the cavern amid those deadly vapours, I boldly ventured forth. No sooner did I appear through the opening of the cavern than a hundred voices cried out all at once: "Long live the beloved of Khadidja, whom the mountains of Iro have brought forth! " And looking round I saw a crowd of male and female slaves grouped around the Sultan's daughter, all watching me. A number of them stepped forward to disengage me from the cavern's mouth, and I, so spent with sores and fatigue, was obliged for some minutes to be supported. But soon a rich silken robe was thrown about me, and I was mounted on a fine whirl, or swift camel, already packed for a Journey, while Khadidja likewise mounting a similar animal, we together rode away alone, her slaves still crying out with ecstasy and rapture: "Peace unto Khadidja and her lover, before whose presence even the sun darkens ! " When we had crossed a smiling country, red with flowers and green with many leaves, I sought of her some explanation of the remarkable ovation, and she then told me that in order to escape she had to deceive her slaves by summoning a lover from the Cavern of the Great Death, at the same time declaring that the will of the one from under the earth must not be thwarted. The existence of the Treasure House was unknown to any save the Sultan and his family, and as her slaves stood there awaiting me they had heard the report of the explosion, and believed some superhuman power was actually at work. Hence, in accordance with Khadidja's plans, they had allowed us to depart unmolested, and they no doubt carried back to the Accursed City the amazing news that the beauteous daughter of Rabah had been seized , and abducted by a visitant from the nether world. After we had traversed one march my pretty companion, in order to secure her safety, unveiled and exchanged her scented robes for the burnouse and haick of a desert-dweller; but even attired as a youth her face was undeniably beautiful. At Agades we succeeded in joining a caravan going North ; yet even though we were travelling com- panions through nearly three whole moons, she always refused to tell me anything _further about herself. Once or twice, however, I fancied that secret attempts were made by some of our com- panions to search my camel's packs. She desired to get to Biskra; that was all the explanation she would vouchsafe. With my treasure safely con- cealed, I journeyed with a light heart towards Algiers, puzzled over the inscrutable mystery that surrounded her. On arrival at the edge of the Filiah Oasis, however, lat length parted from her, not without reluctance, for she was altogether adorable. She gave me no clue to her destination, but on the last night we encamped prior to entering the town of Biskra itself she suddenly disappeared on foot. leaving me her camel, with its costly trappings, as a souvenir. At the Hotel du Sahara at Biskra on the follow- ing night I slept in the first bed since my departure from civilisation, nearly nine months before, and beneath my pillow reposed the Koran of Hamouda. In the night some sound awakened me, and I dis- tinctly saw a dark form crouching near. Reaching my revolver, I fired point blank at it; then, jumping up, dashed to the spot. Next second there was a swish of drapery through the open window, and the intruder had gone. Was this, I wondered, another attempt to obtain possession of the sacred relic? After explaining to the excited hotel servants that I had fired in the belief that a thief had entered, I lay awake guarding my treasure during the remainder of the night, and next day took the train to Algiers, where, amid general congratulations and promise of promotion, I handed to the General of Division the Koran with its glittering jewels intact. Telegraphic information quickly reached Paris of the successful accomplishment of my mission, and in a couple of days the papers were full of my adventures, and I found myself one of the heroes of travel. Within a week orders came granting me a long leave of absence, and returning to Paris for a holiday I one morning entered the Military Club, of which I had long been a member. "For m'sieur," exclaimed the gilt-braided concierge, emerging from his box and handing me a small postal packet that looked like jewellery. I took ir into one of the rooms, cut the string, and quickly opened it. As I did so a soiled. and crumpled piece of paper fell out., and taking it up 1 read, traced in Arabic characters, the words: "A souvenir of the traitress." With trembling hand I lifted the lid, and as I did so a cry of horror escaped me.</p>
        <p>We had been followed; the long arm of the great Sultan Rabah had reached his daughter, even within the European sphere of influence! Inside was a human eye, covered with filmy glaze, shrivelled, yet still luminous; the once bright and beautiful eye of Rhadidja, Princess of the Accursed City.</p>
        <p>(Tim END.I</p>
