      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>(Copyright.)</head>
        <p>(Copyright.)</p>
        <p>THE ACCURSED CITY.</p>
        <p>BY WILLIAM LE QUETJX, Author of "Zoraida," "The Temptress," "Devil's</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>Dice," &amp;c.</head>
        <p>Dice," &amp;c.</p>
        <p>Deep in the limitless red-brown wilderness elf the Erg, one of the most arid regions of the Great Sahara, I was riding in the spring of last year beside the pious old Sheikh Omar ben Atta at the head of his great camel caravan, bound for Kanano, that mystic desert-city of the far south, wherein not half a dozen Europeans have ever entered. In those parched regions the sky at noon-day is milk- white, the sand-laden wind is a veritable breath from an oven, and the sun's rays beat down upon one like shafts of fire, while to the lonely traveller a lame camel or a pricked water-skin is synonymous with death. But as an officer of Chasseurs d'Afrique, who had long ago renounced the asphalt of his native Paris for the wild, free life of the desert, I had _grown accustomed to the hardships of travel- ling without one's bath, and subsisting on kouss- /cause and brackish water, and could, is occasion required, pray a maghrib with all the genuflections of a genuine eon of Al-Islam But I could not disguise from myself that my mission to that little-known city in the heart of the Tintumma Desert, north of Lake Chad, was highly dangerous, and would require all my tact and endurance in order to successfully accomplish it. A month ago, while lounging in the -Easbah at Algiers—once the great harem of the Dey, where hundreds of captive English and French women pined and died, but now our barracks—Colonel Bonnemain had called me to his quarters and entrusted to me the commission of a secret service entailing considerable risk. "You have been chosen for this most important work, Captain Martinon, on account of your wide experience of the Arabs of the South, and your intimate knowledge of the Tarkiyeh dialect," he said kindly, "but our General of Division wishes me to emphasise the fact that it will be necessary for you to cross the country of the Tuaregs in disguise, and of course you are well aware what that means." "Death, if I'm discovered, I replied with a grim smile. Nevertheless, I expressed my willing- ness to make the attempt, for, truth to tell, a little affair of the heart had turned out a wretched fiasco, and I was desolate. The Great Desert was no fresh experience, for during seven years mine had been no idle life at the out-post at Idikelt. But now as, attired in haick and burnouse, I rode along under the blazing sun that shone like burnished copper, chatting to the white-bearded leader of the caravan, I reflected that the object of my mission was indeed a strange one. Three years before, during the revolt against French authority at Constantine, which it will be remembered led to a great and lamentable lose of life on both sides, the historic bejewelled copy of the Koran of the Hadj Hamouda, renowned as the finest and certainly the most valuable manu- script extant, had been stolen. Its binding was of pure gold, into which were set great diamonds, the centre one alone being valued at twenty thousand English pounds. This atone was ip the early part of the sixteenth century the property of Othman, the negro Sultan of the Ahir, but falling into the hands of the powerful Songhay conquerors, it was conveyed to Constantine by the notorious Salah Bey, and for over a century it remained the chief treasure of the beautiful Mosque of Sidi-el-Kettani. But daring the riots this Mosque was sacked and the Koran, with its valuable incrustation, disappeared. After diligent inquiries extending over three years, the French Government had received positive information that the treasured manuscript was hidden in the Mosque of the Seven Marabouts at Banana, and had no doubt been deposited there for safety by some of the wild, lawless Tuaregs, who were known to have been in Constantine during the revolt. Banana was indeed the most likely place where it would be hidden, for beyond the walls of this city no European dared venture on account of the fierce hostility of its fanatical inhabitants. It is called by the Tuaregs the Accursed City, because it is supposed to have been built by Allah in a single night, but on account of the impiety of its inhabitants a curse was immediately after- wards placed upon it, ordaining that it should, at a moment when least expected, disappear into the bowels of the earth as suddenly as it had arisen. My orders were to travel to Kanana, obtain possession of the sacred relic, and return with it intact to Algiers. To accomplish this I had to undertake a journey of a thousand miles each way SA the crow flies, across the wildest and most inhospitable tract of country in the woad. Day by day, week by week, we had journeyed onward ever south, resting daring the breathless noon and plodding forward when at sundown we had prayed our maghrib in the direction of Mecca. We had crossed the Erg and the great plain of El Udj, mounted the stony plateau of Tinghert, and entered the dried-up course of the Igharghar —that mighty river, once as wide as the Nile and almost as long, but which, according to geologists, has for over a thousand years ceased to exist— when one night, having encamped, on account afa coming sandstorm, at a tiny oasis called El-"Hajaj, where there was a small Well surrounded by a dozen palm-trees, we were attacked, robbed and captured by a party of about a hundred fierce, black-veiled Tuaregs, the most dreaded pirates of the desert. In the fight Omar had ben killed, together with a dozen of his followers, and we. the survivors, knew our ultimate fate—slavery. Half-starved, cuffed, and ill-treated, we were burned over the boundless desert during nearly a whole moon, but at length an opportunity for escape presented itself, and I was not long in embracing it, entering Agades a few days later in a starving and half-fainting condition. There my adventures were strange, but not so curious as those that were to follow. I found in a decrepit old story- teller sitting near the city gate a generous friend, who, discovering that I was a stranger from the north who had fallen upon evil times, gave me food and lodging, and subsequently put me in the right road for Kanana, the Accursed City. From my host °I had been enabled to pick up a number, of wonderful tales of the genii, therefore on leaving Agadee I assumed the role of story-teller myself. On foot, begging my way from village to .village across the Tintumma, relating tales weird and wonderful as I went, I at last, one evening at sundown, just before the great iron gate? closed for the night, passed inside the high white walls of the mystic city of Kanana. As I entered the populous place and lost myself in its maze of thoroughfares of high square houses, built closely together to obtain the shadow, I was amazed at its extent and magnificence. The minarets of the mosques seemed numberless ; there must have beem fully a hundred; and the houses for the most part were magnificent in their propor- tions. That night,. after inspecting the chief sights of this unknown centre of Moslem influence, I made my evening meal off an onion I picked up in the market-place, wrapped my ragged burnouse around me. and slept under an archway in one of the ladder-like streets. Next day, finding that I passed among the inhabitants unnoticed, I COTS.. menced my avocation of story-telling, using always the Tarkiyeh dialect. Seated cross-legged on a well-worn mat near the entrance to my goal, the groat Mosque of the Seven Maraboute—a magnifi- cent dead-white pile embellished by some beautiful old blue tiles bearing, in long, lean characters, good counsels from the Koran—l repeated a dozen times each day the amazing adventures of one Ali of Kordofan, who had risen from a cow-herd to be Sultan, and usually I had a little circle of open-mouthed listeners, who smoked their long pipes in silence, stroked their beards doubtfully, or grunted approbation as they tossed me a copper coin. , After a short time I found my audience increas- ing in number, for I became popularly known as "El-Adib," or "the moralist," a nickname be- stowed, I presume, because many of my stories pointed a moral. One evening, half an hour before the mueddin mounted to the minarets above to call the Faithful for the sunset prayers, an old woman, accompanied by a young girl closely veiled, passed the spot where I was holding my listeners spell-bound. Tile dainty little dame, who from her silk stockings and heavy golden anklets I judged must be of wealthy family, halted for a few moments, listening as I described a passionate love passage between my hero and my heroine, and as she did so readjusted the adjar that discreetlar hid the lower portion of her face. Her sequin-covered brow was as fair as that of any Parisienne who trod the Boulevards, but in her dark, luminous eyes was an unutterable expres- sion of sadness, a look which, try how I would. I</p>
      </div>
