        <head>SAFEST MEDICINE</head>
        <p>THE SAFEST MEDICINE</p>
        <p>TOR' BIIIOLB AND LIVER COMPLAINTS, INDIGESTION, WIND! SPASMS, FOUL BREATH, NERVOUS DEPRESSION, LOSS Ok APPETITE, IRRITABILITY, LASSITUDE, DYSPEPSIA, HEART- BURN, LOWNESS OF SPIRITS. GIDDLNESB. &amp;C. AS A Li-ENERAL FAMILY APERIENT MEDICINE DR. SCOTT'S PILLS ARE UNEQUALLED, being composed of the Finest Drugs that ONG be obtained, are as mild and tonic in their action as they are certain in their curative effects. They give an healthy tone and vigour to the different secretions, causing the necessary organs of the Stomach and Liver to resume their activity, thus restoring the appetite, promoting digestion, and strengthening the whole system. DR. SCOTT'S PILLS Are prepared only by W. LAMBERT, 173, SEYMOUR PLACE, LONDON W. Do riot be persuaded by anyone to buy any other Medicine Instead,but insist upon having:the rightthing, which lawrapped in a square green package. By Post for 14 or 34 stamps. _</p>
        <p>WITH the advent 4:4 The Congress on the third annual Cor" Tuberculosis. gress for the Abolition of Tuberculosis, now sitting in London, public atterr tion is specially directed to the ravages of this arch enemy of mankind. It is, perhaps, an 120. pleasant experience to be told of the mail dangers from infection we daily escape, danger° lurking in unseen but myriad germs in the air we breathe, the food we eat, and the drink with which we nourish our children or refresh ourselves dui' ing the passage of a heat wave. But it is a some operation. operation. The public require t° made aware of the sources from which danger arises, and to interest theraseic; in the means of obviating or overconlil4", those risks to which they are exposed. 1t.1.54 necessary, in the first place, that those who sm.., to qualify as experts should be properly instructe' in the science of the subject, and that those wk° are capable of investigation, and have devoted tira° and energy to it, should give to the world the re: suit of their researches. Two important facts whic' scientific research has already given to the w°rlci lend a very hopeful aspect to the work of whit,e" these great international Congresses are a part° It has been discovered that tuberculosis, like many other diseases which are now the subject of modern investigation, owe* its origin to bacillus or germ, and that this germ, while " has the power of infinite reproduction, does Ile,: come into being by spontaneous generation. 11 in every case propagated by, or derived from, 1' similar organism. If our scientists are able tri recognise and locate the germ, they will presentlle be able to deal with it ; they are already learniaci to do so, and we have it on the authority of i•J°r„, LISTER that the medical profession can sometitus° sweep away the bacillus, and restore the organise' affected to its pristine health. If the disease call' not start afresh without propagation,there is h°l that, by proper precautions, the spread the disease may be prevented and its ravage; gradually brought under control, perhaP,o finally extinguished. The progress made 1 recent years in alleviating the scourge wile/ attention has been directed to it fully bears this contention. But while the scientific aspect !t a great part of the question it is not all; nor is the greater part, unless it can be communicated a practical form to the knowledge and experieoe: of the people. It is useful, in the first place, %, have the popular knowledge of the subject wide o disseminated, because by the formation of 03 healthy public opinion the hands of those entrust st with the inspection of food in the public intereto will be greatly strengthened. If the public soy made fully aware of the nature of the danger tl3. lurks in a diseased carcase, they will not Per- 0 sanitary inspectors to be regarded as troublesoge meddlers who are to be circumvented in of performance of their duties. If the provisions the law in this respect are supported by a • 0 St informed public opinion which will not toler,t, their evasion, then unscrupulous dealers in doll° , as.P ful meat will learn to bury their suspect care pt, instead of running the gauntlet in the meat nlar,r, Again, if it is-known that an affected udder Jog milch cow serves out millions of disease ge of with every quart of milk, a strict supervisi°43oo byres and dairies will be insisted on. 'Log are illustrations of how the public health v 0 110 be protected by public authorities which oto behind them a healthy public opinion. 00 the matter does not end even there- 10 lur question may well be asked. If danger 00 so lavishly on every side, how does arlY . escape? The answer is that the germ o ovef a congenial seed-end in the unhealthy or ian) 00 hare ished muscles aud other organisms of the 0 0' frame. A healthy, sound, vigorous orgimisaiost the best protection the,individual can have ago 101 the myriads of unseen enemies which throat° igite on every hand. Attention to health and trPhlstliall 0 is therefore the one grand remedy. To bath shall be given, and the remedy is an °'""'</p>
