        <p>to many. How to make it apply to all—especi- allY to those who at present have not the means Within their reach—is the grand social problem that still lies before civilised humanity, both in its individuat and collective capacity.</p>
        <p>The Bill for the Continu- The boles Bill. ation of the Agricultural</p>
        <p>and Tithes Rates Act is</p>
        <p>a transparent job, which is aggravated by the extraordinary circumstances in which it was intro- duced into the House of Commons as a perman- ent measure. The guise under which the agricul- tural dole was first brought forward was that of a Measure of relief to distressed agriculture. The hollowness of the claim was exposed by the fact,of</p>
        <p>_ which Sir H CAMPBELL-BANNERMAN reminded the</p>
        <p>liouso last week, that the bill was not restricted to distressed districts, but applied equally to those Parts of the country where agriculture was in a flourishing state. The industry is now more', prosperous than it was five years ago, and the fact seems to be recognised by the Government, for they have decided to make their measure por- n:tat:tent This admits the whole case put with such force in the extract ftom a speech by Mr CHAMBERLAIN, read with so much caustic and dramatic effect by the Liberal leader in Parliament. It is a case- .9. most audacious one "—of robbing Peter to pay Paul, Peter being the landless millions with no other wealth than their toil, while Paul is a wealthy landlord with his 20,000 acres it may be. It was put with equal force by Mr. Cnamax in 1896, when he showed that the burden of rates falls ultimately upon the landlord ; it is calculated with rent and other Charges which the farmer has to reckon with when he takes his farm, and it therefore has its influ- ence in regulating the market value of the land. Therefore the landlord will recover in an increase of rent all the relief that is now given to the looser. Even where rents may fall, the relief will , have what in the City would be called a stiffening eflect,preventing reductions to the extent of about 5 per cent. The farmer's interest thus disappear- jag,tho Government acknowledge the benefit given to the landed interest,and they attempt to justify i Mr. Loci tried to argue that because per- sonalty which could not be defined had been re- lieved in 1840, therefore real estate had now a Claim upon the Government for relief. But if there is one class of property more than another</p>
        <p>that has benefited _ by the new taxes levied</p>
        <p>Since 1840 it is the land. Not only so, but an examination of the figures 'thews that it has not Paid its ,f air proportion!of:these taxes. Taking the ease as between town and country,it will be found that local rates which in 1840 averaged 2s. 7d,in the 2 in both town and country had risen in 189 to 33. 10d. in towns, while they had fallen in the "Rutty to 2s. 3d. The case between land and houses stands in a similar position. It must be borne in mina that while the relief given to land -4Pplies also to agricultural buildings, all other buildings are excluded from participation. Thus factories, workshops, railways and mines, as well as Bbops and houses, are all classed in what Mr LONG chooses to call personalty,as opposed to real estate. While, therefore, relief is given t, those 'whose burden is already the lighter ons, it is left to press rth all its weight upon the means of industry. t is a tax upon the wealth-producing power of country, the very power whieh creates the Nhie of that so-called real property which is given so unfair an advantage. If anything will awaken the country to the unlisguised blessings it receives under Tory rule, it should be such a flagrant piece of jobbery perpetrated audaciously, in the name of justice.</p>
        <p>The Death of There was a consensus " Mrs. Xruger. of regret in the news papers beregard to the death of Mrs. KRUGER.. The event adds one more to many pathetic touches in the great picture of humairsuffering which is being painted in South Africa. The question now mostly discussed in Connection with it is how it will affect hostilities. , The wife'of President KRUGER came of the old I'' ritignenOt stoek which is accountable for much of the best that is to be traced in the Boer character. As a child she took part in the Great Trek of '1836 Which led to the founding of the Transvaal 'Republic. "The traditions of her descent and early associations were, therefore, strong influences in ' favour of the nationalist cause which her husband 'represented. Her devotion to him led her to accept unquestioned whatever decision he might have 'arrived at, and when the PRESIDENT determined 'tile= deserting his post, and leaving his wife to the lender mercies ,of his enemies, she accepted the 'situation with 'quiet resignation, though it must have cost her many pangs. There is 'little profit in dwelling upon what might have been had Om :PAUL been possessed of the courage and magnanimity to act as the acknow- , lodged head of the State, and remain in Pretoria to.arrange terms of peace. He; chose to adopt a more inglorious course, which, however, had Special attractions for him, and against which the devotion of his wife, or her helpless condition, weighed but lightly in the balance. It mow tran- Aeires that Tante Senna had been suffering from a dropsical complaint, which made it impossible for ller to accompany her husband in his flight, and ris desertion of her in that condition appears sin- '. gularly cruel and pitiless. it cannot be said that Ile sacrificed his own finer feelings for high motives ` 'State; for at the time he fled from Pretoria he 'resigned his official respansibility,while he held on to the official bars of gold which were the chief e emblem of State in the conquered Republic. He no doubt, high expectations of the treat- e °lent:which the British would mete out to his ' wlfe, and in this he;was not disappoirited. It can- riot be said he was grateful, because his protests e aeleiest the alleged ill-usage of Boer women aud ehildreeeeere so feud and lamentable that he was Obliged to qualify them, These 4 indeed, were so utterly at variance with the facts that they l'. alarmed the friends of Mrs KauGEL,who knew the 14i0d0ess and consideration that had been shewn , to her from the first by British generals and offi- 'elals,and feared the result of such reckless misre- . Iresentation. Mrs KEOGER was not in any sense - F.olitician, or a woman of affairs; she could not, /Ste Mrs BoTHA, conduct negotiations, or seek to trsoress leaders with pacYJc views; but it is well iCricwn, nevertheless, that the handsome treat- 'fleet she herself received removed much Gf her ' ari3, ezejudices, and made her anxious to secure 1354 es if that had been in her power. Unfortu- nately her influence with her husband was 1 xatrruenaeilny:mau ; and while he was ready to grant dg acknowledgment of British liberality and kindness in her case, there it ended. The 1055 of his wife, which must have come to him with a shock more or less great, according to the</p>
        <p>measure in which he may be still wrapped up in his own present surroundings, must change his view of many things which he might have done differently. Whether it will tend to a cessation of hostilities, as many people seem to think, will depend much more upon the resources of the generals in the field, which are becoming more slender every day. The revelations made by the discovery of State letters at Reitz indicate that the Boers are at the end of their tether. They cannot continue the struggle much longer in any case. It is not impossible that the personal affliction of the President, who is now in his eightieth year, may afford a suitable excuse for putting an end to a struggle of which everybody is heartily sick—the hopeless, impoverished rem nant of fighting burghers most of all.</p>
        <p>Year by year the list of casualties on British railways continues to grow. Wars come and go, but the loss of life in this exceedingly warm corner of the industrial battle perpetually waged in our midst is a constant drain on the people. The figures, however, which have just been published, of the casualties of 1900 are somewhat more satisfactory than usual, under the most important beading of accidents to servants of companies or of contractors. There were, roughly speaking, fifty more deaths of persons employed on the rail- ways than in the preceding year, but the number of injured of the same class is fewer by about as many. Casualties, therefore, to railway servants may be said to have remained stationary in number; and as during the twelve months not only have additional miles of railway been opened, but more trains run, and ptesumably more servants exposed to the danger of accident, the fact is so far gratifying. The total is, however, sufficient to impress the most casual reader, anu all the more so when to the list of per- sons killed or injuied when dealing with public traffic are added those who sustained injuries in such occupations as loading and unloading trucks, the working of cranes and capstans, or by the fall- ing of waggon-door, lamps, bales of goods, Ace., or in getting on and off vehicles at rest, i.e., through the ordinary risks of commerce apart from those peculiar to railway traffic. Adding all together, including injuries to passengers and "other per- sons," we have a grand total of very nearly 21,000 persons killed or injured in one year on railways in the United Kingdom." With such figures on record, can any precautions which may be enforced on the railway companies by the Board of Trado or by Parliament be considered too stringent?</p>
        <p>A great Parliamentary figure, though he never addressed the Speaker, has been removed by the sudden death of Mr Samuel Pope. This famous advocate was leader of what is known as the Par- liamentary Bar,because the counsel corning within that category limit their pleadings to committees of the two Houses of Parliament. They support or oppose the private bills which are dealt with by these tribunals of Peers and Commoners. They do not often attract much public notice ; but their work is exceedingly responsible, as it treats of public schemes involving huge sums of money, and to argue properly an intimate knowledge of several branches of science. Nearly all the witnesses in such CaFOS are "experts," engineers, surveyors, analytical chemists, and members of other learned professions. These men have to be embarrassed or controverted in their own special sphere of infor- mation, and the effect has to be wrought without the use of dramatic methods, which, useful sometimes with juries, would fail before a Parliamentary Committee. The barristers who succeed in this calling make large incomes. It is said that one at least draws £30,000 a year , and as the money has to be made in eight months (including the time for the preliminary study of briefs) it will be gathered that they must be fairly active when the session is on. It is no uncommon thing for a leader to keep passing through two or three committee rooms during a day, catching up the threads, and displaying apparently a closer grasp of the facts than do those who have been following a particular case from the beginning. Mr Pope had physical difficulties in this regard. He was so fat that he had to be wheeled about, and he was frequently allowed by courtesy to address the committees sitting. But years of bodily infirmity did not dull him, and he has died in harness. The great drawback to a career of this kind is that it ' shuts a barrister out from the floor of the House of Commons. Several, when they have made a ' reasonable fortune, have given it up for that reason—in order that they might satisfy a higher I ambition. Mr Cripps is one of these. But, after all, there are few occupations of such importance.</p>
        <p>"Speech Day" is an institution in connection with our local Grammar School which very few would care to see abandoned. It gives the parents an opportunity of coming into closer touch with the school and its masters and enables them to hear at first hand, the results of the past year's work. A grievous circumstance has this year in- terfered with the holding of the "Speech Day" celebrations. At the eleventh hour, the sad news arrived that the Head Master had been bereft of his eldest daughter in a totally unexpected manner. The griefiof the Headmaster was shared by "his boys" at the school, and they felt that they could not enter upon their annual Speech Day proceed- ings with that spirit of pleasure and whole-hearted- ness which usually actuates them when they were aware that the Master was an absentee under such sad circumstances. Accordingly the governors decided to postpone the proceedings. We feel sure that Mr. VVillcocks and his family will have deep and widespread sympathy in their trouble.</p>
        <p>Sankey Brook is a common drain according to the evidence of the Warrington Eural Council officials who have been holding a sort of inquest relative to the cause of the stench emanating from the stream. They found that the brook began well but ended badly. It has its source from Rainford Brook, an almost clear stream, but after passing through the chemical waste beds of St Helens it immediately loses its virgin purity and takes unto itself a stink far worse than the many "well-defined and different stinks" of Cologne. Crude sewage is dumped in all along the line, apparently by every authority that has an opportunity of so disposing of its waste and including those authorities who complain Of the result. The remedy is not so apparent as the evil, and the possibility of getting the Rivers Pollution Act into operation seems rather remote unless Sankey Brook becomes legally known as a river instead of a common drain. Actually it is both, but for the purposes of legal remedy it must only be one or the other. The "law is a hese we know, but even this resection will not soothe the aggrieved and half-poisoned residents in the neighbourhood of the brook. Until the various authorities each and all cease from polluting the stream it will never improve. Let the Warrington authorities lead the way. They will then have a clear conscience and a good c mse for bringing pressure to bear on the reclining offenders. The facts place them in sad disgrace at present.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Corporation picnic was held last week, the destination being as in previous years, the training ship, "Clio:" Ten members of the School _Attendance Committee were present on this important occasion, and their expenses will, of course, be paid out of the Boraugh funds seeing that they were there in the form of a deputation. The other Councillors and friends who joined the party presumably paid their own expenses. We are assured by one of the deputation that they "had a very nice day," arid there is no reason to doubt the statement. The picnic was quite up to the high standard of previous years—in fact it may have been more enjoyable, for the time at the disposal of the deputation on reaching the " Clio" was so short that only half an inspection could be made. It seems rather rough on those who pay the bill when ten of a deputation only half accomplish their work. Still, this did not mar the enjoyment of the pic- nic, and •no doubt sufficient information was derived from the visit to enable the Committee to present a really businesslike report to the next mooting of the Council.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>EXAMINER FRIDAY, JULY 26 1901.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER FRIDAY, JULY 26 1901.</p>
        <p>Warrington is the poorer by the death of Mr. J. H. Gornall, its Medical Officer of Health. For a long period of years Mr. Gornall has served the town with a conscientiousness and thoroughness of purpose which have done him infinite credit,and as the result of which the condition of the town is vastly improved. It lies in the power of a Medi- cal Officer to do many things which tend to amelior- ate the lot of the masses in particular. Some of these things are easily conceived but not as easily carried out. The restoration or abolition of insani- tary property, for instance, is one of the matters which a medical officer is supposed to keep a sharp eye upon. It is not every Medical Officer of Health who does so, however, or if he does he contents himself with observation and fights shy of active measures. Dr Gornall, as he was generally styled, was a man of a different type. He both observed and acted, and it may truly be said of him that he has left the town in a much better state than he found it. Personally he was a kindly man, but possessed of strong convictions which he never hesitated to openly avow and up- hold—in fact hesitancy or doubt are the last things one would associate with the character of the late Mr Gornall. In politics he was a strong Liberal, and here again his fearlessness of convic- tion was apparent, for he never disguised his political leanings, though occupying an official position uAder the Corporation. He was a man everybody respected, and his death, which occured last Thursday after a long illness, has pro- duced a deep feeling of sorrow throughout the town in which he has played so honest and capa- ble a part during the best years of aby no means lengthy lifetime. We are sure his bereaved family have widespread sympathy in their great trouble.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>MATTERS OF MOMENT. A Provincial Letter.</head>
        <p>MATTERS OF MOMENT. A Provincial Letter.</p>
        <p>Provincial England is especially interested in the important British Congress on tuberculosis which has been held in London during the week. The King, who takes especial interest in the subject, was represented at the opening by the Duke of Cambridge, and there were present many distin- guished scientists from all parts of the world; men who have a particular knowledge of the destruction of life wrought by the tiny bacillus tuberculosis, four hundred millions of which can find standing room on a postage stamp. These men have been able to dilate upon the operations of this minute vegetable fungus from a pathological, veterinary, and public point of view, and our poor humanity has certainly need of all available information on such a subject. For in proportion to its importance our ignorance is lamentably ineagra,though we may be said to be improving and adding a little to our previous stock of knowledge. As a matter of fact England stands to-day, in regard to consumption, in much the same attitude that she did three hun- dred years ago as to the plague, when our estimable ancestors fired pistols in the air to ward off the evil. There are something like seventy thousand deaths from consumption in Great Britain every year, and a million in Europe. It is not twenty years since Dr Koch, whose voice has this week been heard at the Congress, isolated the bacillus of the disease ; and not forty years since Villemin de- monstrated that it came about by contagion, and not so much by reason of any hereditary taint. During the interval investigation and experiment have been busy in many directions, but there is much yet to be learned.</p>
        <p>It was a judge of Assize at Liverpool who first directed attention to the marked effect of a long spell of warm weather upon the length of the calendar, as relating particularly to crimes of vio- lence. There is likewise a theory to the effect that a long period of sunshine intensifies the emotions in other ways. Hence the heat-wave is held accountable for its effects upon morbid-minded persons. who appear to have committed suicide wholesale as it were: while at Shepherd's Bash it has made itself felt in the wilful destruction of a whole family by the male parent. The epidemic of: suicide and of violence is not confined to a particu- lar district, but is spread over many parts of the country. As rain has fallen and it is cooler in some of these places,there may possibly be a correspond- ing decrease of crime.</p>
        <p>When the Duchess of Kingston was tried for bigamy, in 1766, in Westminster Hall, Mrs Pitt, who was present, wrote a letter saying "I am persuaded that the Duchess i not in the least degree hum- bled by her position, but mightily pleased with herseltv for having created such a sensation, an for having secured so brilliant a house." In like manner, it may easily be conceived that in his en- forced temporary seclusion in Pentonville prison Earl Russell will at least enjoy the consolation of having first made a "brilliant house" for inquisi- tive ladies of high degree, and others, at Westmin- ster, in his bigamy affair. But if some of our legis- lators have their way the farce will not be re- peated. The."surprise" of the occasion came when the Earl pleaded guilty and declared that he had ignorantly violated the law. The spectacle then was not even impressive, and the announcement that he was seirtenced to three months imprison- ment in Holloway gaol, must have been the most prosaic climax imaginable to a ceremonial so ela- borately prepared. Few persons, after this, can be expected to hold that "trial by peers' shall remain a recognised part of the British constitution. The sooner this survival from a mediasval age is swept away the better. The performance may possess a kind of academic interest, but it cannot add to thG dignity of the constituticrn.</p>
        <p>It is a far cry to Kent, and so our lioerties and lungs are not affected by a certain rather elaborate Kentish bye-law, which aims a cruel blow at the hilarious Cockney who on returning from an outing , has been went to beguile the homeward way with music and song. Others, far removed from the quiet lanes of Kent,have no doubt heard the strains of similar revellers, and perhaps wished there was some such local bye-law then and there in force. Usually the songs rendered are distinctly urban,and the listener needs all the sarcasm of Coriolanus to quote his words, " I thank you for your voices, your most sweet voices." The bye•law is at hand, but to quote the whole of it might betan infliction. So the reader is asked to draw upon his powers of inference from the last clause all that precedes it. This is, moreover, its saving clause and reads " Nothing in this bye-law shall prevent the use of a horn in a reasonable manner by one person on a coach or similar vehicle?' This saving clause is a boon to the bar (penalty £2 for each offence), and ,is obviously inserted in order that ingenious coun- sel may be enabled to argue that each of their par- ticular clients was the one particular person who was using a horn in a reasonable manner. It is !possible also that evidence will conflict as to whether the singing or playing is loud, but con- stables who can gauge the speed of a bicycle at a glance, will soon be experts in the merits of a piano passage,</p>
        <p>Although Miss Eleanor Ormerod was born in Gloucestershire, her death deprives those engaged in the most ancient of industries in every county of England, of one of their best friends. In her the lady entomologist was an entirely new type,the out- come of Miss Ormerod's combined originality, genius, and earnestness of purpose. By this, it is not meant to be conveyed that no lady had dsvoted herB3ll to the study of insect life before Miss Ormeron time. Yet no lady investigator of these complex, delicate, and obscure problems had pur- sued the studies with a view to attain to the rank of a specialist, and to convert the information thus gained to the most practical of all purposes,namely, the saving of the fleld,the orchard, and the garden from the ravages of winged and creeping things, and the protection of live stock from the attacks of insect-enemies. Yet this is what the late Miss Ormerod did. She struck out a new path for her- self, and the amount of money she must have saved to the agriculturist, the stock-raiser, the gerdener and the orchard keeper can only be vaguely con- jectured, but must be very great.</p>
        <p>The Glasgow exhibition continues to attract numerous visitors from all parts of the English provinces, and not a few from the Continent and America. Its success may be largely attributed to the boldness of the general plan, and to the deter- mination of the promoters to provide adequate and ornate buildings, and to organise the exhibit on a scale of magnitude and completeness never before attempted in Scotland. The exhibition is also a place of entertainment, with the result that all classes of visitors are provided for. There is within the exhibition grounds much to inttrast the scien- tific and mechanioal expert and the machinery in motion is a never failing attration. Art is also splendidly reprsented, and archon )logy, as illus-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN POSTAL ARRANGEMENTS.</p>
        <p>On and from the 25th met, the afternoon delivery by postmen at 4.35 p.m. will begin at 4 20 p.m. The Head Office letter box will close at 4.15 p.m.for the local area.</p>
        <p>MERSEY MISSION TO SEAMEN, RUNCORN. The usual open-air concert in connection with the Seamen's Institute, was held on the field off the Hulloway on Saturday evening. An interesting programme of music was rendered by St Edward's Brass Band, and a variety of recreations were in- dulged in by the visitors. RESCUED FROM DROWNING. William Harvey, aged 8 years, son of Captain Harvey, of South Bank Terrace, Runcorn, had a narrow escape from drowning on Monday afternoon The boy was fishing for crabs in the Ship Canal near Runcorn Bridge, when he fell into the water, which is very deep at this point. He was being cairied away by the tide when his brother John, aged 13 years, who was preparing for a bathe, ERW his perilous position. Without hesitation he plunged into the water, swam to his brother and after a plucky struggle, brought him to the shore. The little fellow was at once conveyed home and is none the worse for his adventure.</p>
        <p>WESTON WESLEYAN DAY SCHOOL. The Government report has just been received, and is of a very gratifying character. The average attendance, which is higher than for some time, is 61 in the mixed school, and 46 in the infants. The Iprincipal grant for the year:for the mixed school is the highest obtainede, viz. : 225, while for the whole school it is £lO7 18s; fee grant, £53 10s ; total grant, £l6l Bs. The summery is ;—"The school is in good order, and maintains its position with very fair success. The first class does well." This speaks well for the master (Mr Bowyer) and his helpers. RUNCORN PARISH CHURCH, SUNDAY SCHOOLS. On Saturday afternoon the teachers at the above schools had a most enjoyable outing. The party, numbering about 25. under the direction of Messrs J Littler, W E Dudley, T Clare, and the Rev H B Firth, boarded one of Mr Yarwood's conveyances in Victoria-road, and after a delightful drive, arrived at Chester shortly after three o'clock. The pictur- esque scenery of the Dee was immediately sought, and a barge was chartered, in which the visitors rowed to Itccleston Ferry. Tea came as an accept- abk variety, and after the refreshment derived from this function, the barge was again requisit- ioned, and a short but pleasant sail landed them again at the cathedral city. Runcorn was reached about midnight. ST. JOHN'S, WESTON. The annual choir sermons in connection with the above church were preached on Sunday. The preacher at matins was the Rev J Shilletto, vicar of Dunham Hill. In the afternoon a children's ser- vice was held, at which the Viar of Dunham gave a special address, and appropriate hymns were ren- dered. In the evening the choir sang selections from "Judas Maccabteus," with commendable pre- cision, supported by the Runcorn Orchestral String Band. The pulpit was occupied by the Rev W H Stables. The church was cooled by ice, and was crowded at all the services. The collections were slightly below those of the;preceding year.</p>
        <p>FREEMASONRY AT RUNCORN. The annual installation of the Worshipful Master of the Ellesmere Lodge (No. 758 Runcorn) of Ancient Free and Accepted Masons, was held on Thursday evening at the Masonic Hall, Runcorn. Tiers were a large number of visitors present, lincluding I—Bros T Owen, PM, 2,609,P P G, A DC; J C Robinson, PP G, DC (W.est Lancashire) ; J H Lee, WM, 1.384; WH .1 Jenkins, P M 125. Prov. G R (West Lancashire). The installation ceremony was conducted by Bros T 11 Annett, PM; C W Phillips, PM;HU Linaker, ; and W Mellor, P.M. The newly installed Master, Bro E C Hicks (inspector of police) afterwards invested the following officers :—Bros T Forster, SW;Hvy Handley, J W; J Littler, 8 D; T Hill, J G Bellamy, 3 ; W Wass, PM. treasurer, PPG D. D at C ; T H Annett, P P G T, PM. secretary ; C w Phillips. PG A, Dofc, p Isf, D of C; J Cooper, P DI, organist; J Hill. tylert The customary ban- quet was afterwards held and the usual masonic toasts were honoured.</p>
        <p>FUNERAL OE TEE LATE DR. MCKENZIE. The funeral of Dr. McKenzie took place on Satur- day at Runcorn Cemetery. The cortege left thel deceased's late residence in Belvedere, and pro- ceeded to the cemetery via Regent-street and Greenway-road, the coffin being borne by the fol- lowing ;--tiessrs James Green, James Hibbert, William Beesley, Robert Jones, Richard Richardson Walter liazlehurst, Charles Bridges and Daniel Bridges. ihe mourners were :—Dr. Robinson, Cap- tain McKenzie (brother), Dr, McLennan, Ormsby, Delaney, McDougall, and Messrs J H Weston, J K Digges, J Edwards (Hamer Lodge of Freemasons, 1393) ; the following representatives of the Shep- herds' Tent Lodge:—Messrs iy Rhodes (late secre- tary), S Hillidge (secretary: I) Hulse (tressurer), Captain Heasley. Messrs C iichards,George doodler William Pullen, John Simon, George Walters. and R Houlbrook. The funeral service was conducted by the Rev C H Killick, vicar, Holy Trinity. The coffin was of polished oak, with brass mountings, and on a brass plate was inscribed deceased's name, date of death, and burial. Wreaths were contri- buted by "His loving wife," Miss M Clark. Mr. and Mrs Houlbrook and family, of the Infectious Hospital, Weston; Mrs Carlow and Muriel, and Mr and Mrs Digges. The undertaker was Ntr W Ba nkes.</p>
        <p>CRAWFORD S</p>
      </div>
