      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>IN ARREARS</head>
        <p>IN ARREARS</p>
        <p>At Runcorn PoliCe-COn-ii-,-cin-iVednesday, William Penny, was brought up on remand charged with failing to pay to his wife arrears of an order made under the Married Women's Maintenance Act amounting to £2O 10s. The prisoner paid his wife 10s., and promised to pay 103. per week and the case was withdrawn. RuNconisr IIomING SOCIETY,</p>
        <p>I On Saturday this society flew their second young bird race from Worcester, a distance of 82 miles. Eleven members sent 101 birds,which were liberated, by Mr Ashton, Liverpool, and district federation convoyer, at 12 noon. Weather tine wind, south. The following velocities per minute were attained :—Dunbavin,lsl7 yards; Dyer, 1404; Dyer, 1378; Howard, 1283; Edwards, 1261; Howard, 1261 ; Shaw and Son, 1247; Shaw and Son, 1210; Marshall, 1196; shaw and Son, 1176; Killcross, 1161 ; Wllitby, 1150 ; Dean, 1111.</p>
        <p>• SCHOOL TREATS. The popular functions this week have been school treats. On Monday the Mersey Mission Scholars went to Creed Wood on the Weaver by boat and the Salvation Army went by train to Frodsham. This latter favourite resort was also visited on Tuesday by Halton-road Wesleyans and St Luke's Congregational. On Wednesday Brunswick Wes- leyans journed to Frodsham, and Holy Trinity had a sail to Daresbury along the Bridgewater Canal. The junior members ofSt Michael's Church Choir spent tke day in Southport. Unfortunately the Wednesday excursionists had wet weather to con- tend with and their pleasure was consequently much lessened. A WIDNES GROCER HEAVILY FINED.</p>
        <p>At the Widnes Petty Sessions, on Thursday morning, before Mr B Brown (in the chair) and Major It Lewis, Charles Frederick Yates, grocer, Widnes-road and Derby-road, Widnes, was sum- moned by Messrs Lyle and Sons, Limited, sugar refiners, London, for breach of tbe Merchandise Marks Act. 1887, by selling in their tins some golden syrup not of their manufacure, Ills sister, Mary Jane Yates, who managed the Derby-road shop, was also summoned for u similar offence. Both defend- ants pleaded guilty. The bench regarded the case as a very serious one, and the male defendant was fined .£5 and £5 costs on the first charge, and on the other two summonses he would have to pay the court costs, and the female defendant would be fined 2s 601 and costs on the first summons, and would have to pay the court costs on the second.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>...,......1.0 RUNCORN NOTES.</head>
        <p>...,......1.0 RUNCORN NOTES.</p>
        <p>[BY A CORRESPONDENT.] At last the long wished for change in the weather has come. Farmers in the Runcorn dis- trict were becoming anxious for their crops, which, to use a common phrase, were being "scorched to death." In consequence of the long draught bay is a very light crop.</p>
        <p>' Within the past decade Runcorn has had two severe periods of trade depres4on, and the town is now suffering somewhat severely. Every class of tradesman is grumbling about the difficulty of making ends meet, and there seems no present prospect of an improvement. A revival of ,the chemical industry is sadly needed. It is a pleas- ing fact that the cyanide works at the Old Quay which were recently destroyed by fire, are being rebuilt. The new buildings and plant, it is said, will be a great improvement on the old works.</p>
        <p>I see that the Liverpool School of Tropical Medicine, are about to send out another expedition with respect to malaria. It is considered probable that Dr Annett, of whose achievements all Runcorn is proud, will be in charge of the pr&amp;jected expedi- tion. He has made a special study ot bacteria and his original researches and contributions to the medical press thereon have gained him some dis- tinction amongst the profession.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>RA:LWAY RATES.</head>
        <p>RA:LWAY RATES.</p>
        <p>Application by Wafrington Brewers.</p>
        <p>The Railway and Canal Commission Court on Thurs- day heard the case of Greenell. Whitley, and Co. v. the London and North-Western Railway Company. Appli- cants are brewers at Warrington, and their premises are connected with the railway by a siding some little distance from Arpler Sta:.on. Over this eking the applicants carried hops, malt, and beer from Warring- ton and Burton and North Wales, and vice versa. Applicants complained that the railway company Charged other brewers for carrying similar goods rates which were relatively lower than the rates • charged them, thereby giving undue preference to the Burton and Lichfield brewers. The court was asked to decide what was a reasonable rebate to allow the applicants. They also asked to be all ,wed such rebates as from Ist February, 1899, after crediting the railway company with the deduction already made by the applicants. In the alternative the latter claimed £1,500 damages in respect to overcharges, and £lOO for undue preference. The railway company denied any undue preference. and while admitting allowing 40 per ton to the Burton brewers traversed the statement that the applicants performed the same terminal services as those per- formed by the Burton brewers. They denied that the applicants had suffered any damage, and counter- claimed for £1,156 which had been retained by Messrs Greenall. The council having argued the construction of an agreement, the matter was adjdurned until Tuesday next.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>._____4•_. WERE THEY BRITISH LIONS? --- A Potato Action. --- ;Damages for a Local Farmer.</head>
        <p>._____4•_. WERE THEY BRITISH LIONS? --- A Potato Action. --- ;Damages for a Local Farmer.</p>
        <p>This case was heard at the Manchester Assizes before Mr Justice Ridley. The plaintiff (Mr NM Warburton) is a farmer carrying on business at Woolston, and the defendant is a p )tato merchant at Smithfield Market, Manchester. The claim was for £77 124 damages for , breach of contract Mr Acton (instructed by Messrs Arthur Browne and Crompton was for the plaintiff and IMr Langdon (instructed by Messre Cobbett, Whee'er land Cibbett) for the defendant. It was stated that last , year the plaintiff purchased from the defendant a quantity cf seed potatoes which were representod as of the British Lion " variety. When they grew the potatoes proved Co be another variety altogether, and the crop, it was said, was principally one of small potatoes only fit for pigs. Mr Gender inspected the crop, and admitted that the potatoes were not" British Lions." He offered £4O compensation, but this plaintiff refused. Mr Acton called evidence to show that excel- lent crops of "British Lion" potatoes were grown last year. Mr Laudon, contended that his client did sup- ply the ...British Lion" variety, and called a number of farmers to describe theirilast year's experiences with it. The burden of their evidence was to the effect that in 1898 and 1899 the "British Lion" prnducesl g,od erens, but in 1900 it was a great failure. Tnis they artributed to the facet that the variety had i'gone back," which was the way of the hrbrid potato. One of the witnesses was Mr J Barclay, a potato merchant of Smithfield. Be said that he examined a potato of the"Britieh Lion" variety early last year and advised a customer not to try it.</p>
        <p>;Thi Judge : Flow would you describe the " British Mon "?—Toe witness : It has what you would &amp;scribe as the Chinese eve. (Laughter.) The Judge : Will you describe the Chinese eye ?—The withers : It is very long and very narrow. (Renewed laughter.)</p>
        <p>Mr B aclay said the " Britiab Lion" potato had been on the market for three or four years. fie was not at all surprised that it had ''gone back.' There was no fixed life in a hybrid potato. The "Yorkshire Regent" main. tamed its character for sixty years : other had done th 4same for twenty years and sixteen years. The famous " Schoolmaster" lasted only three or four re4rs, and &amp;for selling at £5l per tan, disappeared altogether in one season.</p>
        <p>The jury he:d that plaintiff was not supplied with pltatoes of th:l variety stipulated for and returned a ver- dict hi his favor for the amount claimed.</p>
        <p>Local Military Appointment. Major and Honorary Lieutenant-Colonel W H Hand has been promoted Lieutenant-Colonel to command the old 2nd Royal Linca.hire Militia, Warrington, known under the territorial system as the 6Pb. Battalion the King's Liverpool Regirn,,nt. Colonel Hand got his company in 1882 and field tank in 1889 He has passed the military school of instruction and has served with the embodied battalion at Fermoy, Ireland. 'Mr WT E Wallace, who has been serving in the Transvaal war 89 a Ilieutenant of Loch's Horse, has been appointed to a ' captaincy under Colonel Hand. Newchurch Church Alterations. At the Liverpool Consistory Court on Tuesday, before Ali- Chancellor Esnin. the Rev E W W Kaye, rector of Newchgreb, Winwick,asked for authority to make structural alterations, costing £lBO, and including the removal of the gallery, the seating of a portion of the transept, a new choir vestry, and stained glass windows. —The faculty was granted. TUP GILEN-SPEGY DISTILLERY. Situate in the finest Whiskv.producing District of Scotland, is the property of W. &amp;A. allbey. This Whisky is made entirely rem bome-grosyn Battey and Is kept absolutely unblended in His Majesty's Bonded Warehouses to mature, and sold by W.&amp; per bottle A. Gilbey's agents in every town at 3s. 6d</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>PARR'S BANK, LIMITED,</head>
        <p>PARR'S BANK, LIMITED,</p>
        <p>Meeting at Warrington.</p>
        <p>The half-yearly meeting of this company was held at the Bank premises, Winwick-street, Warrington at noon on Thursday. In accordance with custom, one meeting of the comrany is held in London and the other in Warrington, which as the general manager remarked, is the cradle of the bank. Mr Cecil I? Parr presided and the following directors were also present: Mr A Crewdson (Manchester), Mr Thomas H Dix3n (Gresford Denbighshire), Mr John • Dann (Chislehurst), Sir John J Harwood (Manchester), Mr A Hewlett kWarwick), Mr R Neill (Prestwich), Mr Peter Stubs (Newnham Gloucestershire), and Mr A IA Turner (Liverpool). There were also present Messrs E D Hyatt (secretary), W Skinner (manager at the Vi'ar- rington Bank),R Wright,E C Cooper,H Nickson,E Waite, Guy Sanart, J M Croesdale, J Highfield, Gil S Mason, Charles Baker, James Duckworth, William Owen, J Speed, Henry Milani:, George Dakin, R Hargreaves, D Garnett, A G Gray, R Cram and :others.</p>
        <p>INTERESTING- STATEMENT BY -THE CHAIRMAN.</p>
        <p>The report showed that including £95,595 101 5d brought forward, the net balance of profits, after pro- viding for all bad and doubtful debts, and rebating the current bills at 5 per cent. is £256,550 7s 6d. From this a dividend at the rate of 19 per cent. per annum was recommended. leaving a balance to be carried to next account of £97,517 17s 61. Toe CHAIRMAN moved the adoption of the report and balance sheet. At the outset he said they would be sorry to hear that their old friend and director, Sir David Gamble would be unable to be present. He telegraphed to the effect that as president of the Lancashire Agri- cultural Society his duties lay at St Helens that day, otherwise he would hove been very pleased to bave been with then,. It had been the custom at these meetings for the chairman to make a few observations. Really there was very little in the banking world to tell them. During the last six monilaa rates certainly had been profitable from a banker's point of view and after making the usual full provision for all bad debts—and the amount of those debts was small in comparison with the large business which the Bank did—they had pleasure in presenting the shareholders with another Satisfactory balance sheet. The profit they showed of over £160,000 for the half year would have bsen appre- ciably greater if no further fall had occurred in the value of their investments since last December, but as most of them knew the prices of all investments had materially fallen again during the last six months. He hoped however,they had at last:touched the bottoin,and they believed time would show that improved prices would have restored to them in a great measure the values which, for the present at all events, bad disappeared. It was a matter of consolation to know that in Spite of these shrinkages they were still able to show a handsome margin of profit beyond the amount necessary for paying their dividend of 19 per cent. From what he had said absait the fall in the value of their investments it might seem parodoxical to some when lie stated that their investments were worth £96,000 more than was shown on the balance sheet. This was owing to their very prudent practice of always writing down their securities but never writing tbern up. Let him give them an instance. If India 3's stood at a higher figure in the minket than in the books of the banks and if London County Council stock stood at a lower figure teey would not set the gain on the one against the loss on tbe other, but they would provide for the whole of the loss on the one oueof cia rent pr .fits and they would leave the gain on toe other security where it was. And hence it came about that in the aggregate the bank's investments were fully £BB,OOO more in value than they were stated to be in the books' The sh reholders would observe in the report that they had opened a branch at Market Drayton. This town was the centre of a large country district, and as already they were as near "as Crewe an Nautwich they were hoping that their 'bank would not be unknown there and its time they trusted to build up a satisfactory business. They proposed placing another £20,000 to property account. Since they last met they hal entered into their new premises in Liverpool—premises that they thought worthy both of the city itself, and of the very large business done in Liverpool. Their director,air Eckersley was on his way home from South Africa, not again, he (the Chairman) hoped to return ithere. Be trusted the country sufficiently recognised the great sacrifice that a great many civilians had made in the cause of the empire. (Hear, hear.) The loans and advances to customers were not quite so heavy, as the demands of trade were not so great. In previous reports, they would probably have noticed one line which I must have grown familiar to them all. He referred to "£1,000.000 consols at 90." That line no longer appeared for they had changed half of that million into exchequer bonds. As they were aware consols would only carry interest at 21 per cent after 1903 and they were redeemable in 22 years time at par. The exchequer bends were redeemable in 41 years' time at par arid they carried interest at the rate of 3 per cent. If both Classes of these securities wete paid off at the times named the yield of interest would lea Bs 10a in favour of the exchequer bonds. It was most unlikely that they would be redeemed ha 1923 and the difference therefore in return was not less than 14s 8 I—fully a per cent in favour of the new investment. This cat- culation was based upon the present price of consols which ranged from 92 to 93. If they took the price at which they sold the consols as the basis of calculation, namely 971, the increased yield wou.d be/ per cent and even more. Be need not rernied them that the security in each case was absolutely identical. Since they last met many interesting and important things had occurred in the world at large, Every important event had its influence, direct or indi- rect, on banking and finance. The half year had been unrematkatle except in respect of the large borrowings that the Chancellor of the Each, quer cnrrird out. Though the customs and revenue returns still kept up in volume, trade continued to languish. The drop in the price of raw maierials, especially in coal, still enabled the manufacturers to make a fair profit on the reduced profits that they com- manded. At all events, he thought we could comfort ourselves with the tbought that at the present moment we were better off that our neighbours. There was always, as a French philosopher remarked, something not unpleasant in the misfortune of our friends and we could perhaps view the present industrial crisis in America with greater eqinimity when we reflected that quite lately they had been endeavouring to captere some of our trade. In Germany too, perhaps a too eager industrial expansiveness had resulted in a certain amount of financial embarrasrnent,—that kind o ' embar- rassment which invariably followed a SOMPV'hat too eager desire to go ahead too fast. Though we might learn sornethina from the errors of our neighbours we might learn still more from their industrial virtues and endeavour by improved methods to better r quip ourselves for the race in commercial supremacy which every year was becoming more strenuous and exacting for us. (Applause). Sir Jona HARWOOD said he had great pleasure in seconding the resolution. He always noticed that there were very few questions asked at shareholders meetings when there was a good dividend declared. Tnat was the best report a Chairman could make. He could assure them that the dividend was satisfactorily and honestly earned. Every penny of the divi- dend was earned. Referring to the securities as they appeared in the books and their value at the present time, he would like to add to what the chairman had already said that the plain English of the position was this. If they sold the securities to-day they would fetch £66 000 more than they appeared in the accounts. He thought that was very satisfactory and a matter upon which they might congratulate themselves. (Hear, hear.) The report was unanimously adopted. VOTES OE THANKS. _</p>
        <p>Mr. GEORGE DAETN-R-aid it -T;€;-ciWno words of hig to resommend the resolution be had to propose for their accep,ance, and that was that a vote of thanks b, given to the directors for their service 4 clurioß the oast year. Mr GARNETT seconded,and it was unanimously agreed to.</p>
        <p>Mr PETER STUBS then proposed a vote of thanks to the general manager, assistant general managers, the secretary, and the other ofacers of the hank for their services during the past half-year. The report. he said, would commend this resolution mnch more eloquently than any words of his, for their acceptance. Dr WHITE in seconding the resolution, expressed the wish tha': Mr Dun might be spared to continue manager of the Bank for many years to come. The rosolution was cordially_ agreed to.</p>
        <p>Mr Dux, in responding. said such resolutions were I generally considered mere matters of form but he felt that in Warrington—the cradle of the old bank—this resolution was passed wito a warmth and enthnsiat-m. fie had to regret al. the present moment the somewhat serious illness of his colleague and assistant Mr Munro. He was happy to say that they had a better account of his health and they had every reason to expect that he would be bappily re- stored to them within a comparatively short time. Two mnre efficient colleaaes than Mr Whalley and Mr Munro did not exist in the banking world. He had also to add his thanks to the members of the staff who had assisted in the management of the bank and brought about the surxesFful result which hadibeen laid before them that day. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>THE CHAIRMAN. Mr WILLIAM UNYEN proposed a vote of thanks to the Chaim3ao for preelding. They were indebted, be arid. , for the very clear statement which the Chairman had 'given them with reference to the securities of the bank. Mr H Mituso seconded the proposition, and hoped that Mr. Parr might live long to preside over the deAinies of the bank. (ApplausE) The proposition was unanimo-ashy agreed ta. The GUMMI/3N aciLmowlee]ged the complment, and said it was gratifying to feel that the shareholders wished him to fill the position he did. (ApP:arlse)• The proceedings then terminated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>LA.DIRS FIRST flt. ke 1 TV) )CS Lvb UNDER THE CLOCK, BRIDO-EC-STRRRT.—Advt.</head>
        <p>LA.DIRS FIRST flt. ke 1 TV) )CS Lvb UNDER THE CLOCK, BRIDO-EC-STRRRT.—Advt.</p>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Cricket.</p>
        <p>The value of a single run was powerfully Illus- trated in the match between Warrington and Bootle on . Saturday. When time was called the Bootle men were just . one run becind the Warrington total with four wickets to felt. Warrington compiled 201 runs, the largest contributor being JA Briggs, who made the creditable score of 65. Bootle had an hour and three-quarters in which to reach the Warrington total, and sucks good use did they make of their opportunity that whets the close of play arrived they nad put together 200 for the loss of six wickets. It must have been a terrible disappointment to the home toain to be so near and yet so far from victory. I understand that there were some who questioned the umpire's ruling as to the time for the drawing of stumps, but when the match is such a close one there is certain to be a differance of opinion as to the exact time for the closure to be applied. The match, it appears, was not altogether free from un- pleasant incidents, for during the Warrington innings, rodmore was given out by the home um .ire, tut this decision was overruled.</p>
        <p>Warrington going first to the wickets made anything but a promising start, for with only 25 rune on the score sheet both B Silcock and Thowlis bad been sent back. J A Briggs, following his capital performance at Aigburth, gave a brilliant exposition, and together with the Warrington captain, J W Bibby, the bowling was collared. Runs came at a rapid rate, and although a double change was tried at 64, Barlow and Crouch super- seding the original bowlers, Smith and Howarth, runs still continued to come with a consistency whicia augured well for a substantial score. At 35 the game was delayed owing to Briggs receiving a hard knock from a hard drive by his partner, but on resuming Bibby was caught at cover slip,having made 24 in fault- less style. J Callon came next and saw Briggs complete his 50, at the same time sending the century up. At 119 Callon was out lbw, and six runs later Briggs was adjudicated stumped, having made 64 without a faulty stroke. Hie chief hits were eleven fours. W H Polmore batted well for his runs, but during his innings of 38, he was ruled to be run out by the home umpire, but by the intervention of Mr Johnson the decision was over. ruled, and Mr Podmore was allowed to continue what was a stylish display. Both ilarripson and A Duckworth reached double figures, and the innings realized 201.</p>
        <p>Bootl2 commenced their t.,sk—", with an hour and three. quarters la which to accomplish it. From the com- mencement the batsmcn forced the game and runs came at a rapid rate. Johnson survived a cot. fident appeal for being stumped, and though Podmore was substituted for Silcock, the slow bowler was treated severely. Thewlis relieved Gibson, and this change soon worked. for Johnson hit one on to Bibby's foot, the ball travell- ing to W M Silcock, who safely held the catch. H. Williams joined Schofield and the batsmen c intinued to carry out their mission, keeping in front of the clot k. At 101 Thompson was tried, and got ‘liliiams caught ag the wicket in his first over. R. W. Killip came next an.l he too survived a palpable catch at the wicket. At 12g Schofield was caught for a vigorously hit 57. A fine catch by Thempsou sent Smith back at 164, whilst it 186 two wickets fell, bath Killip and Barlow getting in front to Thewlis. Both Moody and Crouch made a bold effort to win the match, and at 730 when time was called the total was 200 for six wickets, B )otle being one:run behind with four wickets in taad.</p>
        <p>The Sankey Wire Mills have been doing much better recently and their record for this season is week by week improving. Following up their victory of the previous week,they decisively beat Chester St Mary's on Saturday. The Cestrians could do very little with the bowling of Cook and Burton and weie all dismissed for the small total of 25 runs. This total was soon passed, Woodcock again being the top scorer for the wire m(n with 31 runs credited to him.</p>
        <p>Rival teams representing the Warrington Woiks and the Stratford Works of Messrs Richnoonds played a interesting game on Leicester County Ground on Sat, - day which was witnessed by a large number of specta- tors, including several of the staff and workpeople from the different branches. The advantage ultimately rested with Warrington though the match was drawn. A well I played innings by Bailey realised 66, while the bowling lof Fleetwood proved very disastrous to the Stratfcrd and London Contingent. Good batting was also exhito hited by Ransorne, Brown, &amp;tape and Carter. On the London side the batting honours rested between Makin and Dunn, who played good cricket for their respective scores, while the stonewalling tactics of Mclntyre eel- tainly saved the Londoners from defeat, who seemed to highly appreciate their Captain's efforts to save the game, which he just managed to do, and convert into a draw what would othe:wisl have been a tng sacresS for Warrington. Tte b.3st bowling on t.-,e Londom side was shown by Fowles.</p>
        <p>Angling. The members of the Howley Hotel Angling Stciety, held their opening match on Sunday last, the place selected being Vale Royal. The party having arrived in good time, the match commenced at eleven o'clock and was of two hours duration. 'fhe positions having been drawn for and the anglers having got into their allotted positions the signal was given and the match commenced. Scarcely had the lines reaehed the wat,r than a call of " landed " was heard. This locked like business and it was expected the bank-walker would be fully employed for the next two hours. This turned out to be the case, but the fish canght were of the usual Vale Royal weight. This match is looked forward to with keen interest by the novices of this district, and comment was rife as to their respective merits. "Uncle" was greatly fancied for premier honours by some of the nienthm, while others thought " Nick " would have a look in, but the tipsters were altogether "001" as these anglers finished near the bottom of the list. Time was called,and the weighing-in process having been completed the winner turned out to be T Robinson who caught 45 fish, the aggregate weight being 2:hs. Appended is a list of the principal weights caught.—Thomas Robinson. 21bs ; E Gilbert llb 7ozs.lodrs. ; G Beckett,llb. 6zs. ; G Spencer,llb. ; J Crooks,ls zs. Br.; J Berry, 14ozs. 3drs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
