        <p>ATTENDANCE. —GIRLS DEPARTMENT: Misses Prostor, Harrison. N Warrington, H Viarrington, Greavea, GriMn, Smith and Branwood.</p>
        <p>BM. DEPARTMENT : Mastois Bentley. A Wroe,Walkei, Gem, J C Sutton, Harry Su:ton. Har.slo. Smith,Roger, Schr,les, Grant, W Finnigan, D Jacks, J Dean, C Wroe, II Wroe, Stanley Wm?, Sampson, Whitworth, C Parr, $ Parr, Carter, H Finnigan, J Finnigan, and Hubert Sutton.</p>
        <p>: Misses Prcctor, Harrison, N War ringtOn, Greaves, Gran,Srnitti, Branwoad,Crossley and H Warrington. 1331'S : Masters Smith. Grant, Simpslri, Hanson. Scholes, II Sutton, J C Sutton, Roger, Hubert Sutton, C Wroe, H Wroe, C Parr,Jacks, &amp; Wroe, S Wroe, Gera and John Dean.</p>
        <p>-- - EX,S.itIi,IATION. —GIRLS Misses GreiVeB, DOSSIeVe GrltEn, H Warrington, A Harrison. Boys : Masters A Vi'rce, K Roger, J C &amp;Won And Harry Sutton.</p>
        <p>, SPECIAL Aweircs.—English, Miss Gziffin and Master it Roger ; singing, Mister C Taylor ; athletics, presented by the Sgaire, Masbr K Roger ; painting, Master H Sutton presented by Mr Carr ; vacation sketches, .kiaster II Finnigan ; gardening, Masters Ranson, Parr , and Poliard ; calisthenic., Miss Et Warrington ; needle- work, Miss Davidson ; Stanley geographical award. Miss M Greaves. Master E De4n was voted by hi2l fellow pupils to be the most manly boy and received a, silver medal.</p>
        <p>A letter was read from Sir H M Sta .p.l con. gratulating Miss May Greaves upon having won his. special priz for geography and regretting the fact that some boy had not taken it but he took it as a token that the girla were progremUnz in all re:Teets and in future,- he hoped thy would make good mothets for a rising . generation,</p>
        <p>- Dr SEPHTON proposel a bearty vote of thanks to Cap- tain and Mrs Withingron. Mr 8 Sutton seconded and said that in all hie experience be had never known a better conducted 6cboot.and he did not know @f a school in vi hien tha laoys and girls fared better.. This Wall tribute which Mr and Mrs Granville richly deserve&amp; Mr Sutton, of Culcbeth, also supported the vote,. which was carried unanimously.—Captain Withington, replied in suitable terms. The interesting proceeding then tarninitti d.</p>
