        <head>OUR FOOD,</head>
        <p>OUR FOOD,</p>
        <p>Constant overworking of the stomach must produce disease of that organ. More nutriment is furnished to ttx body by a small quantity of food well digested than be a large quantity half digested. The only trustwolthy and permanent remedy is Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, which, acting upon the digestive organs, strengthens them and stimulates them to healthy action and gives tone to the whole stomach, imparting new life, hope, strength, and happiness. Overwork ought to be overpaid. Nature insists upon payment of her dues. Intense devotion to business gives the digestive organs only a bare chance to perform their work. He who comes in a hurry from active work, rapidly swallows his food awl hurries cut again, is piling up a debt which Niture makes him pay in a painful dyspeptic experience. Gwilyrn Evans' Quinine Bitters will help you to pay, and sooner you rake it the better. Delicate patients often complain that they are unable to take quinine, a, it produces headaches, &amp;c., but it has been established beyond doubt that Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters bas overcome this difficulty, containing, as it does other medicinal herbs of great therapeutic value,sucla as sarsa- parilla, saffron, dandelion, gentian, and laventhr' scien- tifically prepared in happy proportions. Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters is sold in bottles 2s. 91. and 4s. 61. each, by all cbenaists and stores. Beware of imitations. The name " Gwilym Llvans " must be found cn the label, scamp, and bottle, to insure its being genuine. When you find the name, then you are sure there is no mistake. The Sole Proprietors are The Quinine Bitters Manufacturing Company, Limited, Llanelly, South Wales.</p>
