      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</head>
        <p>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</p>
        <p>SYSTEMATIC THEFTS BY A WARRINGTON CARTER.</p>
        <p>William Lowe. PMlip-street, was cnarged on remand at the Warrington Court on Saturday with having stolen a quantity of cart fittinge.valued at £3o,the pronerty of the mente employer, Mr Ed ward Isherwood, of Bewsev- road —Lowe pleaded guilty.—The Chief Constable said that prisoner was in Mr Isherwood 's employ as a carter. Mr Isberwood keet a lot of carts and cart fittings in Eeweey-streat. Oa the 17th July a man named George Henry Daniels visited the yard for the purpose of fitting a bush to an arm, but was unable to find any. He knew that there had been plenty there a month previously. He asked his employer and Mr Isherwood told him thate he knew nething about it. Be neat saw the articles produced, which weighed lton 3 at. 3qr„ at the Police Station. It appeared that prisener had been from time to time going to the yard and taking these articles , pirseeeeeal. He took them to Mr Peter Taylor, 5, Waver- , street, a marine store dealer, who occupied an arehwa7 in Crown-street. Prisoner had acted very ingeniously. He went to the yard when nobody was about. On mare than one occasion he got a young man named Frank Taylor o go and help him by promising him one half of the proceels. The pence were satisfied that Taylor had no guilty knowledge. To Mr Taylor, the marine store dealer, he said his name was "J. Jackson " and that he was a carter residing in John-street. He said he was employed by a Mr Lee as a carter at Bewsey, who was giving up carting and he hart received the articles as a portion of ha wages. That was a most feasible tale and Mr Taylor bought the articles. The police made no allegations against Mr Taylor. The sale was duly entered on the sheet which every dealer kept in the manner required by the police. The articles were valued at £3O and the price paid WAS £l. las id. Prisoner had made 16 vieits to the place between the 20:h April and the 4th July inclusive. When he was arrested by Superintendent Monaghan, he admitted the thefts, and said he had taken two barrow loat's and a cart load. The most serious feature of the case was that prisoner might haw! got Mr Taylor into very serious troubie, and also Mr Frank Taylor. Mr Isnerwood said that when the arcicles were missed sus- picion was thrown on all his emplosas.—Lowe said it was the first time he had been in Court, and he hoped the magistrates would give hires a chance.—The Chair- man said the case was a very serious one. Their decision was that Lowe should go to gaol for six EConths.—The Chief Constable siiel he applied for the whole nf the articles to be returned to Mr Isherwood,— Mr A Browne, who represented Mr Taylor, acquiesced.</p>
        <p>THE EFFECTS OF EIAnD.DRISKING. At the County Borough Police Court on Saturday, before Mr T Birchall (in the chair) and other magis- trates Thomas Garrity, labourer, was charged on re- mand with assaulting James Buckley Mellor near the 4rple7 Cricket GrouLd on the previous Saturday even- ing, and with stealing 21i from bins—Defendant, who bad been remanded from Wednesday, was represented 'by Mr Browns.—The Chief Constable said the prosecu- tor, James Buckley Mellor, an elderly man, lived at 22. rinner's Brow. He left home On Saturday about 7 o'clock and walked round town, afterward; going to the "'Three-cornered-pit " in Arpiey to look for his son, who bad been fishing there. About ten minutes past eight he was returning home, and when opposite the bawling green of the Warrington Cricket Club he met the defendant. Tne latter then appeared to be labour- .ing under excitement due to drink. He was a perfect stranger to Mellor. He rushed up to him, to®k hold of his muffler and said, " Give me some money." He butted prosecutor in the face and prosecutor then fell to the ground. Defendant got on top of lorm and put his right hand into one of prosecu- tor's trougers pockets. The roan on the floor shluted ... Murder" and defendant put his knee on his chest. Mellor was considerably knocked about, and bled pro- fusely from the ears. Defendant afterwards got up and . walked away. M -Mot collected himself as best he could ,and walked towards town. He there met a young man named James Warburton, and a young lady. These two ;acted the part of the Good Samaritan to him ny brush• -bog his clothes and wiping the blood from him. Defen- dant was pointed cut, and Warburton and another man ; kept prisoner in sight until a policeman arrived. When IP.C. Hewitt arrived on the scene be received the com- plaint from Mellor. D;ifendant also looked dirty and was covered with dust. Ein was taken to the police . station, and on being charged said " I don't know whether I did it or not."—Mr Browne, after some con- ,sultation with his client, said that on his advice defen- dant pleaded guilty to common assault. He (Mr Browne) ;bad a little explanation to make. a volunteer who 'igived d'se to the defendant had recently come from the front and had had his usual month's tv-liday. The Volun- 54eer bad also had the usual amount of money, and had telnnetimes taken the defendant out at half-past six in the linorning and they had been drinking all the day. They 1 sbad been drinking all that week. On the day in question ,defendant had tan much drink. He remembered going 'down Arpley, but remembered nothing about the —assault. He had an exceedingly good character and this g, was his first offence. It was merely a drunken freak. —James Buckley Mellor said that the statement made by a. the Chief Constable was correct. Defendant was like a il madman when he came to him. Defendant was quite a S •string.r to hirn.—The Chairman said that defendant bad been in custody since Monday and the week seemed to ' :have done him good. It was. however, a very serious offence. He would be fined 20; and c.oats,ot in defau't, .1 one month's imprisonment. . ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>MINOR OASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR OASES.</p>
        <p>, Warrington Borough Court. , ,</p>
        <p>• FRIDAY--Before the Mayor (In the chair). Councillor Smethurst and John Roberts and Messrs Hepherd and Thornton. UNH \PEE MARRIED LlFE.—David Griffiths, 20, Etew-rosd. was summoned fr assaulting his wife, Jane'Griffiths, on the 14th inst The evidence was to the effect that defendant struck his wife on the head with a bottle, inflicting a wound ' which bled freely—A fine of ICs. was inflicted, —Complainant t then applied for a separation order, stating that her husband had constantly il:used her. A thort time ago she had to ~. run o t of the house to escape h'm. Ile had told her thst ; if he lived with her fily years he would never keep hi, hands off her—A rei.aration order and 113 per week alimony ;• was granted. 4• TEREAT3.—John Lancaster, E.O. Sharp-street, was bound, CV er to keep the peace and ordered to pay lOs costs for . making use of threats towards John Braudwocd, 20, •• Wingred-street. SA'runn.sy—Before Mr Birchall (in the chair), Councillor John Roberts and Messrs UT Nairclom.,,la and J W Hayward. A WAWDERER.—g3WSrd Salith, last from Manchester, was charged with wandering about Winwick-road at midnight on Friday without having any visible means of subsistence P C Mulroy proved the case.—The Chief Constable said that t'Zithe man was not in his ri4ht senses, and had informed him that he had been drinking heavily of late. He woald, there- fore ask for him to be sent to the workhouse for 14 days In</p>
        <p>order that his mental condition might be ascertaine (I,—T he Bench agreed and Smith was accordingly remanded. " A GOOD Syncx OF SO2NOW."—EIIen Smith, of no settled abode, was chirged wiL having been drunk and disorderly in Wellington-street on Friday night.—P.S. Williamson proved the case.—Prisoner said she was very sorry,—The Chief Constable said that she had always `• a good stook of sorrow." This was her 55th appearance. but she had been out of town for some time, and they had not seen much of her. --She was sent to gaol for a month ISCHOOL AIrENDA.NCE CARES.—The following persons were fined for not sending their children regularly to school Samuel Kean, 75, Sharp-street, and John Noon, SS Church street, 4s ; George Edward Roberts, 69, Plumpton-street, and James Primer, 33, Catherine-street, 5s ; George Jones, 1, Ireland-street and Henry Tranter, 55, Aiken-street, 2s 6d. In the case of James McKeen, of 11, Albert-street., an order for the child to attend school was made.—Mr William Jones, school attendance officer, prosecuted in all the cases. MONDAY.—Before Mr F Blonks (in the chair), Alderman, Heaton, Councillor imethurst and Messrs Eirchall, Hay- ward, Wright and Thorrvon. MISCFLLANEOUS.—BIiza Hill, 7, Dakin's-yard was fined ss. and costs for having been drunk and disorderly on Saturday Right in Dakin's-yard. —Thomas Patrick Carrot was bound over to k -ep the peace and ordered to pay the( sets (13s 6ff) for having failed to appear to a summons charging him with committing a breach of the peace by fightinw in Horse- market-street on March 16th —John Spencer, 4, Morris-yard, and John Ratcliffe, 46, Watkin-street, were fined 5s for having been drunk and disorderly in Winwiek-street on Saturday night —William Datdes, 37, Scotland-road, was sent to gaol for a month for sleeping in a stable belonging to Mr Edward Conno-, Sankey-street, and having no visitde means of subsistence. A box of matches was found upon the prisoner, INDECENCY.—James Maxwell, labourer, 17. Tilley-street, was fined 418 and costs (22 I6s), or in default a month's imprisonment for indecent conduct In White-street on Saturday evening The defendant stated that he was under the influence of drink at the time and did not remember anything of the occurrence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>Sir Richard M Brooke. Bart, and Major C W Hazlehurst held thesesre;alons on Monday when Peter Rogers. Canal- street, and Mary Ellen Rose, Bridgwater-street, were each fined 108 for using obscene language.—Teresa Yarwood. a warded woman living apart from her husband, was charged with assisting Mrs Emma Burrows in the management of a disorderly house at 1, Egerteu-street . She pleaded guilty and was sentenced to a month's hard labour, Ann Corco—n made her 35th appearance. the charge on this or -melon being " drunk and disorderly." P.C. Bratt proved the case and the prisoner was sentenced to a month's hard labour.—John Lambert, to, Surrey-street, labcurer. %Yes summoned for using abusive language to his wife Eliza Lambert of 7, Clarence-street. The parties have lived apart for five years, the defendant by order of the magistrates contributing 75 6d per week towards the support of wife and family. Recently he had constantly annoyed her by visiting her home and using abusive language towards her.—A fine of 5e and costs was Imposed, and the defendant was advised to leave hie wite alone.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>GREAT SANKEY PARISH COUNCIL,</head>
        <p>GREAT SANKEY PARISH COUNCIL,</p>
        <p>A meeting of thii Council was held in the Board School, Great Sankey on Toesday evening. Mr G Thomp3on presided and there were also present, Messrs Fulpon Smith, W Taylor, and J Singleton, with J Smith (clerk). THE BIOKING OFFICE ARRANGEMENTS AT I.IE STATION.</p>
        <p>The Clerk read a communication from Mr I) Mel- drum, of the Cheshire Lines Committee, stating that in reply to the communication of the Parish Council he had looked into the point raised and found that it was customary at Sankey station for the booking office window to be opened to the pub- lic at Least ten re iuutes before train time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>I NCREASED POSTAL FACILITTE3.</head>
        <p>I NCREASED POSTAL FACILITTE3.</p>
        <p>Mr J L Vin-ey-,-instmaster at 'Warrington, wrote on July Ist stating that a later collection would be made frprn the Great. Sankey post office, to be sent in to War- rington fly the 932 p.m. train. This applied to week- days. The other matters raised by the Council were still under consideration. _ - -</p>
        <p>Mr John Afli:ol3, of the General Post (Mice, London, wrote that the Postmaster General had given care- ful cons;deration to the Council's recent application to the Postmaster of Warrrington but regretted that the circumstances of the case were not such as to justify him in opening a telegraph office at Great Sankey. The number of telegrams sent by residents in the neighbour- hood was not large and the telegraph requirements of the locality seemed to him to be sufficiently met by the existing telegraph offices. THE COUNTY MEDICAL OFFICgR OF HEALTH AND SANKEY</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>BROOK</head>
        <p>BROOK</p>
        <p>Dr Edward Sergeantot the Public Health Department of the County Offices, Preston, wrote stating that the Council's communication of the 3:d ult was receiving attention, but before completing his report be should Like to know where Mesqrs Houghton, Ridgway and Sloane, the chief complainants resided, and whether they hici works or conducted their business operations in the parish. Might he ask whether the nuisance they complained of had increased during recent yearn; and if so could the clerk suggestoloy cause for the additional nuisance ? Did the Sankey' Brook now form the bound- ary between the Warrington Rural District and the Borough ? The Clerk stated that be bad replied to the letter, pointing out that the gentlemen named did not reside in Great Sankey, but they came into the parish every day to conduct their business,and many of their workpeeple resided in the parish. He was unable to suggest the cause of the additional nuisance. Dr Sergeant had since acknowledged the receipt of the letter, but nothing further had been received.</p>
        <p>THE REMOVAL OF NIGHTSOIL. MEM ',RILL FROM HOUSEHOLDERS.</p>
        <p>The Clerk read a petition he bad received, signed by 63 householders and ratepayers of Great Sankey,calliug attention to the objectionable arrangement now exist- ing for the remnal of the night-soil, which was done between the hours of 9am and 5 p.m. There had been several cases of fever in the neighbourhood recently and it waf not unreasonable to suppose, con- tinued the petitioners, that these cases bad been the immediate outcome of the intolerable system now ih operation. Should the present hot weather continue, it was only reasonable to assume that the number of tever cases would be considerably increased if immedi- ate steps were not talon to have the insufferable nuis- ance abated. the petitioners urged that the removal should be conducted during the night-time. A discussion took place with rcference to the matter and the opinion was expressed that an improvement ip the existing state of things was desirable. It was de- cided to leave the matter in the bands of the Chairman for the preent and to further consider the:matter at the next alee irg.</p>
        <p>TliE CINDITION OF HALTON BRIDGE.</p>
        <p>Mr Ttrirapson n,croduced this matter, and said the Penketh Palish Council bad been doing their best to get an improvement made at the (x'sting dangerous bridge. He felt sure there would be a serious accident there if an alteration wers not made. The greater portion of the bridge was really in Great Sankey and he suggested that the Council should call the attention of the County Council to the dangerous state of the approach to the bridge and asked for it to be remedied. He proposed a resolution to that effect. Mr Singleton seconded the proposition and it was un- animously agreed to, This was all the business of public interest.</p>
        <p>Death of Mrs. J. Forbes. The death occurred suddenty on Sunday morning at Ainsworth ouse, on Moor, Bolton, of Mrs Forbes, wife of the Rev James Forbes, and eldest diughter of the Rev E J Willcocks, headmaster of the Warrington Grammar Szhool. The deceased lady was 26 years of age, and was married to Mr Forbes on June 2nd,1898, at the Warrington Parish Church. Mr Forbes is headmaster of the Church Institute, Bolton, and was formerly at Warrington. In consequence of Mrs Forbes death the annual Leech day at the Grammar Bchool, which was to have taken place on Thursday, was postponed.— The funeral of the dead lady took place in Prest- wich Churchyard, on Wednesday.</p>
