      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>EX.AMINEE, FRI!) AY, JULY 26 1901.</head>
        <p>THE EX.AMINEE, FRI!) AY, JULY 26 1901.</p>
        <p>THE WAR. By Arthur H. Crosfield.</p>
        <p>[[ll.] Sir,—ln a previous letter I have dealt with two of the main excuses for this war, namely the arguments (1) that because Mr Kruger issued the ultimatum which began it he must bear the responsibility, and (2) ' that he refused to meet us half way by granting reason- able reforms. I have attempted to show (1) that tha Boer ultimatum was preceded on our side by provoca- tions to which it was a natural though a foolish reply and (2) that the Boer Government did in point of fact offer to yield what we had demanded. It was, I have argued, the needless baste and the reckless diplomacy— or lack of diplomacy—of the Colonial Searetary, work. hog on the fears and suspicions of the Baers, which brought the war about. But an opponent of the war who reasons on these lines from the dates and the Blue books is always met by another set of excuses, " There was," we are told, " a great Boer conspiracy to drive the British into the sea. The Dutch had been arming for years and were only waiting for a favourable oppor- tunity to sapel us from South Africa, this is, therefore, a war to repel aggression, to defend the empire and re- pair the mistakes of Mr Gladstone, who restored t d the Transvaal after the battle of Majuba an independence which it has only abused." This argument sank into the mind of the country during the days when every week brought news of some fresh disaster to our arms— Scormberg, 3lagersfontein and Colenso. It flattered our vanity to oelieve that the Boers were a formidable military power. We for the moment overlooked the fact that they were never able to put more than 50,000 untrained men into the field against our force of 250,000 and that it their guns were of the latest pattern (while our own were more or less obsolete) that only went to show how recent their armaments were. But alarm is an emotion that is fatal to logic and destructive to a nation's sense of humour. We fell a victim to this gro— . _ _ . . .</p>
        <p>tesque myth of a plot in which two little pastoral com- munities were leagued against the greatest Empire in the world, much as the French gave way during the Dreyfus case to the legend ot a great cosmopolitan con- spiracy against France. Men are usually just uatil their fears are awakened. The French Jingoes used the story of a plot in which Jews and Protestants were banded together with E 'gland and Germany to destroy the Republic, as an argument for refusing a fair trial to one unlucky Jewish citizen. [be same appeal to panic has been employed in this country to induce as to crush the two Boer commonwealths. But let us examine the evidence,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>BOER ARMAMENTS.</head>
        <p>THE BOER ARMAMENTS.</p>
        <p>The only definite piece of evidence that has ever been brought in support of this theory of a Dutch Conspir- acy is the fact that for some time before the war the Transvaal had been arming on an extensive scale. That of course is perfectly true. But the real question is, the motive behind these armaments. Were they for attack or defence ? The question answers itself if we can only fix the date of these preparations. If they began more or less after Majuta, then it might be fair 1 to argue that the Boers,not satisfied with Mr Gladstone's act of justice, were determined to assert themselves at our expense and evu-st South Africa, from us. But this question of dates is linked to another crucial event in South African history—the Jameson Raid of 1195-13. The Boers had learned that a cod- spiracy existed between the gold indnstry in their midst, the Diamond interest at Kmberley, and the Chartered Company in Rhodesia with Mr Rhodes, a Privy Councillor and the Premier of the Cape, at its head, to rob them of their independence. The exploits of the raiders were applauded in Capetown and London and sung by the Poet Laureate, Mr Rhodes 1 was whitewashed in the House of Commons by the 1 Colonial Secretary, and to this day the Boers—and not the Boers alone—believe that Mr Chamberlain himself</p>
        <p>aas implicated in this guilty business. That suspicion is not confined to the Transvaal, to the Ccntinent, nor</p>
        <p>yet to some of his politicAl opponents in this countrp, for even a Unionist writer like Dr Conan Thyle hints at</p>
        <p>the same thing. When they saw that the Imperial authori- ties cared neither Co punish Mr Rhodes nor to clear themselves of any shadow of complicity, what conclu- sion could they possibly reach ? They knew that all the wealth and the power of British South Africa ss as against them, and that powerful interests in England were linked with those of the South African magnates. They had therefore every reason to fear a renewal of the raid on a larger and more menacing scale. The open partisanship of Sir Alfred Milner with the Rhodesian party aD the Cape after 1897 only increased their fears, and Mr Chamberlain's baseless re-assertien in 1897 of the</p>
        <p>claim to a suzsrainty over the Transvaal, which we had dropped in the Convention of 1834, must have redoubled their suspicions. If under these circumstances they armed,'the obvious presumption would be that they armed to defend the independence which Mr Rhodes and his party had threatened. THE DATES.</p>
        <p>And this presumption is confirmed beyond question by the dates of these armaments. These dates can be fixed. It is true that the " Daily Mail" has declared that the Boers have been arming for " nearly eighteen years . . . . to oust England from South Africa." But the facts are against it. In the first place we have before us the yearly military budget of the Transvaal. In 1893 it spent only £19,340 on armaments, in 1894 owing to a native war the figure reaches £28.158 In 1895, the year of the Raid it stands at £87,308. In 1896 it leaps at one bound to a clear half million, and remains at that relatively high figure or even exceeds it in subsequent years. But we are not left to the evidence of the Boers themselves. A few months before the* Raid Major Robert White was sent by the Chartered Company to Pretoria to take stock of the situation. His inventory of the few,obsolete guns which the Boers then possessed will be found in the blue books (Cape Report on the Jameson Raid, p. 169). as also his conclusion," None of the guns I saw were fit for much work." More recently Dr Jameson himself has declared at Kimberley in a public speech that at the time he made his raid the armaments of tNe Boers were beneath contempt. He actually proposed to rush the Pretoria Arsenal, which was guarded he tells us, by no more than three men land a "broken down corrugated iron fence "! We know moreover exactly when and why the arming began. Captain Younghusband, a very competent authrr ty, visited Pretoria early in 1898. He states in his book, "South Africa of to-day," (pp 141/3) chat "The Boers had very nearly been caught napping at the beginning of the year. They were then, however, ordering guns and rifles in great numbers, engaging European artillerymen and constructing formidable C orta near Pretoria. " One attempt "he reinarks„.4haa been made to take their country from them ; they were thoroughly convinced that the attempt would be renewed at some future date ; so the Boers were determined to be on their guard the second time." Further comment on this point is unnecessary. If the primary aim of the Boers bad been to conquer the Cape, they would not have spent vast sums of money in fortifying Pretoria. Forts are for defence. Finally all this evidence hi been ratified in an Official British Publication. Some time before the war General Sir John Ardagh, the chief of the intelligence depart- ment of the War Ofile, prepared a very careful report on the military resources oi the two Republics, portions of which have since found their way into print. It contains full particulars about the guns which the Boers had bought in Europe, and shows conclusively that every piece had been purchased since t Jarne3oo Raid. Before the Raid. says this report, the Transvaal had no more than 13,500 Martini rifles. Its vast store of Mauier magazine rifles has all been purchased since, while the artillery force which then only numbered one handred men was at once increased to four hundred with large reserv^s. Is it necetisary to labour this point further? the armamenta of the Transvaal were pur- chased SINCE the Raid and were designed for DEFENCE; and this is so obv:one that to argue any more seems like killing the dead horse. If I pursue the sub:ect it is because this alleged conspiracy of the Dutch and Boers has been more sethilously spread about and done more duty among the coostituencies than any of the other afterthoughts and plausible fictions which have helped to salve consciences and delude the public. IThe art of what in trade circles is sometimes called "gulling the public," and the corresponding art in politics of praying to the gallery, is very well understood in Birmingham. The cue given by the astute and unscrupulous wire puller who rules the roost there, has been taken readily enough by the rest of his party in the country to an extent and in a manner which, as a Conservative remarked to me the other day, "has done more than anything else in our timb to vulgarise the tone of political life in E ialand." The methods I allude to con- sist in appeals not to reason,or any sense of fairness and English chivalry, but to ignorance, popular prejudice and panic. THE CAPE DUTCEI AND THE CONSPIRICY.</p>
        <p>And so it is further said in support of this theory— nnless we should rather call it a wild canard—of a gigantic conspiracy that the Cape Dutch were in sympathy with tha Boers, and in some obcure way in league with them. It is not easy to treat such a sugges- tion with patience. It is true that the Cape Dutch regard this war as unjust and am oecessary. They have in addition a natural and proper sympathy with their kinsmen. But the best praof of their loyalty is the fact that during two invasions of the Cape,though they have seen their constitution set aside, though they profoundly distrust Sir Alfred Milner, though they believe the Republics to be in the right, they have not risen. The Africander Bond which had been descrined as a disloyal body his done its utmost to urge its members to remember their obligations as British subjects, and the few hundred Dutchmen who have joined the enemy did so largely under compulsion ard against the advice of their political leaders. Yet it is hardly doubtful that had the Dutch risen in any large numbers after the reverses of t•tormbe.rg, Magersfontein, and Coleus) our armies wou'd everywhere base been forced to retreat to the coast, or to shut themselves np in towns and endure a siege. ,Toe story that the Transvaal had secretly armed the Dutch in the Cap and in Natal is clearly shown to be false, and the Bond has proved its loyalty. In fact, I believe the only evidence against it is a series of articles written twenty years ago by a Hutch pastor named Da Toit. He did indeed preach a racial conflict</p>
        <p>and dreamed of an eventual union of South Africa under a Dutch flag. But his programme was disowned by the Bond so soon as it was lormulated.He himself was expelled from the Dutch piety, and he is new a prominent adherent of Mr Rhodes. If it is necessary to give any further proofs of the loyalty of the Cape Dutch one need only refer to the eulogy which Sir Alfred Milner himself penned in an oft nal dispatch so recently as ,23d August 1897, after the Jubilee celebrations. Even the racial bitterness caused by the Reid HAD NOT, be then wrote, "affocted the loyalty of any Portion of the pop- . ulatioa to Her Majesty the Queen," Ithe JAN HOFMEYR THE LEADER OF THE •FRICANDER BOND WAS THE WARM. EST ADVOCATE IN ALL THE EMPIRE OF IHPERIAL FEDER&amp; WON. IN 1893 WITH A BOND MINISTRY IN POWER, THE DUTCH CABINET CARRIED WITII,UT • DISSENTIENT VOTE• THE PR)POSAL TO PAY AN ANNUAL TRIBUTE OF £30,000 TO THE hIPERIAL NAVY. No OTHER COLONY HAS EVER DONE TEIA.T. Surely then it is clear that these people so de- voted to the Queen and the Empire, so mindful of their duty under unparalleled provocations were not privy te any anti-British conspiracy. It is true that Sir Alfred Miner became unpopular, true that Chamberlain was distrusted,true that the Reid had sown discord bstween the two races all over South Africa. But these things do not constitute disloyalty.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>CONSPIRiTORS AT WORK.</head>
        <p>THE CONSPIRiTORS AT WORK.</p>
        <p>But perhaps the most effective bit of evidence that can be found regarding this theory of a Pan-Africander con- spiracy is supplied by a private diary accidentally cap- tured during the war. I': belonged to Mr Van Kratsch- mar, a Hollander and the Managing Director of the Netherlands Railway Company, which rendered the Transvaal such great services during the war. Mr Van Kretschmar though a frank and honest man was clearly a bit of a Jingo. He seems to have come to the core• elusion that war was inevitable very early in the sum- mer of 1899 and his diary (pu`slished in the Blue Book of the Concessions Enquiry) records his impatience and satirises the easy-going hesitations of Mr Kruger and General doubert. On july 21.1 t he writes regarding the British preparations for war, which were even then going busily forward in Natal:— "It has surprised me that the Transvaal Govern- ment has up to the present paid so little atten- tion to the preparations." _</p>
        <p>His alarm took hint to Genertl Joubert with whom he had a formal consultation; he records his impression of Joubert's preparedness thus : He had absolutely no plan of defence. . . . . Re lamented the unpleasantness of carrying on a war," and said that the "Transvaal were not pre- pared fir it."</p>
        <p>Yet General Joubert was the Commander in Chief of the Transvaal. If there had been a great conspiracy to drive the British into the sea, surely he must have known of it, surely he must have thought out some way of effecting his design ? So afar from this, he had not even a plAn of attack ! No he had not even a plan of defence. We have been:told that the Transvaal counted on the aid of the Cape Ditch and had secretly supplied them with rine.. Well Mr Tan Kretschnoar as his friend as a good Hollander, Jingo and the chief of the railway on wh ch so much would depand in case of war, knew nothing of it. Here is what G:neral Joubert said to him on this subject in Jnly . _ .</p>
        <p>•• The Tranevaal had not taken sufficient care of the friendship with the Cape Celony. No assistance could be expected from their side because the Boers in the erne Colony were not armed. All that was to be done now was to try and avoid war."</p>
        <p>This, let us remember, is a contemporary record in a private diary, a diary which contains the frankest con- fessions of Mr :ran Bretschmar's own breaches of neut rality in the course of the war. But the diary contains other records of no less interest. In July Joubert stilt thought war could he avoided. On August 16th tie told Van Bretschmar that he had come to the conclusion that " England wants war." Nevertheless a day later he still refused to begin to mobolise the Boer Commandos. On August Igth, however, Mr Kruger seemed to have taken alarm. A meeting of the Cabinet was summoned, and Mr Van Kretschmar as the chief of the railway was called in to give his advice. Here he was at:least inside the inner ring of the conspirators. Yet still there was no talk of an aggressive war, no scheme for ousting the British from South Africa. The business on hand is thus recorded "The President asked me whin intended to do if the English should attempt to enter the country by rail.' The fact was. as Mr Van Kretschtnar explains at . .</p>
        <p>length, :hat:Mr Kruger had been thrown irro 'conster— nation by the news that the British had got an armeured train in Natal. And these were the astute and daring men whorls we charge forsooth with plotting against the British Empire. If they arm,d it Wa3 only because toey believed that we meant to take their country. If in the end they themselves declared war it was because they were convinced that we were about to repeat the Jame- son Raid no a larger scale. WERE TREY ALT3GETHER wins() ?* • MR. GLADSTONE'S SETTLEMENT OF 181.</p>
        <p>When driven from every other position apologists the war invariably fly to 1831 and blame Gladstone for giving back the independence to the Boers, It would perhaps be pertinent to the issue to inquire whether Disraeli had any right to take away their independence In 1377. But without going too much into ancient his- tory, does any one suppose we should have heard a syllable, about this settlement of 1331 in after years ; that it would have been complained of any more than the independence of the Orange Free State, but for one thing—the discovery of gold in 1336. That introduced new conditions and new problems by no means insoluble, but not to be solved either by Jameson Raids or the " new diplom- acy." As the Uitlandsr population in the Transvaal inereased reforms in the Government became necessary; and as we have seen were on the point of being con- ceded even in spite of Mr Chamberlain's overbearing and exasperating methods. Tory as he no doubt is from temperament —and EXPERIENCE—Mr Kruger could not resist the united pressure of England. Cape Colony, the Free State, the wealthy, powerful and numerous Uit lenders and the Progressives in his own Parliament. Roughly speaking, there were I think eight Progressives against sixteen Tories in the Raid. One third of the Transvaal legislature backed us up ; so did the Free State ; so did Dutch influence ha Cape Colony as long as people believed we were bent on reforms. The revul- sion of feeling and the loss of Dutch sympathy and sup- port came when they saw that what financiers backed up by our Jingoes wanted, was not reforms but the country itself.. . .</p>
        <p>True, every legitimate reform would not have been carried at once._ Well, whtt then What about our</p>
        <p>• noble selves P Have we been so very expeditious in our reforms. Mr Herb.rt Spencer may well remind us of the time it has taken to carry some of our own great measures. CORRUPTION NO EXCUSE FOR THE WAR.</p>
        <p>And he gives us another timely reminder of the cor- ruption that existed in our own Government—the mother of Parliament—up to less than a century ago. This is worth noting by those who hint—and it is one of the minor excuses regularly trotted nut by our Conservative friends—that we were justified in going t 3 war because there was used in Pretoria a good deal of that palm oil which circu- lates So freely in tha commercial life of England and in the commercial and political life of Europe and America alike. Are we going to war with the American Union because leading citizens of Chicago, as has been stated. have escaped by bribery as much as as per cent of the rates justly due by them. Bribery and corruption are bad ; aut if politicians in Pretoria needed squaring as they are squared in Paris or Washington, infinitely better to get reforms that way than to plunge the country into the horrors of war and famine. A man who sees his house and workshop burnt to the ground and is left starving in the streets,does not thank you for offering him an up-to-date system for heating and cook. ing, which saves some waste and perquisites. But we need to consider another alternative, ter we have had Sir Alfred Mih3er's assurance that instead of insisting on particular reforms it would have been better to let the tlitlanders—or those who would avail themselves of the chance—have the franchise and so work out their own salvation. Does anyone doubt they would have done it, with the Rand far ahead of the Bner3 in wealth and population, backed up by the influence of those other Imperial and South African forces to which I have just , alluded.</p>
        <p>FEDER TION.</p>
        <p>And then we come to federation ; is it not probable that the same influences and forces and in the long run common interests would have brought this about ? Not in Kruger's day, but the late President is an old man of YR. If this is prophecy it is nevertheless the opinion of some of the best ju lees on the spot, who ridicule the notion—founded on the remarks of a few hot heads— that the Boers would ever have been so insane as no launch themselves in a hopeless and ruinous attack up to the Empire, when the first to resist them would hay been the Dutch of Cape Colony and the Free State. We are apt to forget how the Free State stood by us as recently as the " Drifts " question. The alliance between them and the Transvaal formed at the time of the Raid was not offensive but del.-naive. The future does not admit of proof or demonstration, but are we justified in courting a war on mere hazy apprehension? IF so we had better at once tackle Russia or some other country nearer our Biz But the proposition carries with it its own refutation, and I have already devoted more than enough space to what is said about a Pan-africander conspiracy in the future, now that another of those afterthoughts also alluded to by:which we seek to justify the war—the wrongs of the Uitlander—has broken down. We all know to-day what a hollow farce this cry was: like the "Women and children" cry manufactured by the Nana people and for the skarn purpose, which preceded it at the time of the Jameson Raid. Suffering Uitlanders ! Toe capitalists were coining money, and the Cornish miners earning a poond a day, enjoyed a prosperity for which alas they now sigh in vain, and will have no chance of regaining when the war is over. INTERESTS OF LABOUR.</p>
        <p>BPCIAIS. apart from certain questions of rail- way rates and dynamite, THE MAIN OBJECT OF THEI FINANCIERS WHO HAVE BEEN AT THE BOTTOM OF THIS STRUGGLE, IS Ta SUBSTITUTE CHEAP LABOUR</p>
        <p>BY BLACICi AND BAFFIRS, FOR WELL PAID LABOUR BY EUROPEANS. TIDS is not surmise, for they have plainly told us so. A nice object truly for wnich to sacrifice all these lives, saddle our3elves with a moun- tain of debt and permanently increase the weight of taxation. Do evil that good may come is a doctrine at which the Anglo-Saxon has always flattered himself he looks askance, but even that doubtful object cannot be argued on behalf of any single section of the combatants on either side by way of eVenuatinz this war. CASE FOR THE WAR NON—PROVEN.</p>
        <p>And now I think we have considered all the excuses that serve to explain away the simple facts of the case in South Africa. Most attention has been devoted to the Pan-Africander conspiracy because so much elec- tioneering capital has been made out of it, and to the diplomacy because the original responsibility turns so much upon it. To say the very least, the case for the war on these grounds is—in the old Scotch phrase—" Non Proven." This is a heavy charge at all times and grows heavier as the sciences are dragged in to make the in- struments of instruction more and more deadly. And so those who have to make the best of a bad situation do so very largely by villifying their critics. Well, if we have erred it is in good company. Tories are fond in these matters of sneering at the lesser luminaries on the Liberal side. I notice they fight shy though of the names of some of our greatest living Englishmen opposed to them; men like Mr Bryce, Mr Morley, Mr Herbert Spencer pre-eminent in their knowledge of his- tory, of politics and of science,whose great abilities and achievements have secured them • world wide reputa- tion. Such men as these have exercised enormous influence, and it is not diminished when some insolent political parvenu calls them traitors or pro-Boers. On the contrary we shall be inclined to retort that he is the traitor and the worst enemy this country has had in all this shocking fiasco. Such tu quoques are not edifying and it is better to give Englishmen credit for good intentions all round. But as for the insults levelled at Sir H Campbell Banner- man and others of our leaders,it is as well to remember that in the last great crisis of this kind, the American war of independence, they called Burke and Fox and Chatham " traitors," "Friends of the Americans,' Ememies to the King," " Eoernies of England " and " Emissaries of ttbe enemy." Are they any the worse for it now ? Are there any names in the great roll of English statesmen who occupy a higher niche in the Temple of Fame. But what about the Tories who threw mud at them, and the Tory policy of that day ! ! Where do they stand now? !</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>TERMS.</head>
        <p>TERMS.</p>
        <p>So much for the past. I h-"I-s;c3 left myself but little space to deal with the future, but one word before conclusion on this vexed question of terms. It is argued every day in the week in the Conservative press that terms are out of the question because the Boers insist on independence. Do they ? What about Sir Redvers Buller' ov,rtures, knocked on the head when he was told the only terms we could listen to were unconditonal surrender. What about Lord Kitchener's still more recent proposals ? They are best summarised by Sir 13 Campbell Bannerman in his speech at Ponty- pridd, full of the common sense and sound judgment which have characterised our leader's utterances on the war. I give the following verbatim from his speech in reference to the negotiations between Lord Kitchener and Botha :</p>
        <p>" The military guard was to be replaced by Crown Colony administration, consisting of an executive, with an elective Aasembly to advise the adminis- tration, to be followed by a period of representa tive governinents.Ele would have liked representa- tive government at once ; but seemed satisfied with the condition that the represehtative government was to be put off for a period, and in the meantime there was to be government by an Executive, advised by an Elective Assembly- This was the proposal of the Colonial Secretary —that there should be a nominated official ele- ment in place of this elected assembly in the first stage of the government, and for representative government a representative element to be sub- stituted, thus introducing an additional proba- tionary period. The privilege of self-government was to be conceded, ultimately. "Ultimately' must be read by Lord Salisbury's words, that it would probably no take place for years, or even for generations. Therefore, in place of Lord Kitchener's frank gift of a hostage to the prirf- ciple of self-government, there was a grudging, tardy, conditional promise of a privilege. Which was the method of the statesman here, the sol dier'a or that of the Minister ho described Lard Kitchener' idea as preposterous." _</p>
        <p>Well it is for our statesmen and diplomatists—if we have any left—to settle nice points in arranging terms. WHAT STRIKES PLAIN MAN IS THE ALL-IMPORTANT FACT THAT BOTHA WAS FULLY PREPARED TO NEGOTIATE EVEN THOUGH LORD KITCHENER'S FIRST CONDITION WAS THAT THE BOERS SHOULD YIELD TO THE INEVITABLE AND ACCEPT THE BRITISH FLAG. That sounds little to us perhaps but it was a great deal to them, and would be to English. men if we stood in their shoes. But a certain familiar maxim which says" Put yourself in his place," and a much older precept " Do unto others as ye would the, should do unto you "are much out of fashion just now —in fashionable circles. The party ,of force—sheer unadulterated force, the party whs belirve that might is right—or if not may be made so by a little skilful manipulation, have done and are doing a great deal to makl it difficult for the Boers to accept our common citizenship. Yet it is probable enough that even now, given the right man and the right means a settlement might be achieved ; and I for one do not believe that the majority of responsible householders and decent Englishmen prefer to go on for another sit, twelve or eighteen months in this desper- ate endeavour to neat a small nation to its knees,piling up the agony and adding to the legacies of hate and fury which will mean prolonged repression and costly government when all is over, We are told the Britt sla people will never listen to " unconditional surrender e No they will not, but the remark does not help us to get any " forrarder " for the simple reason that thy demand for unconditional surrender has been just the other way. In the American war, the analogy with which has been vividly shown in Me Methuen's valuable little boot t the same demand for unconditional surrender was made by the Tories of that day. Burke called it " impious "then. It is as impious now, and only helps to nvike still worse a policy which from its sordid isception,and from the days when the Boers were prodded into extremities—and the powers that be thanked heaven for their escape from peace—has been one of those blunders which are worse than crimes. It is a primary object of Liberalism to lessen the mass of misery and suffering in the world. We cannot pretend to render practicable every impracticable scheme for improving hnmanity off the face of the globe, but we can and ought to protest ag 'last any policy which adds so gratuitously and so terribly to the wretchedness and despair of an unfortunate people. . Roylake, July 18th, 1901. isAir early as June. 1899. Lord Virolseley proposed to Lord Lansdowne to seize Delagoa Hay imme4lately, call out the reserves and invade the Transvaal at once, so as to finish the war by November. Private though that was, only too many indications were given to the Boers as time went on to show them what was in the wind. I'LL better shilling's worth on this diffisult South African question cannot be bought.</p>
        <p>Warrington Chamber of Commerce. A meeting of the Chamber was held in the Market Gate Chambers on Monday. Mr C E Parker presi- ded. The President, Vice-President and any other member who can make it convenient to attend were elected as di-legates of the Chamber to the forth- coming autumnal meeting to be held early in Sep- temberat Nottingham. A circular was read from Mr J Lockie explaining a scheme he is promoting with a view to the federation of all the principal organizations of employers and employed through- out the United Kingdom. The object of the federa- tion is "to improve the commercial situation and to strengthen our hands in the ever increasing com- petition with foreign nations." The Secretary was instructed to reply that the Warrington Chamber cordially approves of the objects the proposed , federation has in view. The Secretary read a series of communications from the China League upon the subject of tariff revision in China, and was requested to communicate with the Marquis of Lansdowne in support of the representations of the League. The Secretary was also requested to write to the member, Mr It Pierpoint, asking him whether he could see his way to support a Bill which is being promoted by the Machinery Users' Association, and which has received the approval of the Royal Commission on Local Taxation. At the request of the Australasian Chamber of Commerce which has just been established in London the names of the principal local manufacturers inter- ested in British Australasian trade were forwarded by the secretary. A vote of condolence with the relatives of the late Mr Robert B. Fleming, whose firm have been for many years connected with the Chamber, was unanimously passed.</p>
