      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>EXAMINER; FRIDAY, JULY 26, 1901:</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER; FRIDAY, JULY 26, 1901:</p>
        <p>MARRIAGE OF MISS HILDA GARNETT.</p>
        <p>There was a typical village wedding at Penketh on Thursday last week. The entire village took an in- terest in the proceedings, the school children es- pecially being greatly interested. The occasion was the marriage of Miss Hilda Mary Susannah,youn,g- est daughter of Mr. Robert Garnett, J.P., of Hall Nook, Penketh, and Mr. Carlton Burwood Heal, manager of the Northampton Tanning Company, son of Mr. John Heal, of Finchley, London. Miss Garnett has prominently identified herself with the work of the Wetleyan Church and Sunday School, and is very popular in the village. The time fixed for the ceremony was two o'clock, but long before, that time a con- gregation gathered in the church, and when the proceedings commenced every available seat was occupied. The approaches to the church had been carpeted with crimson felting, and across the roadway, a line of flags fluttered fitfully in the too gentle breeze. The school children lined the approaches, the boys- on one side and the girls on the other. Many of the girls held bunches of flowers in their hands and others greeted the bride and bridegroom with showers of confetti as they passed. The church had been decorated with a choice array of plants and flowers brought from Hall Nook. Within the communion rails some beautiful hy- drangea and maidenhair ferns were displayed and the steps leading to the choir gallery were lined with white ribbons and bunches of lilies and asters. The pretty dresses of the wedding party and the guests considerably heightened the effect of the scene, the whole presenting a pleasing ensemble. The officiating minister was the Rev John Gunnell, resident minister. The choir of which the bride was a member, was in attendance and led in the singh.g of the hymns "The Voice that breathed o'er Eden," "God be merciful unto us," and " Oh, Perfect Love." Mr. Wigfield presided at the organ, and during the assembling of the guests played several appropriate marches, and at the close,Mendelssohn's "Wedding March."</p>
        <p>The bride wasgiven away by her father. She was charmingly attired in a dress of crepe de chin trimmed with silk lace flounces, looped up with sprays of orange blossom, with court train of brocaded satin and trimmed at the base with a lovers knot of lace and a horse shoe of orange blossona.The train was carried by Ruth Parker and Maisie Posnett. The bride wore a jewelled necklet specially designed,the gift of the bridegroom. She carried a lovely bouquet composed of orchids,whito heather, niphetos roses and lilies of the valley. She was accompanied by six little nieces as brides- maids—Miss Mollie Parker, Miss Ruth Parker, Miss Rosamund Parker,Miss Rosalie Garnett,Miss Maisie Posnett and Miss Madeline Doeg. They were prettily attired in dresses of pale blue liberty satin trimmed with ecru lace, with picture hats of ecru straw, trimmed with chiffon and forget-me- nots. They each wore a forget-me-not brooch of turquoise and pearls' gitts of the bridegroom. They carried shower bouquets of sweet peas and gladioia,also gifts of the bridegroom. The duties of best man were satisfactorily discharged by Mr Stocks,of Kirkcaldy, cousin of the bridegroom. A reception, at which over 150 guests were present was held from 3to 6 atHall Nook. Refreshments were served on the lawn where a marque had been erected. Here and there were a number of large Japanese umbyellas under the shades of which sat small Arties enjoying a tete-a-tete conversation and partaking of light refreshments, which were handed round, The catering was in the hands of Mrs J Hayward, of the Wininarleigh CafB, and gave every satisfaction. She also supplied the wedding Photographs of several groups were taken by Mr Birtles, Sankey-street. At 4 30 the bride and bridgroom left for the honeymoon, which is to be spent in Switzerland. They took their de- parture amid the hearty good wishes and cheers of the guests. The bride's travelling dress was of green muslin trimmed with lace, and travelling coat of fawn cloth, trimmed with lace and velvet. Numerous and costly presents have been received, including three presentations. The bride received ' a tea and coffee service in white and gold china from the teachers of the Penketh Day and Sunday Schools and Choir • a case of Coronation spoons from the Cycling Choir; of which the bride was secretary. • The bridegroom received a cut glass salad bowl and salver from his employees of the Northampton Tanning Company.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>CONSTRUCTION OF THE CORPORATION TRAMWAYS.</head>
        <p>THE CONSTRUCTION OF THE CORPORATION TRAMWAYS.</p>
        <p>Mr EWART, the contractor for the laying of the per- manent way of the Warrington Corporation Electric Tramways is making satisfactory progress with the work now that he has been able to obtain a delivery of the steel rails. Excavations are taking place at four d:ff Tent points on the route, viz Bridge-street linutsford-road, Liverpool-road and Bank Quay I Bridge. bed of concrete has been laid in some instances and at Bank Quay Bridge the rai's have been fixed in position. We understand that the Tramways Committee have decided to alter the existing buildings at the Gas Works in Mersey-street and utilise them for a car sh-d. Tois course will avoid the expenditure of a considers ole sum of money which I would have been incurred in the erection of a new building, and it is thought that some alteration of the present retort houses will adapt them for the purpose. On Thursday,the members of the I nommittee paid a visit to the works of Messrs Dick, Kerr and Company, Preston, and inspected the cars in the course of construction. It is expected that the Committee will come to a decision as lo the t7pe lof car to be adopted in Warrington. at an early date. It may be stated that Mr Ewart is under con- tract to complete his portion of the work within thirty weeks.</p>
      </div>
