      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>DANGERS OF HIGH DIVING.</head>
        <p>THE DANGERS OF HIGH DIVING.</p>
        <p>A well-illuatrated article on " High Diving" is a prominent feature in the August number of the' Wind- sor Astsgaz'ne." Of the daagers attributed to this branch of the sport, the writer says : " A good diver may occasionally lose his balance, but being generally cool- headed he ean regain it, or at any rate sufficiently so to prevent injury to himself, for if he came from a great height flat on to his stomach, he would, we are told, be torn open, most probably with fatal results. This is anatomically possible, I suppose, but I'm inclined to think it would prove otherwise, should it ever happen. There is no actual case on record, so far as my inquiries have teen made amongst medical and diving authori- ties. Men sometimes go deaf through not sufficiently protecting their face and head. Many find it difficult to drop the head sufficiently to get it well bstween the arms ; the result is, they get some tremendous blows on the head, and if the water is struck a bit sideways, the ear gets most of the concussion, and the result will not improbably be a rupture of the membrane of the tympanum. A little medicated wool, dipped in Oil and placed in the ears is said to be a preventive to such an accident, but is disregarded by most experts, When diving from a height one would naturally expect to experience the sensation of falling known to us only Se dreams, but no divers I have yet met have ever ex- perienced that sensation—the dive is over too Soon. two pleasures of high diving may be summed pn w words—exhilarating and fascinating. When once an ordinary diver has learned to take a header, hew dis- dainful he is about entering the water in any ethey manner! And the higher the dive, the keener is the' sense of exultation in the diver. Watch him, and count I hew many times he will climb a tedious height bare- I footed, just for the sake of his dive. Most high divers will tell you that the sertqation is the nearest approach they can imagine to eying. You must hays perfrct control over yourself or you will get nervous and lose rue head. Confidence in oneself and the proper amount of practice will make each SUCC.6SiVO part of the dive more or lees mechanical. The tension experienced br some div*rs is over when they spring :with others rm: until they enter the water. But this is chiefly a M itter of temperament, for others feel no tension what. ever, and anpreciate pech part,the filling the lungs with ai•, the spring, the falling zier-e:gh spacn, the bresk,and tb(e entering and cleaving of the water,"</p>
        <p>Au extraordinary post office blur:der, which will :AIM humorous complications. has completely bewilder- ed the French Ministry of Agriculture. It appears that the voluminous packet containing the examination papers of candidates for the grade of agrieultwal engin- e" was wAted on the morning of the 14t1) of July. Since then they have been missing, although every seureh and inquiry has been made. It would (sirs the Paris correspondent of the " Daily Chronicle ") he an toheard-ot breach of red-tapism to order a second exaraination,and there seems to he no practical way out of tbe difficulty.</p>
      </div>
