      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>irons</head>
        <p>irons</p>
        <p>letters tu the atizt..:r.</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS.</p>
        <p>Au: :r of letters intended for publication must lo aii c4.Ee send their names and addresse3 to the _ _</p>
        <p>Matter v intended for publication must be written en one side only of the paper and sent in, if possible tot later than Thursday. Correspondents are strongly advised to write briefly. A short letter has much greater chance of ir.sertion than a long one. We cannot under ordinary circumstarcf,s under- take to return the manuscripts which are not used. %Vow,</p>
        <p>Magistrates and the Vaccination Law. Sir.—Previous to the passing of the Vaccination Act of 1898 magistrates never wearied of telling vaccination defaulters who were summoned before them that they (the magistrates) had nothing to do with the goodness or badness of the law, but had to administer it as they found it. But now the law—which purports to provide a means of escape from the vaccination of their children for those parents who conscientiously object to it—being out of harmony with the bias in which many magis- trates are steeped they appear to think their duty is not the administration of the law but the bantering with a lot of irrelevant matter of those who attempt to avail themselves of its provisions. One amongst many such instances occurred the other day when the following little colloquy took place consequent upon an applica- tion for a certificate to exempt from vaccination being made at the Warrinaton Borough Police Court, Alder- man James Fairclough being In the chair. The applicant stated that he had a conscientious objection, and added that he had lost one child and another was suffering owing to vaccination. The Cnairman : How many deaths have there been in Glasgow? Applicant : I cannot answer that. The Chairman: You will let all the people of War- rington die for the sake at your one child. Applicant said he bad, however, a conscientious obj^c- tion. The Chairman: I have a conscientious objection to granting it. The Chairman then left the bench. Now, would it not be instructive to learn what the tnmter of deaths in Glasgow has to do with the granting of an exemption'certificate? Most surely the administra- tion of the law is not dependent on the number of deaths in Glasgow. Nor is the intention, of this reference to Glasgow altogether clear. If it was intended to point Out the danger of neglecting or the safety in accepting vaccination, then nothing could be more eff,ctaally self- destructive, for if vaccination possessed any value as a preventive of smallpox there would have been no small- pox deaths nor cases in Glasgow, the best vaccination city in the world. Again, if vaccination safeguards against smallpox how is it possible for one unvaccinated child to endanger the lives of all the people in Warring- ton all of whom are at liberty to be vaccinated and thus protected. Although this palpably illogical argument has been exposed times out of number the vaccination zsalots still continue to use it with all their wonted zest without, apparently, seeing its utter absurdity. nurs faithfully, Bradbrd-on-Avon, July 24th, 1901.</p>
        <p>J. WEST.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>LATEST NEWS.</head>
        <p>LATEST NEWS.</p>
        <p>CHINA.</p>
        <p>Shot for Insulting the English.</p>
        <p>Telegraphing on Wednesday the Rome corres- pondent of the " Morning Leader" remarks : Letters received in Istria from China state that an Istrian seaman named Meoni, wfth the Austrian contingent, was shot for insulting the English. Knutsford Murder Trial. The trial of Parrott, at Chester Assizes, is fixed fer to-day.</p>
        <p>Disaster on the Matterhorn.</p>
        <p>The Fate of English Tourists</p>
        <p>Uncertain.</p>
        <p>L&amp;L'SANNE, TIIIIRSDAY.</p>
        <p>News has been received here that at two o'clock on the afternoon of the 23rd three English tourists (a gentleman and two ladies), who were making the ascent of the Matterhorn, were, together with a guide, precipitated down the southern slope of the mountain in Italian territory. There are no further particulars yet to hand of the accident. A</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>£5,000 Derby Sweepstake.</head>
        <p>£5,000 Derby Sweepstake.</p>
        <p>Important Trial in London.</p>
        <p>On Thursday, at the Central Criminal Court, London, Joseph Stoddart, 10, Red Linn Court,Lon- 'don, surrendered to his bail on a charge of unlaw- fully publishing on May 17 and other dates pro- posals to "sell chances in a lottery, to wit a lottery called '£5,000 Derby Sweepstake,' to be drawn in a foreign country,to wit,the kingdom of Holland." The case was tried by Mr Justice Wills and a jury. The defendant was also charged with conspiring with one Davis ira conducting a lottery on the Derby, and alsc with publishing an advertisement of chances in a lottery. Prisoner pleaded guilty. In passing sentence Mr Justice Wills said be looked upon it as a deliberate case of law-breaking and for the purpose of putting money into prisoner's own pocket by carrying out this transaction. He thought this could not be met by a fine alone. It was desirable that -people who conducted themselves as the prisoner bad done in this matter should suffer not Merely the discredit of being subjected to the term of "rogue and vagabond" but should suffer the indignity of imprisonment. He could not shut his eyes co what was, stated in the previous case that came before the court of the enormous profits which had been made by the prisoner and a fine of fifty pounds would be absolutely inadequate. The prisoner would:therefore have to:go to gaol fer three Months, and pay a fine of £5O.</p>
        <p>The prisoner,af ter sentenco had been pronounced. asked for his s!lk hat,which was banded to him,and ' having shaken hands with a friend in the well of the court he was removed belo iv in the custody of the officers of the court.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>WAR.</head>
        <p>THE WAR.</p>
        <p>LATEST TELEGRAMS.</p>
        <p>Fighting in Cape Colony.</p>
        <p>-- The Enemy Driven Northwards.</p>
        <p>UITENHAUE. THURSDAY.</p>
        <p>The local Jansenville and Willowmore forces, under Captain Hodson, attacked the Boers oa the border of this district. The engagement which followed resulted in the enemy being driven north- wards. The Boers had one man wounded. There,, were no casualties on our side.</p>
      </div>
