      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>TERRORS OF A MINER'S LIFE.</head>
        <p>THE TERRORS OF A MINER'S LIFE.</p>
        <p>In the August number of the "Windsor Magaz;ne" there appears a thrilling article describing the life of a Cornish miner. The following extract will show how full of hardship and terror is the existence of these brave men. "In August of 1883 a terrible fatality occurred at Wheal Agar. There were twelve men in the gig, and a thirteenth, contrary to regulation, had clambered on to the roof, and was standing there, holding on by the cable. The men had done their work and were going I home. It is reported that according to general custom, ! they sang hymns as they ascended, Oa reaching the surface the man on the roof stepped off and turned round to watch the issue of his mates. But where the ,gig bad been but a second previously was now nothing but as gaping void. The rope had snapped,and the gig with its living freight, had been dashed to the botom of the shaft. Not one of the poor bodies could be recog- nised in the mass of human debris that was br lught op from below. On another occasion, when the cable parted and a headlong rush to death had begun, the lives of the men were saved in such a way.that one can- not wonder at the belief of the miners that there was miraculous intervention on their behalf. The rush of the freighted gig flicked the loose cable attached to it like a whip, so that it lashed around an upwright timber that came within its reach. The steel strands cut into , the timber until it was nearly severed—nearly, but not quite. The cable jammed, the gig was arrested in its plunge, and the men were saved. Other such miracu- -1 i bus escapes are on record. There is living n Camborn to-day a miner named Bennetts who, with five other men in the cage at Tincroft mine. fell 200 fathoms, at which depth a gate, closed over a depth of sixty feet of water, arrested them. The gig with its freight weighed over a ton. Yet only one man was injured. At B3taltack Mine,in IB76,nine men were killed in a similar accident. At Dolcoath.in May, 1892,there was another miraculous escape. The shaft of the fly-wheel broke, and the gig containing four miners, fell 330 feet sheer fall, but was jammed at the sharp turn which the shaft takes from perpendicular to underlie, and so stopped, 300 feet from certain destruction. The danger of 'runs '—that is to say, the downfall of:tons of rock and rubbish which the timbering has been insufficient to support—is what, perhaps, is most feared by the miners. The run' is so sudden that,when men are at work within the ceurse of it,there is rarely any escape. There was a terrible acci- dent of this kind in Dolcoath Mine. 'I had not been sawing more than three minutes,' said the one survivor, ' Richard Davies, when I heard some timber cracking. and then came a tremendous deafening rush of stuff which knocked me eight or nine feet under the levellers at She bottom of the level. I was struck on the head and legs. My partners aimed to run, but they must hay° been knocked down where they stood and buried. Dick James called out to Charley White, "Look out, Charley ! " The noise was so terrific I was unable to hear any more. The downfall was of thousands of tons of rock, so that the gallery was choked with stuff for a distance of tWenty-eight yards. The displacement of air by this fall was so great that a man named Ned Tre:garthen, who was standlng many yards away. Wag stripped naked. Other men were dashed down and cut and bruised. A stationary coach, or iron trolley, was bi.,vrn di=tarce of twenty feet and unturned. Men in a level 400 feet above felt the earth shake beneath them. The noise wasdeafening. It was as the simultaneous discharge of thousands of cannon. With such force were the granite rocks ground together that firs flashed. And in the midst of th‘s were eight men, only one of whom, Davies, lived to tell the talc."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>EXAMINER FRIDAY, JULY 26, 1901</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER FRIDAY, JULY 26, 1901</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON. WARRINGTONTIO CLEGRY INSTITU- I NS. POLICE CASE. At the Newton Police Court, on Tuesday morn- ing, before Mr T J Gillespie, William Lee was charged with being drunk the preVious night, and in default of paying a fine of 103 and 'c3sts was sent to gaol for 14 days. BOWLING HANDICAP AT BARLESTOWN. A handicap at bowls was played on the Institute Club ground on Saturday for Mr E T Evans' prize of £l, and the entrance fees. There were 32 entries and the day being fine there was some good bowl- ing. The first prize was won by P Talbot, 2nd J Savage, and .3rd A Lomax. MUSICAL SUCCESS. At the recent examination held by the London College of Music Liverpool Centre, Miss Lilly Green pupil of Mr C H Dawson L.V.C.M. has gained the Diploma of associate and is therefi re allowed to wear the College dress of cap and 43W11 and to append the letters A.L.C..M. to her li .me. It may be added that she is only 15 years'of age. BRITISH AND FOREIGN BIBLE SOCIETY. In connection with the above special services were held in St. John's Church, Earlestown on Sun- day morning and evening. On both occasions the preacher was the Rev H Price and the same gentle- man addressed the Men's Bible Class in the after- noon. The services were well attended and col- lections in aid of the Society, were made.</p>
        <p>RAYDOCK COLLIERS AT BLACKPOOL. The annual excursion promoted by Messrs Evans and Ca., of Haydock for the benefit of their em- ployees took plena on Saturday, to Blackpo)1. Three special trains were requisitioned to convey the colliers their wives and families; over 2,000 being transplrted from Haydack to Blackpool in the space cot a little over two hours. They were favoured with beautiful weather, and the outing was thor3ughly enjoyed without mishap. The re- turn journey was commenced at different hours, the whole being completed before midnight. VIADUCT INSTITUTE TECHNICAL INSTRUCTION CraassES.</p>
        <p>The following results of the examinations in Physiography and Steam held in connection with ' the above classes, have COMO to hand :—Physio- graphy : Lily Battersby and Edith Bunting, A 2nd; Margaret Evans, Annie Kenny, Percy Laith- waite, Mary Longworth, Samuel SAdeleg, E Ist; Annie T Brodie, Bella Hewitt, Florence Kenning, Lilian Longwortb, Edith D Lyons, Lucy Makeham, Annie Massey, Alice Smith, Florence J Welsby. Steam :—Frank Gee, Joseph Stringer, E Ist; Frank Emerton, E 2nd. Applied Mechanics :—William Bates, E 2nd. _</p>
        <p>TE iVATEIL DIFFICULTY AT EATILEsTowN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>Since we referred to this subject last week, there has been a decided and welcome improvement in the supply of water. The matter bad become most serious, and no clear way out of the difficulty I appearing, a special meetinv of the Water Commit- tee of the District Council was held on Friday after- noon last. There was some discussion, and it was decided to ask the L and N.-W. Railway Company to again allow their main to be connected with the, Council's main in order to ensure a sufficient sup- ply of water during the drought. Mr F W Webb, of Crewe, was communicated with, and on Saturday a message was received from that gentleman sanc- tioning the connection of the mains, The wat sr I from the Company's main was not turned on, bow ever. until Monday evening. Consequently the I tswn has since then been in possession of a con- tinuous and plentiful supply. PRESENTATION TO A VIADUCT WORKS FOREMAN. SBNIr Nathan Slinn,who for 43 years has been in the employ of the London and North-Western Railway Company in the wheelss eights department of their waggon works at Earlestown, 31 of which he had officiated as foreman, has retired OR a well-earned pension, and will shortly leave the "district to take up his residence near Hereford. When it became known that he was retiring a movem nt was set on foot amongst the wheelwrights, by whom he waS highly esteemed and respected, for the purpose of presenting him with some tangible memento of the regard in which be was held. The matter was spiritedly taken up by the workmen and it ex- tended to the other foremen in the works, and the office staff. Consequently a good sum was obtained, and it was decided to present Mr Slinn with a gold watch, and Mrs Shinn with a silver hot water jug. The watch was supplied by Messrs Russells of Liverpool, and bore the inscrip- tion "Presented to Mr Nathan Slinn by his fellow workers, as a token of respect on his retirement after 43 years service, 34 of which he has been our foreman, and greatly respected by the men under his charge. July 12th, 1901." On the water jug was the' inscription: Presented to Mrs Slinn on her husband's retirement July 12th, 1901." On Saturday evening, a large number of the subscribers to the presentation fund, mustered at the OdHellows Arms, Earlestown for the purpose of making the presentation. About 50 persons sat down to a substantial and well-served tea, to which ample justice was done. After the cloth had been temoved, Mr Robert Wright presided and said that as workmen they were parting with one of the best possible foreman they could have had. Mr John MOB 3 another old employe made the presentation remarking that Mr Shun had been in the, capacity of a general who had served the London and North- Western Railway Company faithfully and success- fully for the past 31 years, and he had led his forces with tact, thoughtfulness and ability. The com- pany were losing a good servant, and the men a good foreman. Mr Slims's retirement was well earned and it was a just reward for long, faithful, and honourable service. He hoped the ticking of tho watch would remind Mr Slinn of the ticking of the hearts of so many friends he had left behind in Earlestown. (Cheers.) He also asked Mrs Slinn's acceptance of the silver water jug as a little present to remind her of the happy days! spent in Earlestown. (Applause.) Mr Shun replied in suitable term aon behalf of himself and his wife. Councillor Gilmour proposed the toast of the " Wheelwrights," and spoke in eulogistic terms of the work of Mr Shinn, expressing the hope that his son, Mr George Slim], who had succeed to the posi- tion of foreman, would follow in the steps of his I father. (Applause.) Mr 0 Slinn acknowledged the toast. Songs, &amp;c, were given at intervals by Messrs J Edwards, Cope, Isherwood, &amp;c. and a recitation by Mr W Sutton, a very pleasant evening being spent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>NOTES BY THE WAY AT EARLESTOWN• [BY A CORIiESPONDENT.]</head>
        <p>NOTES BY THE WAY AT EARLESTOWN• [BY A CORIiESPONDENT.]</p>
        <p>CRICKET.</p>
        <p>Earlestown again went down on Saturday, this time before an eleven from the Liverpool club, and not a very strong eleven. They certainly ought to have won, seeing that Liverpool were all out for 102, whereas Earlestown had put on nearly 70 runs for the loss of only four wickets. Alderson resumed operations with the ball,af .:er a two or three weeks rest from an injury, and no douht he was mainly responsible for the smallnsss of the Liverpool I score. No exrmse can be off ired for the falling away of the Earlestown team after the fall of the fourth wicket.</p>
        <p>The second eleven visited the Bolton Grammer School (old boys) and succeeded in gaining the victory by 93 runs to 85. lAgh made a useful 30 and Harrison and Morse were successful in the bowling.</p>
        <p>Newton ban an unexpectel defeat on Saturday on their own ground, by Stanley, The defeat is hard to explain but Smnley were evidently the better team on form. The alores were Newton 85, Stanley 167. Storer (21),Iloish (18) and C Hindley (15), were the chief Bcores for Newton.</p>
        <p>The second eleven visited Stanley and were ignominously defeated. Newton's score was °9, and Stanley 68. They complained bitterly of the unsportsmanlike conduct of the Stanley spectators and it is quite on the cards that the fixtures with Stanley will be dropped.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>FOOTBALL. Northern Rugby Union. Lancashire Senior Competition.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL. Northern Rugby Union. Lancashire Senior Competition.</p>
        <p>A meeting of this body was held in Mainothea blstecroir.n Tuesday evening, Mr UWalsh (Stockport) n h After helping the statements of Mr Marsh, the late Secretary of the T7lt4eeley Club, it was decided to with- draw the invitation given to that club on July sth 0 join the Lancashire Competition, a position being thus vacant-. The Radcliffe Club 0735 etec'-ed to membership A resolution was passed to the effect that the Competi- ion should not consist of rl3-e than twelve clubs. W. T. WILLIAMS' SALE is now proceo3! See Windows for Bargains In MILLINERY. MANT COATS, DRESSES. 87.0119 XS &amp;C.—Sankey-street. L Legh-street, Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>A _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>Service at the Parish Church.</head>
        <p>Service at the Parish Church.</p>
        <p>A service was held in the Parish Church at norm The congregation included the students of the Clergy Dauglr.ers' School. The preacher fee the occasion was the Rev W L Paige Cox, 51. A , (vicar of Rock Ferry and Rural Dan.) ;le based his discourse on the second chapter of Acts, first verso " They were all with one accord in one place." They were met that day,he said. as representatives of three different dioces,..s, and they felt it was go id to meet under such con- ditions. They would observe that there bad been a wholesome revival of diocesan life durin7 the last half century but there was a Slight clan. ger of their opinions and methods of church work be- comine too exclusively diocesan, as formerly they were too pArochini. Thep were not: members of one diocev‘ only but of the National Church. Their Warrington in. stitutions had been not much more than a name to those who lived a distance from Warrington but those who attended the anniversary had their interest quickened. There were no persons who had a greater claim on the kindly thought of church people than the wives and children of the clergy, especi shy in these days when the incomes of many of the clergy had been so much diminished. If there sac one thing they ought to for ward to the best of their ability it was the education of the children of the clergy on thoroughlytChristian and Foiled lines, What the Church gave in this way he believed she most truly gave to the Lord and got back with Interest. The ministry was always well recruited through the ranks of the clergy, and some of the noblest women workers of the Church, both at home and abroad, had been trained in the rectories and vicarages. A collection was taken on behalf of the inatitutions. THE LUNCHEON. THE QUESTION OF EXTENSION.</p>
        <p>Luncheon was subsequently served in the Clergy Daughters School. Mr Beresford V. Melville, M.P.. presided. The first toast was that of " Church and King," and it was proposed by the PRESIDENT. Such a toast, he said, opened up a very large constitutional question with which most of them were intim ttely con- nected. It might open such a subject as the Coronation Oath and subjects of even greater importance than that. Ile asked them to believe that the present reigning sovereign had at heart thn maintenance of the church and her authority, and also those great institutions and charities which were connected with the church's work. The toast was then drunk.</p>
        <p>The PHISSIDHNT,III proposing the toast of the "BishopS and Clergy of the Three Dioceses," Said he was glad to think that in this charitable work the clergy and laity were drawing perhaps more closely together and recog- nising their duties and responsibilities. They were agreed, he said, about the necessity of repelling all attacks upon their national Church. As laymen they were anxious to support their Church and to do every. thing they could to keep it pure and absolutely tsar- MOniCILI9, but they were sometimes hindered by certain things which took place in the church itself. With the clergy and laymen working harmoniously together he believed the Church would march triumphantly through any crisis through which it may be called to pass. The BISHOP of Chester, in responding, read a letter of apology from the Bishop of Liverpool, in which he alluded to the loss which the Rev EJ Willcocks, of the Warrington Grammar School,had sustained in the death of his daughter, and he was sure they would all sym- pathise with that gentleman. The speaker said they all knew the Bishop of Liverpool had already identified himself very markedly in the welfare of all the Warring- ton institutions (hear, hear.) The Bishop proceeded to refer to the worth of these old diocesan institutions at Warrington. They were moulding these young lives in character. These were days when everybody was start— ing a new society with the best instincts and intentions. If he were going to writelais own epitaph he should be inclined to put on his tomb the statement that he had done his very best to prevent the establishment of new societies and ins:itutions, as he was (pita cettain they would damage the good old institutions, which were apt to bn pet in the shade, Whatever they did towards the new let them stand firmly .and sympathetically by the old.</p>
        <p>!dr Jogsrsosr proposed the toast of "The Diocesan Institutions of Warrington," and spoke of the progress of thetsehool under the direction of Miss K-nnedy. He looked for a very brilliant future for the school. The introduction of scholarships would enable them to assist any student who might be worthy of a university career. The DEAN OF MANCHESTER responded to the toast. It was perfectly clear that further ex Tinsion was necessary at Warrington, if they as Churchmen were to do their duty as educationalists, either C' those who were to be the elementary teachers of the future by training them in their training colleges, or to those who had been entrusted to their care in the Clergy Daughters' School. Both institutions—the Training College and the Clergy Daughters' School—were being used to the utmost capacity and he felt that one would have to give way to the other in order to make room for the necessary ex- pansion. It was inadvisable for them to remove the Training College for he wai aivised by the Duke of Devonshire that if they decided to remove they would not only be compelled to rebuild a college providing accomm-dation for resident students but also for day students. The problem would b to find new accommo- dation for the Clergy Daughters' School, and if he had £lO,OOO at his disposal he would instantly solve the problem. He hoped that at an early date they would be able to solve this problem of the necessary expansion of the institutions he had named. (Applause-) Mr FULLAGAR proposed a vote of thanks to the preacher, the Rev W I, Paige Cox, and the President's 'services were fittingly acknowledged on the proposition of the Rev Canon Gore.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>CONCERT AND PRIZE DISTRIBITTION,</head>
        <p>CONCERT AND PRIZE DISTRIBITTION,</p>
        <p>Later in the day.there was a concert and prize-giving in connection with the Clergy Dsughters School. Mr meiville, M.P., presided. An exc,llent programme of vocd and instrumental music W3B given by the students in alm•soner which reflected the highest crEdit upon Miss Kennedy and her staff. The programme concluded by the singing by request of the" St Elphin's school song.'! prizen were distrtbuted by the President as follows :</p>
        <p>EXHIBITION 3 AWARDED BY THE COMMITTEE.—Laura Willington, Hilda Ash, Katherine Thatcher. Charlotte Garnett; Dorothy Temperley, Constance Collinson, Nes% Thomas. _ _ _</p>
        <p>.... PRIZES FOR MUSIC (Presented by R H Joyuson. Esq.)— Ellen Morgan (Higher Division). Emilie d'Auquier (Lower Division), Dorothy Bradford (Elementary Division). PRIzE FOR DRAWING (Presented by R H Joy:non, Esq,)— Enid Evans PRIZES FOR WORK SPECIALLY COMMENDED BY THE MISTRFSSES or THE Scitoot. (Presented by B H Joynson, Esq )—Marion Fletcher, Catherine Kennedy. PRiZEs FOR NEEDLEwoRK (presented by Mrs Belmont.; First Prize, .11.1r1,l Clayton. Commended : Marion Flet- cher, Eiblin O'Brien. Second Prize. Grady&amp; Wilson. Corn- mended: Grace Biakelock, Doris Reed.</p>
        <p>AWARDS TO PREFECTS FOR GOOD ADMINISTRATION IN THE SCHOOL. (Presented by It 11 Joynson. Esq.)—Aruca Davin- ney (Honour award), Laura Willington, (Honour award), Annie Jones (Honour award) Hilda Ash, Katherine That- cher, Catherine Adatnson. Charlotte Garnett. • AWARDS TO BIONITRESSES FOR THEIR ASSISTANCE IN THE MAINTENANCE OF GOOD ORDER IN THE SCHOOL.—DOrOtheS. Trott (Honour award), Annie L Farmer. Gwendoline Phillips, I Maud Reed, Ellen Morgan, Muriel Clayton, Ida Schofield, Gladys Temperley. Mary Wright. Constance Collinson. Agnes Coultnn, Marion Hiekling. Margaret Percival. AWARDS FYR GOOD OONDUCT.—Ida Schofield, Dorothea Trott. Mary Wright. Constance Collinson, Agnes Coulton, Ruth Garnett, Crace Harris, Catherine Kennedy, Margaret Percival, Isabel Wllcockson, Agnes Holden.</p>
        <p>CERTIFICATES OF THE ASsOCIA.TED BOARD OF THE ROYAL ACADEMY AND ROYAL COLLEGE OF MDSIC —Higher Division: Ellen Morgan and Muriel Clayton. Plano'orte distinction. Lower Division: Emilie d'Anquier, Pianoforte distinction ; Riblin O'Brien, Marion Fletcher, Grace Harris, Pianoforte Pass. Elementary Division : Dorothy Bradford. Frances Harris, Doris Reed, Gladys Bradford, Catherine Kennedy, Pianoforte Pais; Joyos Gardiner, Violin Pass.</p>
        <p>CERTIFICATES OF THR ROYAL DRAWMEI SOCIETT.—Ron- ours : Division iv.. Maud Reed; 111., Emilie d'Anquler ; Grace Harris; 111., Dorothy Harris; lit. Dorothe % Trott ; 11., Dorothy Hodges; li., Gertrude Peters; 1.. Enid Evans ; 1., Mabel Hodges. Passes: Division v., Marion Fletcher ; v., Dorothy Temperley ; iv., Cassendra Colston ; iii„ Edith Farmer ; iii.. Catherine Kennedy; 11.. Gwendolen Birch Jones; It.. Isabel Garnett ;1., Eleanor Blakelock ; i , Grace Blakelock ; 1., Violet Greenall ; 1., Frances Harris.</p>
        <p>At the close, the Dean of Manchester proposed a vote of thanks to the President. The Rev Canon Penrhyn sec)nded and the proposition met with the hearty approval of all present.</p>
        <p>Volunteers and the Heat.</p>
        <p>Colonel J C Ridgway, writing from Porham Down Camp, Ludgershall, to the " Liverpool Post " says : "In your rem irks in Saturday's paper on the great heat you state that the lst V.S. Suith Lancashire Battalion had over twenty cases of heat prostration in hospital. This, lam happy to say, is ex iggerated. and if not contradicted might Cause anxiety to the' wives sad relations of the men. I have made careful itcoiries, and find that up to Saturday's parade only one di so was in hospital from my battalion, and during the we only six men fell out, and after a short rest were able to rej)in the battalion again. The heat is great, but at times there is a breeze,and to-day a bri4x one, which tempers the hat considerably. We drill in shirt sleevPs, and in the afternoons go easy. We lost one horse, but not throught heat, which probably give rise to the rumour that a man had died," .</p>
        <p>Prevention of Cruelty to Children. " The Child's Guardian '' the official organ of the National Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Chitdren " states that the number of Cases proved in the Warrington Police Court "(lifting the last recorded montn " was as follows :—Six persons ; offences against 15 children, five months' hard labour; two adjourned for better behaviour.</p>
        <p>ASSINETTES Alqn MAILCARTS In latest Art Shades —7. S. GIRLIND 51, 53 and 55,11ridge.at. Warrington,—Advt</p>
        <p>BEST BOOTS FOR ALL.-1/ND-ER THE CLO-CK.—Ad</p>
        <p>Port Sanitary Authority. The Bill constituting a port sanitary authority for Manchester and the districts along the water- way of the Ship Canal came before the Rouse of Lords Committee on Thursday. The only opposi- tion came from the Warrington and Widnes Cor- prrations, both of whom desired to be left out of any share in the responsibilities of the board. The committee coafirmed the order.</p>
        <p>BOWLING.</p>
        <p>The Inter-County Match. Lancashire and Cheshire v. Yorkshire.</p>
        <p>At Di isbury on S Iturday. The York3hire team were younger men than Lancashire and Cheshire, and were by far the keener players, pitching their jacks cc ,rever an opening presented, and so kept our men from their favourite places. Leigh Railway representatives played best for us, H Harrison gaining the medal for the best average in the two matches. Opinion prevails that a much stronger team could have been raised for Lanca- shire and Cheshire. The grern was very quick, and, as at Mirfield, the Yorkshiremen were more at home on its glassy surface than our men. Scores :</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE. YORKSHIRE, Aid. J J Meakin, president 12 J W Pickles, president.. 21 J W 0 Coombs, vice- E Hargreaves, vice-presi- president 9 dent 2l J Isherwood, Winans 3 Isherwood, Leigh l7 S Ramsden, Clifton 2l H Harrison, Leigh Railway 21 W Peel, Waterl 0 H Bennett, Newton-le-W's 16 J West,Tay-lor Mil 2l 3 Williams, Warrington .., 21 J Lowe, Cradock ll H Hopewell, Pendleton l4 A Verity, Dewsbury ......21 3 Williamson, Newton P Fielding, Broughton 2l W Hirst, Cleckheaton ... 10 J Whitehead, Pendleton E Baker, Tyldesley W W Wells. Didsbnry 2l H Squire, clifton 2O A Twiss, Stretford Ci. Drinkwater, Stretford... 15 H Savory, Spen Victoria 21 J F Wells. Dlcisbury........ 21 E Eastwood, Clifton C Worsencroft, Didsbury... 21 WH Byrom,Hudderslield 13 R Williamson, Didsbury ... 21 S Berry, Primrose Hill lB Ci Jagger, Hollinwood A J Jones, Warrington l7 J Stead, Spen Victoria ... 21 A Boone, Leigh Railway W Wood, Warringto a F Berry. Warrington l3 T Hirst, Cleckheaton 2l 3 C Howe, Didsbury ...... .. 18 A Roebuck, Taylor Hill 2l J Harrison, Leigh Ely ...... 21 H Fleetwood, Mirfield l6 J Stewart, Stretford ...... ... 12 W H Roberts,Cleckheaton 21</p>
        <p>450 479 Messrs. Crosfield's •Employes Annual Main. This main took place on the Cemetery Hotel green on Saturday. Na fewer than 77 entered and as many of the workmen .re excellent bowlers, some exciting games were witnessed. The games were 13 up, and whilst the serni-finalists created great interest, tha two finalists provided the " match "of the afternoon. The oppo- nents were T Rigby and A Warburton, the latter receiv- ing six start. At the end of the third turn the score was Rigby, 5; Warburton 7. After the next two the saora stool R;gby, 9 • Warburton, 7. The last three resulted as follows:—Rigby, 12; Warburton 9. Hisby, 12; War- burton, 10. Rigby, 12; Warburton, 12, and then Warburton won the last peel. The Mayoress (Mrs H Roberts) distributed the prizes. She was accompanied by Miss Raberts, Miss Crosfield, Miss Mackie and Mr Arthur Crosfield.</p>
        <p>Mr J WILLETT, who had carried out the arrangements in connection with the main, said it was one of the pleasantest mains they had ever had. It was a great pleasure to be honoured with the presence of the Mayoress. They were extremely sorry that the Mayor could not be present. Obtaining subscriptions for the prizes had been one of the _easiest things in the world. Every director and manager in the firm had. subscribed something. That was very encouraging to the working men. He had much pleasure in asking the Mayoress to distribute the prizes. The MAYORESS then presented the prizss as follows:- 1, fnarhle clock, A Warburton ; 2, oak timepiece, T Rigby; 3, cruet, J Hill; 4,4Eelery dish, J D ivies; 5 and 6, cases of c Irvers, P Wright and W E Clew! ; 7 and 8, half-dozen knives forks and carvers (each), H Lawton, and I Wilcox; 9, 10,11 and 12,counterpanes. A Griffiths, J Hatton (son), A Webb and J Barton; 13, 14,15 and 16, table cloths. P G save, G Podmore, H Welshy, and J Gleave. Seventeen other prizes, consisting of lsoxes of Crosfield's herb toilet soap were also distributed. Mr A H OROSFIELD prop tsed a hearty vote of thanks Ito the Mayoress. He said that they could the more appreciate her kindness in distributing th, priz,s Ithat afternoon, whm they recognised the many claims upon her time. Wherever the Mayoress went her charming nature added to the pleasure of the proceedings. He was very sorry that his father'sl health was such as to prevent him from joining them in the games. As far as bowls was concerned, be owed his first introduction to the game to his father. It took place nearly 30 years ago. He used then to carry the " footer," when his father played the game with his neighbouring friends. Personally he did not kn )w very much about the niceties of the game at all. He thought, however, that it was a most excellent thing for the English artisan to indulge in a game like bowls, and thereby obtain such pleasant ex Sr John Brunner said at Winnington the other day t iat they should work hard whilst they worked, and play hard whilst they played. (Hear, hear). He asked them to give a hearty response to the proposal for a vote of thanks to the Mayoress. (Applause.) Mrs ROBERTS briefly replied,and votes of thanks were passed to Miss Crosfield, Miss Roberts. Miss Mackie, and Mr Crosfield, for their attendance, and to Mr and Mrs Allison, for the use of the green. Messrs Crosfield's ' band was present during the afternoon and played selections of music.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>Anniversary Meetings.</head>
        <p>Anniversary Meetings.</p>
        <p>Irianor'a nt meetiags in connection with the anniversary or the %Warrington Clergy Institutions(the Clergy Widows and Orphans' S )eiety aud rile Clergy Dlughters' School) were held on Wednesday. At eleven o'clock the annual meeting of subsc-ibers was held in the Training College. Mr 13,reaford V Melville, MP. (president) pre- sid.d, and there were also present : The Right Rqv the Lord Bilhoo of Chester, the Dean of Blanchester (the Rev Canon Mixture), the Rev. Canons Gore, Willis and Penrhyn, Mr J.-)lin White, Mr IV P Fullagar, Mr R H and Mrs J)yuson, the R.v W H and Mrs Kinch, and the Revs EC E Carleton, W Williams, II V Pigot, and W L , Paige Cox (vicar of link Ferry.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>CLEROE WIDOWS' AND ORPRANS' RELIEF FUND.</head>
        <p>CLEROE WIDOWS' AND ORPRANS' RELIEF FUND.</p>
        <p>C inon PENRHYNT read the following repr;;Ciif the Clergy Widow,' and Orphans' Relief Fund :—Since the last anniversary Bishop Cramer-Roberts, whom we men- tioned in our last report as having been appointed to succeed Archdeacon Rawstorne, has been very suddenly and unexpectedly called away from us; and Mr Jacob Fair. after a mere prolonged illness, has been taken to his rest ; both were hard-working members of our com- mittee, and will be much missed by us all. Archdeacon Fletcher, who has had several daughters in the C.D.S., succeeds Bishop Cramer-Roberts, and will no doubt take a like interest in the charity. In place of Mr Fair your CJminittee weuld recommend Captain Withington, of Culcheth Bad, who has long taken a warm interest in these institutions. No other names having been sent in to the secretaries, the retiring members, as under, are re-eiected, subject to the confirmation of the annual meeting :—Chester : Rev Richard Greenall and the Rev Canon Blenco ale ; and Messrs J B Grafton and James Tinsley. Manchester : Rev Canon Rogers and the Rev Canon Hoskyns ; and Messrs W 8 Kinch, R Joynson, and W P Fullagar. Liverpool : Rev Canon Armour' ani the Rev Canon Blundell ; and Mr R Ireland Blackburne and I.cird Lilford. Of those to whom grants were made last year, there have since died two widows from Chester diocese, and one from Manchester; one widow and one orphan (both from Manchester) la,ing in better circumstances, have with- drawn their applicatioas. We have on the other hand new applications from Chester, on' widow and two ' orphans ; from Manchester, two widows and two orphans ; and from Liverpool,two widows. The annual meeting is asked to sanction pensions and deflations to the amount of £1,775, and if the recommendations of your committee are approved there will be en our list: from Chester, 18 widows and 16 arphans ; from Man- chwter, 31 wilows and 18 orphans ; from Liverpool, 14 widows and 10 orphans. The income for the year ended December 31st, 1900. was £2,003 12; 43, made up as follows :—Donations, £29 51 61, as against in 1899, £59 Os ill ; interest on investments, £791 8; 31, as against £Bl6 153 101 ; collections, £333 18; Bd, as against £326 5 Id ; subscriptions, £725 19; 31, as against £727 8, 9 1 ; grants from diocesan funds (Ches- hire and Lancashire), £993; 91, as against £ll5 ; bank interest, £23 16; lid, as against £l4 6; 91. l'he un- satisfactory character of these figures is enhanced by the fact that having begun the year with an adverse balance of £36 5; 111,the overdrawn balance at the end ot the year was no less than £304 16; 8!. This must compel the reduction of some ot our grants this year, as although six grants have lapsed through deaths, or resignations, there are no fewer than nine new appli- cants. The appointment of Mr Thomas as organiz ng secretary for the Archdeacenries of Manchester and B ackburn has not as yet bad any marked effect ; but it is perhaps still toa early to pronounce any decisive opinion. Anyhow, it is perfectly clear that the claims upon our resources are by no means dimi aishing ; and that the three dioceses must not relax their efforts to .give ever-iriareasing support to these most useful institutions.</p>
        <p>CLERGY DAUGHTERS' SCHOOL. THE DEBT WIPED OUT.</p>
        <p>The Rev Canon-S-fielis-;ea.ti- the following report The Committee of Management have reasons for the I liveliest satisfaction in presenting their report for the past year. First, the number of pupils is the highest yet reached and the largest that the school with its present accommodation is capable of receiving, namely 70. Secondly, the results of all the examinations lately piablished in the diocesan reports of Chester, Man chester and L;verpeol for the year 1900 are excellent and those at present received for 1901 from the Associ- ated Board of the Royal Aeademy and Royal College of Music and the Royal Drawing Society of Great Britain and Ireland and from the Oxford delegates and head mistress and her staff, who have just concluded the examination of the higher and lower divisions of the school, are equally good. Thirdly, the conduct and health of the girls during the past year have been all that could be desired. Fourthly and lastly, the debt upon the schoel,wssich last year was reduced from £7OO to £340, has this year been entirely wiped out, and the I school is now for the first time for many years able to present a perfectly clean balance sheet. (Hear, hear.) These are indeed causes for thaeleulness, and the sin- cerest gratitude is due to the headmistress, by whose able management and indefatigable exertions such results have been chtained. It will doubtless be remem- bered that in last year's report mention was made of a scholarship fund wherewith to supplement the income of any student who might win an open scholarship at the University or to be thought worthy of a college career. The c momittee have great pleasure in announcing that the sum of £4OO has now been contributed and that a generous friend has most kindly offsred £5O more pro- vided that another £5O is raised before the end of the year. They cannot doubt that this very kind offer will be immediately secured, by which a sum of £2O per annum will boat oace available,and they earnestly trust that in no long time the remaining £5OO will be raised so that an income of £4O may be definitely assured to any needy and deserving student who is se fortunate as to win an open scholarslarp, or be able with the assist- ance of friends to proceed to the University. With the sanction of the subscribers the committee have made an alteration in the rules relating to scho'arships by which the six £5 per annum scholarshipe will be awarded on the results of the yearly examination of the school to senior instead of junior pupils. Certain conditions are attached to the granting and holding of these scholar- ships. During the past year the committee have sold a small portion of their property to the south of Howley Brook, and ssith the proceeds have renovated the Convalescent Home and Hospital, the latter of which is used as a gymnasium when not required for the purpose for which it was built. It is probable that the former will be occupied for the present by the servants of the institutien, whose removal will allow a good room in a distant part of the school to be used for a sick dormitory and also provide further accommodation to senior pupils and junior mis- tresses for recreation and study. The committee in concluding their report desire to express their sense of increased obligation to Miss Kennedy whose invaluable services have contributed so materially to the prosperity and success of the school. Tney wish also to thank cordially her staff, whose loyal en-operation, and willing self sacrifice have been specially commended by the Head Mistress in her report. (Applause). The BISHOP of CHESTER said he moved the adoption of the reports with mixed feelings. They all heard with the deepest regret that the position of the funds for the widows and orphans was not satisfactory, but be was quite certain that it could be left with the three dioceses ,to put that matter right another year. (Hear, hear). He hoped the pensions bad not been reduced in , consequence of the deficit. Canon PENRHEN remarkld that £1,500 had beea voted and £1,700 expended. (Laughter.) I The BPIIOP said that was a very wise lavishness. He borne to the three dioceses 'hoped it would be brought that something should be done to put this matter right. The other report was most satisfastory, and they all j iined with the committee in the desire to make their acknowledgments to Miss Kennedy and her staff. (Hears hear.) One wished that circumstances allowed the ex- pansion of the school but that was a big question he did not propose that day to enter upon. It did seem to him, however, that the school was one which had fur- ther powers of expansion and under such a leader as I Miss Kennedy he could not he,p thinking the school had a brighr future Wore it. (Applause.) The DEAN OF MANCHE3TER seconded and endorsed the remarks of the Bishop. Commenting upon the reports he mentioned, without disparattement to Warrington, that the title of the institutions—that of the Warrington lestitutions--was somewhat confusing to strangers who did not find in it the information that it embraced the three dioceses. The reports werts approved.</p>
        <p>NEV 7 ME3IBER9 OF CIISIMITTEE.</p>
        <p>Mr W S KINCH was elected a member of the Com. natter, of Managernont in the place of a Manchester naernber who has left the diocese. _ PROPOSED SCHOLARSHIPS.</p>
        <p>Mr FULL/WAR moved "That rule 12 be altered by striking out paragraph 2, and by the substitution and ad.iition of the following :—Exhibitions (not exceeding Aix in number unless otherwise determined upon as hereinafter provided) of £5 each may, subject to the approval of the Committee of Management, be awarded from time to time upon the re- sults of the yearly school examination and the report of the examiner to pupils who are either remaining in the school for another year from the time of such examination or who are immediately leav- ing in .rd.r to go to some place of higher education. Notwithstanding anything hereinbefore contained it shall be competent for the Committee of Management at any time to withhold the exhibitions named in the two preceding paragraphs, or any or either of them, or (provided the funds of the school allow it) to increase the numbs? and amount awarded, or to vary the terms upon which the same shall be awarded or held." The Rev Canon GORE, in seconding, referred to the scholarship fund mentioned iii the report of the Corn. mittee of Management, and strongly urged the desir- ability of means such as that being taken to allow students, who were clever, enterprising and earnest, to obtain the higher distinctions of education. I The resolution was carried.</p>
        <p>On the-m-otioo of the DEAN or MANCHESTER, seconded by Mr R H JOYNSON, a vote ot thanks was passed to the President.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>CONSERVATIVE CLUB v. PENKETH PARK.</head>
        <p>CONSERVATIVE CLUB v. PENKETH PARK.</p>
        <p>This return match wag played on the green of the Conservative Club on Saturday. The Coneervatives wan seven double games and 14 singles. The following are the results :</p>
        <p>CONSERVATIVE CLUB. PENKETH PARK. T Turner 7}l3 1 Appleton ll 1 2 1 Leigh ....—........ ......... 11 H Dumbill 4 J Genders H Booth lO f • J Graham ll f I J Taylor ll}l3 R Kerr Btl 2 R Dutton H Turner D Bawling ll J" A Higham s.6 J " S Woods . . 11 il3 A Dunabin S 3 W Hill ll F Swift J Harrison ll 1,2 B Dumbill T Cooper 3 Jones 6 G Rotherham ll J 3 Bowyer P Bradburn 3 .1. G Warburton ll J ' A Cooper</p>
