        <p>Mijority for Conservatives ...138 In the match at Penketh the scores were :—Penketh 245, Conservative Club 177. Toe result of the two games, therefore, is: Conser- vative Club (home and away) 443, Penkrtth (nome and away) 373 ; majority of 70 in favour of Conservatives.</p>
        <p>__.s.._.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES. Wycliffe—P—.S7A—. Society.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES. Wycliffe—P—.S7A—. Society.</p>
        <p>• There was an average atterichince at the meeting of this Society on Sunday afternoon. Mr Webb presided and stated that he had recently come to Warrington from Ashton-under-Lyne. He proceeded to speak of the success of the P.S.A. movement in that town and re- ferr d to certain institutions in connection with it which had contributed to its success. Mr Betts, of Manchester, was the speaker and delivered an address on " Character." Miss Hill very sweetiv nang a solo The service was also taken put in by Mr B Farrar (hon secretary) and Mr William Beswick.</p>
        <p>Ellesmere-street Wesleyan Mission. The anniversary services of the Sunday school connected with this mission were held on Sunday and Monday, when the preacher for Sunday morning and evening was Mr Moses Welsby (better known as" Owd Mo."), of Rochdale. In the afternoon a young people's service was held, when a solo was given by Mr. Levi Richardson, accomp.tnied on the pianoforte by Mr. T. Brough. The chair was occupied by Mr. James Dodds. in the unavoidable absence, through indisposition, of Mr. John Taylor. The basis of the Chairman's address was " Religious and Secular Education." In the course of his remarks he said that to his mind the work of the day school teacher was as much the work of God as that of the Sunday school teacher.—On Monday evening, " owd Mo." gave one of his popular lectures, entitled " Wandering and gleaning with the old van." The chair was occupied by Mr. J. B Isaac, who is ever anxious for the welfare of the school. The singing of the choir,and especially of the children, was rendered in good style, under the leadership of Mr. H. Plumptoti. Mr. Anderson kindly officiated as organist at all the services. The offertories and donations were considerably in advance of last year.</p>
        <p>Railway Servants' Church Parade. The annual Church parade of the Warrington Br- och of the Amalgamated Society of Railway Servant vas held on Sunday. The parades are held for the ro" pose of augmenting, by means of collections, the Cohan Fund of the society. A procession was formed at one o'clock in front of the Town Hall, and shortly after half-past one a start was made for Bewsey-road %Yes- leyan Chapel where the service was held. The pro- cession was headed by the Borough Band, following which came members carrying the new banner, which was recently unfurled. The general body of the members then followed, the officials wearing their sashes. As the procession wended its way through the streets it attracted considerable attention. A special service was held at the chapel, the preacher being the Rev C II Hocken, who delivered an excellent address upon the words, " Even so it is not the will of your Father in heaven that one of these little one should perish." A solo, "If with all your hearts " was sung by Mr G Gough, and a collection was taken on behalf of the funds of the society. In the evening the pro- cession was again formed at the Town Hall and proceeded along Sankev-stieet, Green-street, Peiestley- street, Froghall-lane, Allen-street, Borsemarket-street, and Sankey-street, to 'Friar's-green Chapel, where Councillor James Evans conducted the service. A collection was again taken on behalf of the society's funds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>SOLID AIR AND ITS POSSIBILITIES</head>
        <p>SOLID AIR AND ITS POSSIBILITIES</p>
        <p>Fivn nr,ars ago we could only conceive of air as gas. Then came liquid air. N)cv comes solid. Imagine a philosopher of only ono generation ago stating that it would be possible to pack up blocks of solid air and to hurl pieces of it through the comman air! Urt would have been regarded as a hopeless lunatic. 3LE these things it is now possible to do.</p>
        <p>This discoveryris the culmination of a long series of laborious experiments by Professur Metz with liquid air. He has been seeking to ascertain the possibilities and properties of this strange substance,abcut which little 19 yet known. He had nearly come to the end of his supply of liquid when ha determined to use up all that was left inta hold experiment.llie object was to produce solid air He took a large test tube about eighteen inches long and over an inch in diameter and put the liquid air into it, tilling it to within six inches of the top, tie then corked it, and through the cork inserted a bent glass tube which was connected with a vacuum apparatus, and the latter was started to cause the most rapid evaporation possible. The liquid air bubbled and boiled as ejwater would if exposed to the most in...ense heat, but of course it must be remembered that at the time it was put into ton cube the liquid air st pod at the low temperature of 312 degrees below zero, and that thus subjectel to rapid evaporation the temper- ature fell many degrees below its former figure. In fact the cold became so intense that the atmospheric air all about the tube condensed and lique- fied on the outside of the tube and ran down just as steam condenses on the outside of a tumbler of cold water. These drops were caught in a Dewar tube and proved en experiment to be identical with liquid air. In the meantime the original volume of liquid air in the test tube was found to havo been reduced to a small lump scarcely more than an inch deep, in appearance resembling fairly clear ice. Th at was solid air, the first time such a thing had ever been produced, so far ae records show.</p>
        <p>In order to further experiment with the product thus secured it was necessary to break the tube. The little piece of frozen air, which Professor Metz also describeS as resembling a bit of frosted glass, was then subjected to several tests. Ltid upon an anvil, it was struck with a hammer; the sound given out was that which one would hear on hitting a piece of hard ice, but in the present case the hammer rebounded as it it had struck indiarubber. Any object touching the solid air was immediately frozen to it and mucn valuable time was lost in disengaging the pincers when efforts were made to manipulate the strange product, Small quan-ities.of liquid air, condensed from the surrounding atmosphere. constantly trickled down the little block. &amp; metal rod held against the solid air conveyed an intolerable sensa- tion to a haad touching it at the other end. This sensation was so acute that it was impossible to tell whether it was hot or cold. 'rile discovery of frozen air, opens a wide field for speculation. One of the'most interesting is that of the possible presence of crystalline air particles in space. The extreme cold existing in interstellar space is generally recognised and the probability is that it an', air exists there it do!'s 90 in the form of minute crystals. like the infinitesimal frost particles of water which fill the air on very cold days. In space these frozen air particles may well serve to diffuse the solar light, a diffusion which has as yet never been quite satisfactorily explained. In the more practical uses of every day life solid air would seem to go far beyond the promises of inventors respecting the liquid form. Solid air will remain unmelted much longer than liquid air. It IS that fact which may be of such importance conatner. cially. Liquid air often boils away so quickly that itg greatest force cannot b 3 employed. With solid air, for instance, it may be possible to insert a cartridge-shaped piece—just as a cartridge is placed—in a pistol and itS power would be ex3rted both sideways and in the direction of the muzzle. This way of using solid air would make it of inestimable value in coal mines, sores of which are so filled with explosivrs gases that blasting is impossible.—" Science Siftings."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-07-26_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>ELECTRIC EXPRESS BILL.</head>
        <p>THE ELECTRIC EXPRESS BILL.</p>
        <p>Summing-up for the Opposition. The House of Commons Committee resumed thelr consideration of the Manchester and Liverpool Electric Express Railway &amp;It cn Tnorsday morning. Mr Littler, KC, in summing up the case on behalf of the petition of the London and North-Westein Rail waY and Cheshire Linos, said the mono rail scheme v;35 doomed to failure by its inherent defects. It was exceedingly complicated, having five rails instead of two, and if for no other reason It war fort that practically foredoomed. It was a significant fiic that the system, although old, had been adopted on .nc, practical railway as yet, except the Ballybunion 1113 t. which was a ridiculous affair. The Americans, who were up to date in everything, and who seized an anY°,,osv ri_. thing with money f 0 it, had not even consider, . c:' e ously the advisability of taking up di the morm rail. , did not believe a single financier in the worlworldout a sovereign on a line such as that proposed. "the did Mr Behr take such a pathetic Interest is -s engineering progress of England. Why did het German, come to Eaglandto bless it i";l3t „,- his mono rail. He suspected Mr Behr th-nig'd " John Bull is very rich, John Bull is verlt foolish and very apt to put his money into wild schemes, and some one gets the advantage." why, would like to know, if Mr Behr had such an interrst Eegland, did he try his experiments in Belgium would like to point out that those experiments were only ones upon which the Manchester and Liverf3or Committee had to rely. Mr Balfour Browne, replying on behalf of the prorPOl ters, submitted that it was unnecessary for the Coln. mittee to consider very closely the details of the pro posals, because the Board of Trade would not sanctiol the running of the line until they were convinced ti?i° It was absolutely safe, High speed could not be n:121i3.. tamed on ordinary railways going round curves, but op this line the highest speed would be maintained witholl danger. The invention was very simple. It merell resolved itself into the fact that the centre or grasitl was below the top rail. The greatest inventions strefa simplicity itself, and this invention being so simple Wa.6 great scheme that would revolutionise railway travel' ling.</p>
        <p>END OF TRH CASE. The Cimmittee reserved their decision until to-dal (Fri d ay).</p>
