        <p>WARRINGTON. A Wiredrawer Found Drowned. Last week we reported the firldiElg of a body in the .Mersey near Dobby's Locks on Friday evening. It transpires that it was the body of Thomas Wood, 44 years of age, wiredrawer lately residing at 14, Gaskell-street, Warrington. Mr S Brighoase,county coroner, conducted an inquiry in the Parade Room of the Police Station on Monday afternoon into the 'circumstances. The first witness called was Mary Wood, wife of the deceased, who etated that she , lest saw her husband alive about MillP o'clock on Friday morning of the previous week when he left home for the purpose of going to the Infirmary to , have his arai dressed. For the past five years he. had been unfible to follow his employment owing to the fact t'ar, he had a hand taken off at his work He also suffered from fits. She had never heard him threaten to take away his life. He went along the river every day for a walk.—John Rathbone, lock-keeper at Paddington Locks deposed the at 7.15 on Friday night last he was standing on the bank of the river when he saw the deceased floating in the water. He succeeded in bringing the body to the bank by means of grappling irons —Mrs Wood, recalled, said her husband had had a fit almost every day during the ' last six months.—John Rathbone (lock keeper) informed the Coroner that he saw the deceased have a fit on the river bank some tiine ago.—The Coroner : Is it quite easy for anyone to fall into the water ?—witness : Yes.—The jury returned a verdict of "Found drowned." 41. COMMERCvAL FAlLURES—According to " Kcmp's Mer- cantile Gaz.tte," the number of New Bankruptcy rrp- credings in England and Waleq gazettcd during the week ending July 20:h, was 85. The number in the cerrrapondinfr week of last s.ear wa. 96, showing a deistesse of 11, heing a net decease in 1901, to date, of 100.</p>
